
var SPELL_FULL={"Acid Splash": "You conjure a bubble of acid at a point you choose within range, and it bursts in a 5-foot-radius Sphere. Every creature inside that Sphere makes a Dexterity saving throw, taking 1d6 Acid damage on a failure. **Cantrip Upgrade.** The damage goes up by 1d6 when you reach level 5 (2d6), level 11 (3d6), and level 17 (4d6).", "Blade Ward": "Until the spell ends, any creature that makes an attack roll against you subtracts 1d4 from that roll.", "Chill Touch": "With the chill of the grave in your grasp, you attack a target within your reach using a melee spell attack. If the attack hits, the target takes 1d10 Necrotic damage and is unable to regain Hit Points until your next turn ends. **Cantrip Upgrade.** This damage goes up by 1d10 at level 5 (2d10), level 11 (3d10), and level 17 (4d10).", "Dancing Lights": "Up to four lights, each the size of a torch, appear within range and hover for the duration. You make them look like torches, lanterns, or glowing orbs. Or you can merge the four lights into a single glowing form of Medium size that is vaguely human in shape. Whatever form you pick, every light gives off Dim Light in a 10-foot radius. You can take a Bonus Action to move the lights as far as 60 feet, to a space within range. Each light must stay within 20 feet of another light from this spell, and any light that goes beyond the spell's range vanishes.", "Druidcraft": "You whisper to the spirits of nature and create one of these effects within range. **Weather Sensor.** A Tiny, harmless sensory effect appears that forecasts the weather at your location for the next 24 hours. It might look like a golden orb when skies will be clear, a cloud when rain is coming, drifting snowflakes when snow is due, and so forth. The effect lasts for 1 round. **Bloom.** At once, you cause a flower to blossom, a seed pod to open, or a leaf bud to bloom. **Sensory Effect.** You produce a harmless sensory effect that must fit within a 5-foot Cube, such as falling leaves, ghostly dancing fairies, a soft breeze, an animal's call, or a faint skunk smell. **Fire Play.** You light or put out a candle, a torch, or a campfire.", "Eldritch Blast": "You hurl crackling energy in the form of a beam. Choose one creature or object within range as the target and make a ranged spell attack against it. A hit deals 1d10 Force damage to the target. **Cantrip Upgrade.** The number of beams grows to two when you reach level 5, to three at level 11, and to four at level 17. You can aim the beams at one target or spread them among different targets, and each beam gets its own attack roll.", "Elementalism": "You take command of the elements and produce one of these effects within range. **Beckon Air.** In a 5-foot Cube, you stir up a breeze that can ripple cloth, raise dust, rustle leaves, and shut doors and shutters that are open. A door or shutter that someone or something is holding open isn't affected. **Beckon Earth.** You spread a thin layer of dust or sand over surfaces in a 5-foot-square area, or you make one word appear in your handwriting in a patch of sand or dirt. **Beckon Fire.** In a 5-foot Cube, you create a thin cloud of harmless embers along with colored, scented smoke. You pick the color and the scent, and the embers can light lamps, candles, or torches in that area. The scent of the smoke remains for 1 minute. **Beckon Water.** In a 5-foot Cube, you create a spray of cool mist that lightly wets creatures and objects. Or, you create 1 cup of clean water in an open container or on a surface, and that water evaporates after 1 minute. **Sculpt Element.** Dirt, sand, fire, smoke, mist, or water that fits within a 1-foot Cube takes on a crude shape (such as the shape of a creature) for 1 hour.", "Fire Bolt": "You fling a mote of fire at a creature or object within range and make a ranged spell attack against it. A hit deals 1d10 Fire damage to the target. If the spell hits a flammable object that no one is wearing or carrying, the object starts burning. **Cantrip Upgrade.** The damage rises by 1d10 at level 5 (2d10), level 11 (3d10), and level 17 (4d10).", "Friends": "You magically radiate a feeling of friendship toward one creature within range that you can see. Unless the target succeeds on a Wisdom saving throw, it has the Charmed condition for the duration. The save succeeds automatically when the target isn't a Humanoid, when you are fighting it, or when you have already cast this spell on it in the last 24 hours. If the target takes any damage, or if you deal damage, make an attack roll, or force anyone to make a saving throw, the spell ends early. Once the spell ends, the target is aware that you Charmed it.", "Guidance": "You touch a willing creature and name a skill. While the spell lasts, whenever that creature makes an ability check using the chosen skill, it adds 1d4 to the check.", "Light": "Pick one Large or smaller object that nobody else is wearing or carrying, and touch it. Until the spell ends, the object sheds Bright Light out to 20 feet and Dim Light for a further 20 feet. You can make the light any color you like. Anything opaque placed over the object blocks the light. Casting the spell again ends it.", "Mage Hand": "At a point you pick within range, a floating, spectral hand appears and stays for the duration. The hand disappears if it ever gets farther than 30 feet from you or if you cast the spell again. As part of casting the spell, you can have the hand manipulate an object, open a container or door that isn't locked, place an item in or remove one from an open container, or pour out what is in a vial. On each of your later turns, a Magic action lets you control the hand like this again, and that action also lets you move the hand up to 30 feet. The hand is unable to attack or activate magic items, and it can carry no more than 10 pounds.", "Mending": "Touch an object to fix one break or tear in it. Examples include a snapped chain link, a key broken into two halves, a ripped cloak, or a leaking wineskin. If the damage measures 1 foot or less in every dimension, you fix it, and no trace of the former damage remains. A magic item can be physically repaired this way, but the spell can't restore magic to it.", "Message": "You point at a creature in range and whisper a message to it. Only the target hears it, and the target can whisper back a reply that only you can hear. You can cast the spell through solid objects as long as the target is familiar to you and you know it is on the other side of the barrier. The spell is blocked by magical silence, by a thin sheet of lead, or by 1 foot of stone, metal, or wood.", "Mind Sliver": "You try to briefly splinter the mind of one creature within range that you can see. On a failed Intelligence saving throw, the target takes 1d6 Psychic damage, and it subtracts 1d4 from the next saving throw it makes before your next turn ends. **Cantrip Upgrade.** The damage becomes 2d6 at level 5, 3d6 at level 11, and 4d6 at level 17.", "Minor Illusion": "Within range, you create either a sound or an image of an object, which lasts for the duration. The effects of each are described below. Casting this spell again ends the illusion. A creature can take a Study action to examine the sound or image. If its Intelligence (Investigation) check against your spell save DC succeeds, it determines that it is an illusion. Once a creature recognizes the illusion for what it is, the illusion becomes faint to that creature. **Sound.** A sound you create can be as quiet as a whisper or as loud as a scream. It can be your voice, another person's voice, a lion's roar, the beat of drums, or any other sound you pick. The sound can keep going without pause for the duration, or you can make separate sounds at different times before the spell ends. **Image.** If you create an image of an object (for example a small chest, muddy footprints, or a chair), it must fit within a 5-foot Cube. It can't produce sound, light, smell, or any other sensory effect. Things can pass through the image, so physical interaction with it reveals that it is an illusion.", "Poison Spray": "Aim a spray of toxic mist at a creature within range, making a ranged spell attack against it. On a hit, it takes 1d12 Poison damage. **Cantrip Upgrade.** The spell's damage rises by 1d12 when you reach level 5 (2d12), level 11 (3d12), and level 17 (4d12).", "Prestidigitation": "You produce a magical effect within range, picking it from the options below. If you cast this spell more than once, up to three of its non-instantaneous effects can be active at the same time. **Sensory Effect.** You create a harmless sensory effect that is instantaneous, such as a puff of wind, a shower of sparks, an odd odor, or faint musical notes. **Fire Play.** You instantly light or put out a candle, a torch, or a small campfire. **Clean or Soil.** You instantly clean or dirty an object of 1 cubic foot or smaller. **Minor Sensation.** You chill, warm, or flavor as much as 1 cubic foot of nonliving material for 1 hour. **Magic Mark.** For 1 hour, a color, a small mark, or a symbol appears on an object or a surface. **Minor Creation.** You make an illusory image or a nonmagical trinket that fits in your hand. The creation lasts until your next turn ends. A trinket deals no damage and has no monetary value.", "Produce Flame": "A flickering flame appears in your hand and stays there for the duration. While it's there, the flame gives off no heat and sets nothing alight. It casts Bright Light out to 20 feet and Dim Light for 20 feet beyond that. Casting this spell again ends it. As a Magic action while the spell lasts, you can hurl fire at a creature or an object no more than 60 feet away from you. Resolve it with a ranged spell attack. A hit deals 1d8 Fire damage to the target. **Cantrip Upgrade.** The spell's damage rises by 1d8 when you reach level 5 (2d8), level 11 (3d8), and level 17 (4d8).", "Ray of Frost": "A beam of icy blue-white light speeds toward a creature within range, and you make a ranged spell attack against it. If it hits, the target takes 1d8 Cold damage, and its Speed drops by 10 feet until your next turn starts. **Cantrip Upgrade.** The spell's damage rises by 1d8 when you reach level 5 (2d8), level 11 (3d8), and level 17 (4d8).", "Resistance": "Touch a willing creature and pick a damage type: Bludgeoning, Piercing, Slashing, Acid, Cold, Fire, Lightning, Thunder, Necrotic, Radiant, or Poison. Whenever the creature takes damage of that type before the spell ends, it reduces the total damage it takes by 1d4. A creature can gain this benefit only once per turn.", "Sacred Flame": "Radiance that falls like flame engulfs a creature you can see within range, and that creature must make a Dexterity saving throw. On a failure, it takes 1d8 Radiant damage. Cover doesn't help it against this save, whether Half Cover or Three-Quarters Cover. **Cantrip Upgrade.** The spell's damage rises by 1d8 when you reach level 5 (2d8), level 11 (3d8), and level 17 (4d8).", "Shillelagh": "A Club or Quarterstaff you're holding fills with the power of nature. For the duration, melee attacks you make with that weapon can use your spellcasting ability instead of Strength for their attack and damage rolls, and the weapon's damage die becomes a d8. When such an attack deals damage, the damage can be Force or the weapon's normal type (your choice). Casting the spell again, or letting go of the weapon, ends the spell early. **Cantrip Upgrade.** The weapon's damage die improves when you reach level 5 (d10), level 11 (d12), and level 17 (2d6).", "Shocking Grasp": "Lightning leaps from you toward a creature that you attempt to touch. Resolve it with a melee spell attack. A hit deals 1d8 Lightning damage to the target, and until its next turn starts, it can't make Opportunity Attacks. **Cantrip Upgrade.** The spell's damage rises by 1d8 when you reach level 5 (2d8), level 11 (3d8), and level 17 (4d8).", "Sorcerous Burst": "You hurl sorcerous energy at one creature or object within range and make a ranged attack roll against it. A hit deals 1d8 damage of a type you pick from Acid, Cold, Fire, Lightning, Poison, Psychic, and Thunder. Each d8 for this spell that shows an 8 lets you roll another d8 and add it to the damage. The number of these extra d8s you can add to the damage of one casting is at most your spellcasting ability modifier. **Cantrip Upgrade.** The spell's damage rises by 1d8 when you reach level 5 (2d8), level 11 (3d8), and level 17 (4d8).", "Spare the Dying": "Pick a creature within range that isn't dead but has 0 Hit Points. That creature becomes Stable. **Cantrip Upgrade.** The spell's range doubles when you reach level 5 (30 feet), level 11 (60 feet), and level 17 (120 feet).", "Starry Wisp": "You fling a mote of light toward one creature or object within range and make a ranged spell attack against that target. A hit deals 1d8 Radiant damage to it. Until your next turn ends, the target also gives off Dim Light in a 10-foot radius and gains no benefit from the Invisible condition. **Cantrip Upgrade.** Damage grows by 1d8 at character levels 5 (2d8), 11 (3d8), and 17 (4d8).", "Thaumaturgy": "You produce a small marvel: choose one of the effects below and create it within range. If you cast it multiple times, as many as three of its 1-minute effects can be active at once. **Altered Eyes.** The look of your eyes changes for 1 minute. **Booming Voice.** For 1 minute, your voice is up to three times as loud as usual, and you have Advantage on Charisma (Intimidation) checks during that time. **Fire Play.** For 1 minute, flames flicker, grow brighter, grow dimmer, or change color. **Invisible Hand.** An unlocked door or window instantly flies open or slams shut. **Phantom Sound.** An instantaneous sound rings out from a point of your choice within range, such as a rumble of thunder, a raven's cry, or ominous whispers. **Tremors.** Harmless tremors shake the ground for 1 minute.", "Thorn Whip": "At your command, a thorn-covered whip resembling a vine lashes at a creature in range, and you make a melee spell attack against it. A hit deals 1d6 Piercing damage, and you can pull the target up to 10 feet closer to you if it is Large or smaller. **Cantrip Upgrade.** Damage grows by 1d6 at character levels 5 (2d6), 11 (3d6), and 17 (4d6).", "Thunderclap": "Each creature in a 5-foot Emanation centered on you makes a Constitution saving throw and takes 1d6 Thunder damage if it fails. The spell's thunderous noise is audible up to 100 feet away. **Cantrip Upgrade.** Damage grows by 1d6 at character levels 5 (2d6), 11 (3d6), and 17 (4d6).", "Toll the Dead": "You point at a creature you can see within range, and a single chime of a mournful bell sounds, audible within 10 feet of the target. The target makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure, it takes 1d8 Necrotic damage, or 1d12 Necrotic damage instead if it is missing any of its Hit Points. **Cantrip Upgrade.** Add one die to the damage at character level 5 (2d8 or 2d12), again at 11 (3d8 or 3d12), and again at 17 (4d8 or 4d12).", "True Strike": "**Material Component.** A weapon worth at least 1 CP with which you have proficiency. A flash of magical insight guides you as you make one attack with the weapon used to cast the spell. That attack uses your spellcasting ability, not Strength or Dexterity, for its attack and damage rolls. If it deals damage, you choose whether that damage is Radiant or the weapon's normal damage type. **Cantrip Upgrade.** At character levels 5, 11, and 17, the attack deals extra Radiant damage of 1d6, 2d6, and 3d6 respectively, whichever damage type you chose for the attack.", "Vicious Mockery": "You let loose a stream of insults woven with subtle enchantments, aimed at one creature within range that you can see or hear. That creature must make a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure, it takes 1d6 Psychic damage, and its next attack roll before the end of its next turn has Disadvantage. **Cantrip Upgrade.** Damage grows by 1d6 at character levels 5 (2d6), 11 (3d6), and 17 (4d6).", "Word of Radiance": "Burning radiance flares out from you and fills a 5-foot Emanation. Every creature there that you can see and choose makes a Constitution saving throw, taking 1d6 Radiant damage if it fails. **Cantrip Upgrade.** Damage grows by 1d6 at character levels 5 (2d6), 11 (3d6), and 17 (4d6).", "Alarm": "You guard a spot against intruders. Pick a door, a window, or an area that is within range and fits inside a 20-foot Cube. For as long as the spell lasts, you are warned by an alarm each time a creature touches or enters the warded area. As you cast the spell, you can name creatures that won't trigger the alarm. You also decide whether the alarm is audible or mental. **Audible Alarm.** For 10 seconds, the sound of a handbell rings out within 60 feet of the warded area. **Mental Alarm.** A mental ping alerts you as long as you are no more than 1 mile from the warded area, and it wakes you if you are asleep.", "Animal Friendship": "Choose a Beast you can see within range. Unless it succeeds on a Wisdom saving throw, the Beast is Charmed for the duration. If you or any ally of yours damages the target, the spell ends. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, you can target one more Beast.", "Armor of Agathys": "A layer of protective magical frost covers you, and you gain 5 Temporary Hit Points. Until the spell ends, any creature that hits you with a melee attack roll takes 5 Cold damage. The spell ends early once you have no Temporary Hit Points left. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, both the Temporary Hit Points and the Cold damage rise by 5.", "Arms of Hadar": "You call on Hadar, and tendrils burst out of you. Every creature in a 10-foot Emanation that starts from you makes a Strength saving throw. On a failure, a creature takes 2d6 Necrotic damage, and it is unable to take Reactions until its next turn starts. A creature that succeeds takes only half as much damage. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, the damage rises by 1d6.", "Bane": "Choose up to three creatures you can see within range. Each one makes a Charisma saving throw. Until the spell ends, a target that failed must subtract 1d4 whenever it makes an attack roll or a saving throw. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, you can target one more creature.", "Bless": "**Material Component.** A Holy Symbol worth at least 5 GP. Up to three creatures within range receive your blessing. Until the spell ends, each time one of them makes an attack roll or a saving throw, it rolls 1d4 and adds the number rolled to that attack roll or saving throw. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, you can target one more creature.", "Burning Hands": "You release a thin sheet of flame. Every creature in a 15-foot Cone must make a Dexterity saving throw. It takes 3d6 Fire damage on a failure, or half that damage on a success. Any flammable object in the Cone that no one is wearing or carrying catches fire. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, the damage increases by 1d6.", "Charm Person": "Pick one Humanoid you can see within range. That Humanoid makes a Wisdom saving throw. The Humanoid has Advantage on this saving throw if it is in a fight with you or your allies. On a failure, the Humanoid is Charmed for the duration. Damage dealt to it by you or your allies ends this condition early. The Charmed Humanoid is Friendly to you. After the spell ends, it knows you were the one who Charmed it. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, you can target one more creature.", "Chromatic Orb": "**Material Component.** A diamond worth at least 50 GP. You throw a sphere of energy at a target within range. First pick its damage type: Acid, Cold, Fire, Lightning, Poison, or Thunder. Then make a ranged spell attack against the target. If it hits, the target takes 3d8 damage of that type. When two or more of the d8s show the same number, the orb jumps to another target of your choice within 30 feet of that target. You make an attack roll against the new target and roll the damage again. Unless you cast the spell using a spell slot of level 2 or higher, the orb can't jump a second time. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, the damage increases by 1d8. The orb can jump at most a number of times equal to the level of the spell slot you expended, and each creature can be targeted only once per casting of this spell.", "Color Spray": "You unleash a dazzling display of flashing, multicolored light. Each creature in a 15-foot Cone extending from you makes a Constitution saving throw. A creature that fails is Blinded until your next turn ends.", "Command": "You speak a command of one word to a creature you can see within range. Unless the target succeeds on a Wisdom saving throw, it carries out the command on its next turn. Pick the command from the options below. **Approach.** The target travels toward you along the shortest, most direct path, and its turn ends if it moves to within 5 feet of you. **Drop.** The target lets go of everything it is holding, and then its turn ends. **Flee.** The target uses its turn to move away from you by the fastest means available to it. **Grovel.** The target has the Prone condition, and then its turn ends. **Halt.** During its turn, the target doesn't move and takes neither an action nor a Bonus Action. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, you can affect one more creature.", "Compelled Duel": "You attempt to force a creature into single combat with you. Choose one creature you can see within range. It makes a Wisdom saving throw. If it fails, it has Disadvantage on attack rolls against any creature other than you, and it can't willingly move to any space farther than 30 feet from you. Any one of these ends the spell: you make an attack roll that targets any creature besides the target, you cast a spell on any enemy besides the target, one of your allies deals damage to the target, or your turn ends while you are over 30 feet away from the target.", "Comprehend Languages": "Until the spell ends, you grasp the literal meaning of any language you hear spoken or see signed. You can also understand any written language you see, as long as you are touching the surface the words are written on. Reading one page of text this way takes about 1 minute. The spell can't decode symbols or secret messages.", "Create or Destroy Water": "Choose one of the following effects. **Create Water.** Up to 10 gallons of clean water appear in an open container within range. You can instead make the water fall as rain in a 30-foot Cube within range, which puts out any exposed flames in that area. **Destroy Water.** You destroy as much as 10 gallons of water held in an open container within range. You can instead destroy fog in a 30-foot Cube within range. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, you create or destroy 10 more gallons of water, or the Cube grows by 5 feet.", "Cure Wounds": "The creature you touch regains 2d8 Hit Points, and you add your spellcasting ability modifier to the amount regained. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, the healing increases by 2d8.", "Detect Evil and Good": "While the spell lasts, you can sense where any Aberration, Celestial, Elemental, Fey, Fiend, or Undead is within 30 feet of you. You can also sense whether the Hallow spell is in effect there and, if it is, where. The spell is stopped by a thin sheet of lead, 1 inch of metal, or 1 foot of wood, dirt, or stone.", "Detect Magic": "Until the spell ends, you can sense whether magical effects are present within 30 feet of you. Whenever you sense such effects, a Magic action lets you see a faint aura surrounding each visible creature or object in that area that carries the magic. If a spell created the effect, you also learn which school of magic that spell belongs to. The spell is stopped by a thin sheet of lead, 1 inch of metal, or 1 foot of wood, dirt, or stone.", "Detect Poison and Disease": "For the duration, you can sense where poisons, poisonous or venomous creatures, and magical contagions are within 30 feet of you, and in each case you can tell what kind of poison, creature, or contagion it is. The spell is stopped by a thin sheet of lead, 1 inch of metal, or 1 foot of wood, dirt, or stone.", "Disguise Self": "Until the spell ends, you change how you look, along with your clothing, armor, weapons, and any other belongings on your person. You can seem 1 foot shorter or taller, and you can look heavier or lighter. Your new form must have the same basic arrangement of limbs that you have, but beyond that, the illusion can be whatever you like. The changes made by this spell don't stand up to physical inspection. For instance, if you add a hat to your outfit with this spell, objects pass through the hat, and anyone who touches it feels nothing. A creature can discern that you are disguised only if it takes the Study action to inspect your appearance and its Intelligence (Investigation) check against your spell save DC succeeds.", "Dissonant Whispers": "Pick one creature you can see within range. A grating melody sounds inside its mind, and it makes a Wisdom saving throw. If it fails, it takes 3d6 Psychic damage, and it must use its Reaction right away, if it has one available, to move as far from you as it can by the safest route. If it succeeds, it takes half as much damage and suffers no other effect. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, the damage increases by 1d6.", "Divine Favor": "While the spell lasts, each of your attacks with a weapon deals an extra 1d4 Radiant damage when it hits.", "Divine Smite": "The target you just hit with a Melee weapon or an Unarmed Strike takes an extra 2d8 Radiant damage from that attack. If the target is a Fiend or an Undead, add another 1d8 to this damage. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, the damage rises by 1d8.", "Ensnaring Strike": "When your weapon hits the creature, vines burst into being and seize it, and it makes a Strength saving throw. A creature of Large size or bigger rolls this save with Advantage. A target that fails has the Restrained condition until the spell ends. If the target succeeds instead, the vines wither away and the spell is over. As long as it stays Restrained, the target takes 1d6 Piercing damage when each of its turns starts. Either the target or a creature within reach of the target can take an action to try ending the spell, rolling a Strength (Athletics) check against your spell save DC. A success ends the spell. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Add 1d6 to the damage for every spell slot level above 1.", "Entangle": "Clutching plants spring up out of the ground across a 20-foot square within range. While the spell lasts, they make the ground there Difficult Terrain, and they vanish once the spell ends. When you cast the spell, each creature in the area except you makes a Strength saving throw. A creature that fails has the Restrained condition until the spell ends. While Restrained by the plants, a creature can take an action to break loose: it makes a Strength (Athletics) check against your spell save DC, and if it succeeds, the plants no longer keep it Restrained.", "Expeditious Retreat": "When you cast the spell, you take the Dash action. Until the spell ends, you can take the Dash action again as a Bonus Action.", "Faerie Fire": "Every object in a 20-foot Cube within range gets an outline of light, colored blue, green, or violet as you choose. Each creature in the Cube makes a Dexterity saving throw and is outlined too if it fails. Until the spell ends, the outlined objects and affected creatures give off Dim Light in a 10-foot radius, and they gain no benefit from the Invisible condition. An attacker that can see an affected creature or object has Advantage on attack rolls against it.", "False Life": "You receive Temporary Hit Points equal to 2d4 + 4. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Add 5 Temporary Hit Points for every spell slot level above 1.", "Feather Fall": "Pick up to five creatures within range that are falling. Until the spell ends, each one's rate of descent is slowed to 60 feet per round. Any of them that touches down before the spell ends takes no falling damage, and the spell stops affecting it.", "Find Familiar": "**Material Component.** Burning incense worth at least 10 GP, which the spell consumes. A spirit enters your service as a familiar, taking an animal form of your choice: Owl, Cat, Bat, Raven, Rat, Hawk, Frog, Lizard, Octopus, Spider, Weasel, or another Beast with a Challenge Rating of 0. The familiar shows up in an unoccupied space within range. It uses the statistics of the form you chose (see appendix B), except that its creature type is Celestial, Fey, or Fiend (your choice) instead of Beast. It acts on its own, separately from you, yet it follows your commands. **Telepathic Connection.** You and your familiar can talk telepathically whenever it is no more than 100 feet away from you. Also, by taking a Bonus Action, you can look through its eyes and listen through its ears until your next turn starts, and you benefit from any special senses it has. Lastly, your familiar can deliver the touch of any spell you cast that has a range of touch. For this, the familiar has to be within 100 feet of you and must spend its Reaction as you cast the spell. **Combat.** Your familiar counts as an ally of yours and of your allies. It rolls Initiative for itself and acts on a turn of its own. It can't make attacks, but it is free to take other actions normally. **Disappearance of the Familiar.** If the familiar drops to 0 Hit Points, it disappears, and it comes back after you cast this spell again. You can take a Magic action to send the familiar away temporarily into a pocket dimension, or you can dismiss it permanently instead. While it waits in the pocket dimension, a Magic action lets you bring it back to an unoccupied space within 30 feet of you. Each time the familiar drops to 0 Hit Points or vanishes into the pocket dimension, whatever it was wearing or carrying stays behind in its space. **One Familiar Only.** You can have only one familiar at a time. Casting this spell while you already have a familiar makes it change into a new eligible form instead.", "Fog Cloud": "Choose a point within range. You fill a 20-foot-radius Sphere centered there with fog, and the area inside it is Heavily Obscured. The fog remains until the spell ends or until a strong wind disperses it (for example, a wind created by Gust of Wind). **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, the fog's radius grows by 20 feet.", "Goodberry": "Ten berries appear in your hand, infused with magic for the duration. A creature can take a Bonus Action to eat one berry. Eating a berry restores 1 Hit Point, and the berry gives enough nourishment to sustain a creature for one day. Any berries not eaten disappear when the spell ends.", "Grease": "Choose a point within range. Nonflammable grease spreads over the ground in a 10-foot square centered there, and for the duration that ground is Difficult Terrain. When the grease appears, every creature standing in it makes a Dexterity saving throw, and each one that fails has the Prone condition. Entering the area or ending a turn there also forces a creature to make the same save, and it falls Prone if it fails.", "Guiding Bolt": "You throw a bolt of light at a creature within range and make a ranged spell attack against it. A hit deals 4d6 Radiant damage to the target, and before your next turn ends, the next attack roll against it has Advantage. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, the damage rises by 1d6.", "Hail of Thorns": "As your Ranged weapon hits the creature, the spell makes a rain of thorns sprout from the weapon or its ammunition. The creature you hit and every creature within 5 feet of it make a Dexterity saving throw. A creature that fails takes 1d10 Piercing damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, the damage rises by 1d10.", "Healing Word": "Pick a creature within range that you can see. The creature regains Hit Points equal to your spellcasting ability modifier + 2d4. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 1 adds 2d4 to the healing.", "Hellish Rebuke": "You cast this spell as a Reaction when a creature damages you while it is within 60 feet of you and you can see it. Green flames briefly wrap around the creature that harmed you. It makes a Dexterity saving throw. On a failure, it takes 2d10 Fire damage, and on a success, it takes half as much. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** The damage goes up by 1d10 for each spell slot level above 1.", "Heroism": "You touch a willing creature and fill it with bravery. Until the spell ends, the creature has Immunity to the Frightened condition. Whenever one of its turns starts, it also gains Temporary Hit Points. Your spellcasting ability modifier sets how many it gains. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each spell slot level above 1 lets you affect one additional creature.", "Hex": "You curse a creature within range that you can see. Until the spell ends, whenever you hit the target with an attack roll, you deal an extra 1d6 Necrotic damage to it. When you cast the spell, you also pick one ability, and the target has Disadvantage on ability checks it makes with that ability. Should the target fall to 0 Hit Points while the spell is still active, a Bonus Action on one of your later turns lets you move the curse to a different creature. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** The longest you can maintain Concentration grows: a level 2 spell slot allows up to 4 hours, a level 3 or 4 slot allows up to 8 hours, and a slot of level 5 or higher allows up to 24 hours.", "Hunter's Mark": "You magically mark a creature within range that you can see as your quarry. Until the spell ends, whenever you hit the target with an attack roll, you deal an extra 1d6 Force damage to it. When you make a Wisdom (Perception or Survival) check to find the target, you have Advantage on it. Should the target fall to 0 Hit Points while the spell is still active, you can take a Bonus Action to shift the mark to a new creature within range that you can see. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** The maximum time you can keep Concentration grows: up to 8 hours with a level 3 or 4 spell slot, or up to 24 hours with a slot of level 5 or higher.", "Ice Knife": "You form a shard of ice and hurl it at one creature within range. Make a ranged spell attack against that creature, which takes 1d10 Piercing damage on a hit. Whether the attack hits or misses, the shard then bursts. The target and each other creature no more than 5 feet from it make a Dexterity saving throw, and each one that fails takes 2d6 Cold damage. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 1, the Cold damage increases by 1d6.", "Identify": "**Material Component.** A pearl worth at least 100 GP. You touch an object for the entire casting. If it is a magic item or another magical object, you discover what its properties are and how they are used, whether Attunement is needed, and its number of charges, if it has any. You also find out if any ongoing spells currently affect the item, and what those spells are. If a spell created the item, you learn the name of that spell. If you instead touch a creature for the entire casting, you find out which ongoing spells, if any, are affecting it at the moment.", "Illusory Script": "**Material Component.** Ink worth at least 10 GP, which the spell consumes. You write on parchment, paper, or some other suitable material and fill the writing with an illusion, which remains for the duration. As you cast the spell, you can designate any creatures. For you and them, the text looks ordinary, seems to be in your own handwriting, and carries the meaning you meant when writing it. Anyone else sees writing in an unknown or magical script that makes no sense. You can instead have the illusion alter the text's meaning, handwriting, and language, as long as the language is one you know. Dispelling the spell makes both the original script and the illusion vanish. A creature with Truesight can read the hidden message.", "Inflict Wounds": "A creature you touch makes a Constitution saving throw. It takes 2d10 Necrotic damage on a failure, or half as much on a success. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each spell slot level above 1 adds 1d10 to the damage.", "Jump": "You touch a willing creature. Once during each of its turns until the spell ends, the creature can spend 10 feet of movement to make a jump of up to 30 feet. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 1 lets you affect one additional creature.", "Longstrider": "You touch a creature, and its Speed increases by 10 feet for as long as the spell lasts. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 1 lets you affect one additional creature.", "Mage Armor": "You touch a willing creature that isn't wearing armor. While the spell lasts, the target's base Armor Class is 13 plus its Dexterity modifier. If the target puts on armor, the spell ends early.", "Magic Missile": "Three glowing darts of magical force spring into being. Each one hits a creature of your choice within range that you can see, dealing 1d4 + 1 Force damage to it. All the darts strike at the same moment, and you can aim them all at one creature or spread them among several. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 1 adds one more dart.", "Protection from Evil and Good": "**Material Component.** A flask of Holy Water worth at least 25 GP, which the spell consumes. Until the spell ends, a willing creature you touch is shielded from Aberrations, Celestials, Elementals, Fey, Fiends, and Undead. This protection brings several benefits. Attack rolls that creatures of those types make against the target have Disadvantage. The target also can't be possessed by them, and they can't give it the Charmed or Frightened condition. If such a creature has already possessed, Charmed, or Frightened the target, any new saving throw the target makes against that effect has Advantage.", "Purify Food and Drink": "Choose a point within range. Poison and rot vanish from all nonmagical food and drink within a 5-foot-radius Sphere centered there.", "Ray of Sickness": "You fire a sickly green ray at a creature within range, making a ranged spell attack against it. A hit deals 2d8 Poison damage to the target, which also has the Poisoned condition until your next turn ends. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each slot level above 1, the damage increases by 1d8.", "Sanctuary": "Choose a creature within range to ward. Until the spell ends, whenever a creature targets the warded creature with a damaging spell or an attack roll, it must first succeed on a Wisdom saving throw. If it fails, the creature either picks a different target or loses that attack or spell. The ward offers no protection against areas of effect. If the warded creature deals damage, casts a spell, or makes an attack roll, the spell ends.", "Searing Smite": "As your attack hits the target, the target takes an extra 1d6 Fire damage from it. While the spell lasts, each of the target's turns begins with it taking 1d6 Fire damage, after which it makes a Constitution saving throw. If it fails, the spell continues. If it succeeds, the spell ends. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each slot level above 1, all the damage increases by 1d6.", "Shield": "A barrier of magical force that can't be perceived shields you. Until the start of your next turn, you gain a +5 bonus to Armor Class, which applies against the triggering attack too, and Magic Missile deals no damage to you.", "Shield of Faith": "Choose a creature within range. A shimmering field envelops it, and it gains a +2 bonus to Armor Class until the spell ends.", "Silent Image": "You create the image of a creature, an object, or some other visible phenomenon, no larger than a 15-foot Cube. It shows up at a spot within range and remains for the duration. The image is only visual, with no sound, smell, or other sensory effects. By taking a Magic action, you can send the image to any spot within range. As it moves, you can change its appearance so its movements look natural for the image. For example, an image of a creature that you move can be altered so it looks like it is walking. Touching the image physically reveals that it is an illusion, because things can pass through it. When a creature takes a Study action to inspect the image, it makes an Intelligence (Investigation) check against your spell save DC and, if it succeeds, determines that the image is an illusion. A creature that discerns the illusion for what it is can see through the image.", "Sleep": "Choose a point within range. Each creature you pick inside a 5-foot-radius Sphere centered there must make a Wisdom saving throw. A creature that fails has the Incapacitated condition until its next turn ends, and then it must repeat the save. A second failure leaves it with the Unconscious condition for the duration. For a given target, the spell ends if it takes damage or if someone within 5 feet of it uses an action to shake it out of the effect. Elves and other creatures that don't sleep, as well as creatures with Immunity to the Exhaustion condition, automatically succeed on saving throws against this spell.", "Speak with Animals": "For the duration, you can understand Beasts and talk with them out loud. You can also apply any of the skill options of the Influence action to them. Most Beasts don't have much to say about anything unrelated to survival or companionship. At the very least, though, a Beast can tell you about nearby locations and monsters, including anything it has perceived within the past day.", "Tasha's Hideous Laughter": "Pick a creature within range that you can see. That creature must make a Wisdom saving throw. A failed save leaves the target with the Prone and Incapacitated conditions until the spell ends, and while that lasts it laughs uncontrollably if it is capable of laughter. While the spell lasts, it also can't end its own Prone condition. It repeats the Wisdom saving throw whenever one of its turns ends and whenever it takes damage, and a saving throw caused by damage has Advantage. If the creature succeeds, the spell ends. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For every slot level above 1, you can target one additional creature.", "Tenser's Floating Disk": "You create a flat, circular plane of force 3 feet across and 1 inch thick. Pick an unoccupied space within range that you can see. The disk appears there, floating 3 feet above the ground, and it lasts for the duration. The disk can bear up to 500 pounds. Placing more weight than that on it ends the spell, and everything on the disk drops to the ground. As long as you stay within 20 feet of the disk, it doesn't move. If you go farther than 20 feet away, it follows you so that it stays within 20 feet of you. It can travel over uneven terrain, up or down stairs, slopes, and similar features, but it can't cross a change in elevation of 10 feet or more. For example, it can't cross a pit 10 feet deep, and if it was created at the bottom of such a pit, it couldn't get out. The spell ends if you get more than 100 feet away from the disk (usually because an obstacle keeps it from following you).", "Thunderous Smite": "You cast this spell as a Bonus Action right after your attack with a Melee weapon or an Unarmed Strike hits a target. Thunder rings out from your strike, audible within 300 feet of you, and the attack deals an extra 2d6 Thunder damage to the target. If the target is a creature, it also makes a Strength saving throw. On a failure, it is pushed 10 feet away from you and has the Prone condition. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each spell slot level above 1 adds 1d6 to the damage.", "Thunderwave": "A wave of thunderous force bursts from you. Creatures inside a 15-foot Cube that originates from you must each make a Constitution saving throw. A creature that fails takes 2d8 Thunder damage and gets shoved 10 feet away from you. A creature that succeeds takes half as much damage and isn't pushed. Unsecured objects lying completely within the Cube are also shoved 10 feet away from you, and the boom of thunder is audible up to 300 feet away. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each spell slot level above 1 adds 1d8 to the damage.", "Unseen Servant": "An Invisible, mindless, shapeless, Medium force appears on the ground in an unoccupied space within range, ready to carry out simple tasks you command until the spell ends. The servant's Armor Class is 10, it has 1 Hit Point and a Strength score of 2, and it can't make attacks. If its Hit Points drop to 0, the spell ends. You can mentally command the servant as a Bonus Action, no more than once on each of your turns. It then moves as far as 15 feet and interacts with an object. It can handle simple tasks a human could manage, such as cleaning, mending, fetching things, folding clothes, serving food, pouring drinks, and lighting fires. After receiving a command, the servant works on the task as best it can until finishing, then waits for your next command. Ordering the servant to do something that would take it farther than 60 feet from you ends the spell.", "Witch Bolt": "A beam of crackling energy streaks toward a creature within range and holds an arc of lightning between the two of you. You make a ranged spell attack against that creature, dealing 2d12 Lightning damage on a hit. Then, as a Bonus Action on each of your later turns, you can automatically deal 1d12 Lightning damage to the target. You can do this even if your first attack missed. If the target is ever beyond the spell's range or has Total Cover from you, the spell ends. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each spell slot level above 1 adds 1d12 to the initial damage.", "Wrathful Smite": "You cast this spell as a Bonus Action right after your attack with a Melee weapon or an Unarmed Strike hits a creature. The attack deals an extra 1d6 Necrotic damage to the target, which must also make a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure, it has the Frightened condition until the spell ends. The Frightened target repeats the saving throw whenever one of its turns ends, and a success ends the spell on it. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each spell slot level above 1 adds 1d6 to the damage.", "Aid": "Pick up to three creatures within range. While the spell lasts, the Hit Point maximum and the current Hit Points of each target go up by 5. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** The Hit Points of each target rise by an extra 5 for each spell slot level above 2.", "Alter Self": "You reshape your body. Pick one of the options below. Its effects last for the duration, and during that time you can take a Magic action to swap the chosen option for a different one. **Aquatic Adaptation.** Gills sprout on you and webbing grows between your fingers. Breathing underwater becomes possible for you, and you have a Swim Speed equal to your Speed. **Change Appearance.** You change how you look. You decide every aspect of your appearance, such as your height, weight, facial features, voice, hair length, coloring, and other distinguishing traits. You can look like a member of a different species, but none of your statistics change. You can't take on the look of a creature of another size, and your basic shape stays as it is. For example, if you walk on two legs, this spell can't make you a four-legged creature. While the spell lasts, you can take a Magic action to alter your look in this way again. **Natural Weapons.** You grow one of these: claws, fangs, horns, or hooves. Claws deal Slashing damage, fangs and horns deal Piercing damage, and hooves deal Bludgeoning damage. When your Unarmed Strike deals damage with the new growth, it deals 1d6 damage of that type in place of the Unarmed Strike's normal damage. You also use your spellcasting ability modifier for its attack and damage rolls in place of Strength.", "Animal Messenger": "Pick a Tiny Beast you can see within range. Unless it succeeds on a Charisma saving throw, it tries to carry a message for you. A target whose Challenge Rating is anything other than 0 succeeds automatically. You name a place you have been to and give a general description of the recipient, such as \"someone in the town guard's uniform\" or \"a red-haired dwarf in a pointed hat.\" You also tell the Beast a message of up to twenty-five words. For the duration, the Beast heads toward that place, covering roughly 25 miles every 24 hours, or 50 miles if it can fly. On arrival, the Beast gives your message to the creature you described, imitating the way you communicated it. Should the spell end before the Beast arrives, the message is lost, and the Beast heads back to the spot where the spell was cast. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 2, the duration grows by 48 hours.", "Arcane Lock": "**Material Component.** Gold dust worth at least 25 GP, which the spell consumes. Touch a door, window, gate, container, or hatch that is closed. It becomes magically locked for the duration. No nonmagical means can unlock it. You, plus any creatures you name as you cast the spell, can still open and close the object in spite of the lock. You can also choose a password. Speaking it within 5 feet of the object unlocks the object for 1 minute.", "Arcane Vigor": "You draw on your own life force to heal. Roll one or two of your Hit Point Dice that haven't been expended. You regain Hit Points equal to the total rolled plus your spellcasting ability modifier, and the dice you rolled are then expended. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 2, you can roll one more unexpended Hit Point Die.", "Augury": "**Material Component.** Divinatory tokens worth at least 25 GP, such as specially marked sticks, bones, or cards. An otherworldly entity sends you an omen about the outcome of a course of action you intend to take within the next 30 minutes. The Dungeon Master picks the omen from the Omens table below. The spell doesn't take into account circumstances that could change the outcome, such as other spells. If you cast it more than once before finishing a Long Rest, every casting after the first adds a cumulative 25 percent chance that you get no answer. **Omens.** Weal means the results will be good. Woe means they will be bad. Weal and woe means they will be both good and bad. Indifference means they will be neither good nor bad.", "Barkskin": "The willing creature you touch gets skin that resembles tree bark until the spell ends. While that lasts, the creature's Armor Class is 17 if it would otherwise be lower.", "Beast Sense": "Touch a willing Beast. Until the spell ends, you can perceive through its senses in addition to your own. While you perceive through the Beast's senses, you gain the benefit of any special senses it has.", "Blindness/Deafness": "Choose one creature you can see within range. Unless it succeeds on a Constitution saving throw, it has your choice of the Blinded or the Deafened condition for the duration. Each time the target finishes one of its turns, it repeats the saving throw, and a success ends the spell on it. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 2, you can target one more creature.", "Blur": "Your body takes on a blurred look. For the duration, attack rolls made against you by any creature have Disadvantage. This effect doesn't apply to an attacker that perceives you with Blindsight or Truesight.", "Calm Emotions": "Choose a point within range. Centered on it is a 20-foot-radius Sphere, and every Humanoid inside that Sphere makes a Charisma saving throw. On a failure, it is affected by one of the two effects below, which you pick separately for each creature. First option: until the spell ends, the creature has Immunity to both the Charmed and the Frightened conditions, and if it already has either condition, that condition is suppressed for the duration. Second option: the creature becomes Indifferent toward creatures of your choice that it is Hostile toward. This indifference ends if the creature takes damage or sees any of its allies take damage. When the spell ends, the creature's attitude goes back to normal.", "Cloud of Daggers": "You fill a 5-foot Cube with whirling daggers. Center the Cube on a point within range. Any creature inside the Cube when it appears takes 4d4 Slashing damage. The same damage hits a creature that enters the Cube, that ends its turn there, or whose space the Cube moves into, but a creature can take this damage no more than once per turn. As a Magic action on each of your later turns, you can teleport the Cube as far as 30 feet. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 2, the damage increases by 2d4.", "Continual Flame": "**Material Component.** Ruby dust worth at least 50 GP, which the spell consumes. You touch an object, and a flame bursts from it. The flame casts Bright Light within a 20-foot radius, plus Dim Light for 20 feet past that. Though it looks like an ordinary fire, it gives off no heat and burns no fuel. You can cover or hide the flame, but it can't be smothered or quenched.", "Cordon of Arrows": "Touch up to four nonmagical Arrows or Bolts and stick them into the ground in your space. Until the spell ends, nothing can physically pull the ammunition out of the ground. The first time on a turn that any creature besides you moves into a space no more than 30 feet from the ammunition, and whenever such a creature ends its turn in a space like that, one piece of the ammunition flies up and strikes at it. Unless the creature succeeds on a Dexterity saving throw, it takes 2d4 Piercing damage. That piece of ammunition is then destroyed. Once no ammunition is left planted in the ground, the spell ends. As you cast the spell, you can name any creatures you like for the spell to ignore. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 2, the number of pieces of ammunition you can affect increases by two.", "Crown of Madness": "Choose one creature you can see within range. Unless it succeeds on a Wisdom saving throw, it has the Charmed condition for the duration. A creature that isn't a Humanoid succeeds on the saving throw automatically. A ghostly crown appears on the head of the Charmed target. On each of its turns, the target must use its action before moving to make a melee attack. You mentally choose the creature it attacks, which can be any creature except the target itself. If you choose no creature, or no creature is within reach of the target, it can act normally on that turn. Each time the target finishes one of its turns, it repeats the saving throw, and a success ends the spell on it. To keep control of the target, every one of your later turns requires you to take the Magic action. Otherwise, the spell ends.", "Darkness": "Pick a point within range. For the duration, magical Darkness spreads out from it, filling a 15-foot-radius Sphere. Darkvision can't penetrate this Darkness, and nonmagical light can't light it up. You can instead target an object that no one is wearing or carrying. The Darkness then fills a 15-foot Emanation coming from that object. Putting something opaque over the object, like a bowl or a helm, blocks the Darkness. A spell of level 2 or lower is dispelled if it created an area of Bright Light or Dim Light that overlaps any part of this spell's area.", "Darkvision": "The willing creature you touch has Darkvision out to 150 feet while the spell lasts.", "Detect Thoughts": "Casting the spell activates one of the two effects described below, your choice. Until the spell ends, a Magic action on any of your later turns lets you activate either one. **Sense Thoughts.** You sense any thoughts within 30 feet of you belonging to creatures that are telepathic or know languages. The thoughts themselves stay unread, but you know a thinking creature is present. The spell is stopped by a thin sheet of lead, 1 inch of metal, or 1 foot of wood, dirt, or stone. **Read Thoughts.** Your target is one creature within 30 feet of you that you either can see or noticed with Sense Thoughts. You learn what the target is thinking about most at this moment. If it knows no languages and isn't telepathic, you learn nothing. On your next turn, you can take a Magic action to try to probe deeper into its mind. If you do, the target makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure, you discern its reasoning, its emotions, and something that weighs heavily on its mind (such as a worry, a love, or a hatred). On a success, the spell ends. In either case, the target knows you are probing its mind, and until you turn your attention away from its mind, it can use an action on its turn to attempt an Intelligence (Arcana) check against your spell save DC, which ends the spell if it succeeds.", "Dragon's Breath": "Touch one willing creature and pick a damage type for the spell from Acid, Cold, Fire, Lightning, and Poison. For as long as the spell lasts, that creature can take a Magic action to breathe out a 15-foot Cone. Every creature in the Cone makes a Dexterity saving throw. On a failure, it takes 3d6 damage of the type you picked, and on a success, it takes half that damage. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 2, the damage rises by 1d6.", "Enhance Ability": "Touch a creature and pick Strength, Dexterity, Intelligence, Wisdom, or Charisma. Until the spell ends, the target has Advantage on ability checks that use the ability you picked. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 2, you can target one more creature, and you can pick a different ability for each target.", "Enlarge/Reduce": "For the duration, the spell makes a target that you can see within range, either a creature or an object, grow larger or smaller, according to the effect you choose below. An object you target can't be worn or carried. An unwilling creature you target can make a Constitution saving throw, and if it succeeds, the spell has no effect. Everything a targeted creature wears and carries changes size along with it. Any item the creature drops returns to its normal size right away. A thrown weapon or a piece of ammunition goes back to its normal size as soon as it hits or misses a target. **Enlarge.** The target grows by one size category, for example from Medium to Large. It makes Strength checks and Strength saving throws with Advantage. When it hits with its enlarged weapons or with Unarmed Strikes, the attack deals an extra 1d4 damage. **Reduce.** The target shrinks by one size category, for example from Medium to Small. It makes Strength checks and Strength saving throws with Disadvantage. When it hits with its reduced weapons or with Unarmed Strikes, the attack deals 1d4 less damage, but this can't lower the damage below 1.", "Enthrall": "You spin a distracting stream of words. Creatures of your choice within range that you can see must make a Wisdom saving throw. A creature that you or your companions are fighting succeeds on this save automatically. On a failure, a target takes a -10 penalty to its Passive Perception and to Wisdom (Perception) checks until the spell ends.", "Find Steed": "You summon an otherworldly creature that appears as a faithful steed in an unoccupied space you choose within range. The creature uses the Otherworldly Steed stat block. Any steed you already have from this spell is replaced by the new one. The steed takes the look of a Large animal that can be ridden, chosen by you, for example a horse, an elk, a camel, or a dire wolf. Every time you cast the spell, you pick the steed's creature type (Celestial, Fey, or Fiend), and that choice sets certain traits in its stat block. **Otherworldly Steed.** Large Celestial, Fey, or Fiend (your choice), Neutral. Armor Class equals 10 + 1 per spell level. Hit Points equal 5 + 10 per spell level, and its number of Hit Point Dice (d10s) equals the spell's level. Speed 60 feet, and it also has a Fly Speed of 60 feet when the spell is level 4 or higher. Ability scores: Strength 18 (modifier +4), Dexterity 12 (modifier +1), Constitution 14 (modifier +2), Intelligence 6 (modifier -2), Wisdom 12 (modifier +1), Charisma 8 (modifier -1), with each saving throw bonus the same as the modifier. Passive Perception 11. Telepathy 1 mile, usable only with you. Challenge Rating None (0 experience points), and its Proficiency Bonus is the same as yours. **Life Bond.** Whenever a spell of level 1 or higher restores Hit Points to you while the steed is within 5 feet of you, the steed regains that many Hit Points too. **Otherworldly Slam.** Melee Attack Roll, with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier and a reach of 5 feet. On a hit, the target takes 1d8 plus the spell's level in damage, which is Radiant for a Celestial, Psychic for a Fey, or Necrotic for a Fiend. **Fell Glare.** Fiend only. A Bonus Action that recharges after a Long Rest. The steed picks one creature it can see within 60 feet of it, and that creature makes a Wisdom saving throw against your spell save DC. If the creature fails, it has the Frightened condition until your next turn ends. **Fey Step.** Fey only. A Bonus Action that recharges after a Long Rest. The steed teleports with its rider to an unoccupied space you choose that is no more than 60 feet away from the steed. **Healing Touch.** Celestial only. A Bonus Action that recharges after a Long Rest. It restores Hit Points equal to 2d8 plus the spell's level to one creature within 5 feet of the steed. **Combat.** The steed counts as an ally of yours and of your allies. During combat, it uses your Initiative count, and when you ride it, it serves as a controlled mount under the mounted combat rules. Whenever you have the Incapacitated condition, the steed's turn comes right after yours, and it acts independently with your protection as its focus. **Disappearance of the Steed.** The steed vanishes if you die or if it drops to 0 Hit Points, and it leaves behind whatever it was wearing or carrying. On a later casting of this spell, you can choose to summon either the steed that vanished or a new one. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** The stat block's references to the spell's level use the level of the slot you expended.", "Find Traps": "You become aware of any trap within range that is also within your line of sight. For this spell, a trap is any object or mechanism made to cause damage or some other danger. The spell would therefore detect a mechanical pit trap or the Alarm or Glyph of Warding spell, but it wouldn't uncover a natural weakness in the floor, a ceiling that is unstable, or a sinkhole that is hidden. The spell tells you that a trap is present but not where it is. You do learn, in general terms, what kind of danger each sensed trap poses.", "Flame Blade": "A fiery blade forms in your free hand. It is about the size and shape of a scimitar and stays until the spell ends. Releasing it makes the blade vanish, but you can call it forth again as a Bonus Action. You can take a Magic action to make a melee spell attack with the blade. If it hits, the attack deals 3d6 Fire damage plus your spellcasting ability modifier. The blade gives off Bright Light in a 10-foot radius, plus Dim Light for 10 feet beyond that. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 2, the damage rises by 1d6.", "Flaming Sphere": "You conjure a sphere of fire, 5 feet across, in an unoccupied space on the ground within range, and it remains for the duration. Each creature that finishes its turn within 5 feet of the sphere must make a Dexterity saving throw. It takes 2d6 Fire damage on a failure or half as much on a success. As a Bonus Action, you can roll the sphere along the ground up to 30 feet. Moving the sphere into a creature's space forces that creature to make the save against it, and the sphere then halts for the rest of the turn. While moving the sphere, you can guide it over barriers no more than 5 feet tall and jump it across pits no more than 10 feet wide. Any flammable object the sphere touches catches fire unless someone is wearing or carrying it. The sphere gives off Bright Light in a 20-foot radius, plus Dim Light for 20 feet beyond that. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 2, the damage rises by 1d6.", "Gentle Repose": "**Material Component.** 2 Copper Pieces, which the spell consumes. Touch a corpse or some other remains. Until the spell ends, decay can't affect the target, and it can't be turned into an Undead. The spell also in effect lengthens the window for bringing the target back to life, since the days it spends under this spell aren't counted toward the time limit of spells like Raise Dead.", "Gust of Wind": "For the duration, strong wind blows from you in a direction you choose, forming a Line that is 60 feet long and 10 feet wide. Every creature in the Line makes a Strength saving throw, and each one that fails is pushed 15 feet away from you along the Line's direction. Any creature that ends its turn in the Line has to make the same save. Moving toward you costs a creature in the Line 2 feet of movement for every 1 foot. The wind scatters gas or vapor and blows out candles and similar unprotected flames in the area. Protected flames, such as those in lanterns, dance wildly and have a 50 percent chance of going out. On each of your later turns, you can change the direction the Line blows from you by taking a Bonus Action.", "Heat Metal": "Pick a manufactured metal object within range that you can see, such as a metal weapon or a suit of Medium or Heavy metal armor. You make the object glow red-hot. When you cast the spell, any creature that is touching the object takes 2d8 Fire damage. Until the spell ends, while the object is within range, you can repeat this damage as a Bonus Action on each of your later turns. Whenever a creature holding or wearing the object takes the damage, it makes a Constitution saving throw, and on a failure it drops the object if it can. If it keeps the object, then until your next turn starts, it has Disadvantage on attack rolls and ability checks. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** The damage rises by 1d8 for each spell slot level above 2.", "Hold Person": "Choose one Humanoid in range that you can see. Unless it succeeds on a Wisdom saving throw, it has the Paralyzed condition while the spell lasts. When each of its turns ends, it tries the save again, and a success ends the spell on it. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 2 lets you affect one additional Humanoid.", "Invisibility": "You touch a creature, and it has the Invisible condition for the duration. The spell ends early the moment the target has made an attack roll, dealt damage, or cast a spell. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 2 lets you affect one additional creature.", "Knock": "Pick an object within range that you can see. It might be a door, a box or chest, a padlock, a set of manacles, or another object that has a mundane or magical way of blocking access. If a mundane lock holds the target shut, or if it is stuck or barred, it becomes unlocked, unstuck, or unbarred. When the object has more than one lock, only one of them opens. If Arcane Lock is holding the target shut, that spell is suppressed for 10 minutes, and the target can be opened and closed while the suppression lasts. Casting this spell makes a loud knock ring out from the target, audible as far as 300 feet away.", "Lesser Restoration": "You touch a creature and remove one of these conditions from it: Blinded, Deafened, Paralyzed, or Poisoned.", "Levitate": "Choose one creature or loose object within range that you can see. It rises vertically by up to 20 feet and stays suspended there for the duration. The spell can lift an object only if it weighs 500 pounds or less. If the creature is unwilling, a successful Constitution saving throw leaves it unaffected. To move, the target has to push or pull on a fixed surface or object it can reach, like a ceiling or a wall, which lets it move as though climbing. On your turn, you can shift the target's altitude up or down by as much as 20 feet. When the target is you, you can do this as part of your move. When it isn't, you need a Magic action to move the target, and the target must remain within the spell's range. If it is still aloft when the spell ends, the target drifts gently down to the ground.", "Locate Animals or Plants": "Name or describe a particular kind of Beast, Plant creature, or nonmagical plant. If any creature or plant of that kind is within 5 miles, you learn the direction and distance to the nearest one.", "Locate Object": "Name or describe an object that is familiar to you. If it is within 1,000 feet of you, you sense which direction it is in, and if it is moving, you also know which way it is heading. The spell can find a specific object you know, provided you have seen it from close up, no more than 30 feet away, at least once. As an alternative, it can find the nearest object of a particular kind, for example a certain type of weapon, tool, piece of furniture, jewelry, or apparel. Lead of any thickness on the direct path between you and the object keeps the spell from finding it.", "Magic Mouth": "**Material Component.** Jade dust worth at least 10 GP, which the spell consumes. You place a message inside an object within range, and the message is spoken aloud when a trigger condition is met. Pick an object you can see that no other creature is wearing or carrying. Then speak the message. It can be at most 25 words long, but you can take up to 10 minutes to deliver it. Last, decide what circumstance will make the spell deliver your message. When that trigger happens, a magical mouth forms on the object and speaks the message in your voice, at the same volume you used. If the object has a mouth or something that looks like one (a statue's mouth, for example), the magical mouth appears there, so the words seem to come out of the object's mouth. When you cast the spell, you choose whether it ends after delivering the message or remains and repeats the message every time the trigger happens. The trigger can be as broad or as specific as you want, but it must be based on things that can be seen or heard within 30 feet of the object. You could, for instance, have the mouth speak whenever a creature moves to within 30 feet of the object, or whenever a silver bell sounds no more than 30 feet away from it.", "Magic Weapon": "You touch a nonmagical weapon. Until the spell ends, it turns into a magic weapon with a +1 bonus to its attack rolls and damage rolls. Casting the spell again ends it early. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** With a level 3, 4, or 5 spell slot, the bonus rises to +2. With a slot of level 6 or higher, it rises to +3.", "Melf's Acid Arrow": "You send a shimmering green arrow streaking at a target within range, where it bursts into a shower of acid. Make a ranged spell attack against the target. If the attack hits, the target takes 4d4 Acid damage at once and another 2d4 Acid damage when its next turn ends. If it misses, the arrow splashes the target with acid, dealing only half the initial damage. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 2 adds 1d4 to both the initial damage and the later damage.", "Mind Spike": "You stab a spike of psionic energy into the mind of a creature within range that you can see. The target attempts a Wisdom saving throw. It takes 3d8 Psychic damage on a failure, or half as much on a success. If it fails, you also always know where it is until the spell ends, but only as long as you and it share the same plane of existence. During that time, it is impossible for the target to become hidden from you, and the Invisible condition gives it no benefit where you are concerned. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 2 adds 1d8 to the damage.", "Mirror Image": "Three illusory copies of you appear in your space. For the duration, they move along with you and imitate what you do, constantly trading places so no one can tell which figure is the real you. Each time an attack roll from a creature hits you while the spell lasts, roll a d6 for every duplicate you still have. If at least one die shows 3 or higher, a duplicate takes the hit in your place and is destroyed. Apart from that, the duplicates ignore all damage and other effects. Once all three duplicates are destroyed, the spell ends. This spell has no effect on a creature that is Blinded or that has Blindsight or Truesight.", "Misty Step": "Silvery mist briefly surrounds you, and you teleport to an unoccupied space you can see, up to 30 feet away.", "Moonbeam": "Pick a point within range. A silvery shaft of pale light beams down around it as a 5-foot-radius, 40-foot-high Cylinder. Dim Light fills the Cylinder until the spell ends. On your later turns, a Magic action lets you shift the Cylinder up to 60 feet. As the Cylinder appears, each creature inside it attempts a Constitution saving throw. A creature that fails takes 2d10 Radiant damage, and if it is shape-shifted (by the Polymorph spell, for example), it changes back into its true form and is unable to shape-shift again until it is out of the Cylinder. A creature that succeeds takes half as much damage and nothing else. A creature must also make the save when the Cylinder moves into the creature's space, when the creature enters it, and when the creature ends its turn inside it. No creature makes this save more than once in a turn. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 2 adds 1d10 to the damage.", "Nystul's Magic Aura": "With a touch, you put an illusion on a willing creature or on an object that no one is wearing or carrying. A creature gets the Mask effect and an object gets the False Aura effect, both described below. The effect lasts for the duration. Casting the spell on the same target daily for 30 days makes the illusion last until dispelled. **Mask (Creature).** Pick a creature type other than the target's real one. Spells and other magical effects treat the target as though it belonged to the type you picked. **False Aura (Object).** You alter the way the target looks to spells and magical effects that sense magical auras, such as Detect Magic. A nonmagical object can be made to seem magical, a magic item can be made to seem nonmagical, or you can change the object's aura so it seems to belong to a school of magic of your choice.", "Pass without Trace": "For the duration, a concealing aura spreads from you in a 30-foot Emanation. While in that aura, you and any creatures you choose leave no tracks and gain a +10 bonus to Dexterity (Stealth) checks.", "Phantasmal Force": "Choose a creature you can see within range and try to plant an illusion in its mind. It makes an Intelligence saving throw. If it fails, a phantasmal creature, object, or other phenomenon takes shape for the duration. It can be no larger than a 10-foot Cube, and only the target can perceive it. It has sound, temperature, and other sensory qualities. The target can examine the phantasm by taking a Study action, which calls for an Intelligence (Investigation) check against your spell save DC. A success shows the target that the phantasm is only an illusion, and the spell ends. As long as the spell affects it, the target treats the phantasm as real and explains away any illogical results of interacting with it. For example, a target that steps onto a phantasmal bridge and lives through the fall is convinced the bridge exists and that some other cause made it fall. If the phantasm portrays a dangerous creature or hazard, it can even harm the affected target. On each of your turns, if the target is inside the phantasm's area or within 5 feet of it, the phantasm can deal 2d8 Psychic damage to it. The target perceives this damage as whatever type suits the illusion.", "Prayer of Healing": "Choose up to five creatures. Each one that stays within range for the entire casting gains the benefits of a Short Rest and also regains 2d8 Hit Points. Once a creature benefits from this spell, it must finish a Long Rest before the spell can affect it again. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each slot level above 2, the healing increases by 1d8.", "Protection from Poison": "Your touch ends the Poisoned condition on a creature. For the duration, that creature has Resistance to Poison damage and has Advantage on any saving throw to avoid or end the Poisoned condition.", "Ray of Enfeeblement": "You fire a beam of draining energy at a creature within range, and the target makes a Constitution saving throw. If it succeeds, the first attack roll it makes before your following turn starts has Disadvantage. For the duration, a target that fails makes Strength-based D20 Tests with Disadvantage and subtracts 1d8 from every damage roll it makes. The target repeats the save when each of its turns ends, and the spell ends on a success.", "Rope Trick": "You touch a rope. One end of it floats upward until the rope hangs straight up, perpendicular to the ground, or until it reaches a ceiling. At the top end of the rope, an Invisible 3-foot-by-5-foot portal opens into an extradimensional space that lasts until the spell ends. Creatures reach that space by climbing the rope, and the rope can be pulled up into the space or dropped out of it. The space can hold as many as eight Medium or smaller creatures. Attacks, spells, and other effects can't enter or leave the space, but creatures inside can look out through the portal. Anything inside the space drops out when the spell ends.", "Scorching Ray": "You fling three rays of fire, aiming them at a single target within range or at several targets. Make a separate ranged spell attack for each ray. A ray that hits deals 2d6 Fire damage to its target. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each slot level above 2, you create one more ray.", "See Invisibility": "Until the spell ends, you can see creatures and objects with the Invisible condition as though they were visible. You can also see into the Ethereal Plane, where creatures and objects have a ghostly appearance.", "Shatter": "A loud noise bursts from a point you choose within range. A Sphere with a 10-foot radius forms around that point, and each creature in it makes a Constitution saving throw. A creature takes 3d8 Thunder damage on a failure, or half as much on a success. Constructs have Disadvantage on this save. The damage also applies to each nonmagical object in the spell's area that no one is wearing or carrying. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each slot level above 2, the damage increases by 1d8.", "Shining Smite": "Your strike deals an extra 2d6 Radiant damage to the target. For as long as the spell lasts, attack rolls against the target have Advantage, the target can't benefit from the Invisible condition, and Bright Light spreads from it in a 5-foot radius. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each slot level above 2, the damage increases by 1d6.", "Silence": "Choose a point within range. For the duration, inside a 20-foot-radius Sphere centered there, no sound can be created, and no sound can pass through the Sphere. Creatures and objects that are completely inside the Sphere have Immunity to Thunder damage. A creature completely inside it also has the Deafened condition. No one can cast a spell with a Verbal component there.", "Spider Climb": "For the duration, a willing creature you touch can climb up, down, and across vertical surfaces and move along ceilings, with its hands left free. It also has a Climb Speed equal to its Speed. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each slot level above 2, you can target one more creature.", "Spike Growth": "Choose a point within range. In a 20-foot-radius Sphere centered there, the ground sprouts hard thorns and spikes, turning the area into Difficult Terrain for the duration. A creature takes 2d4 Piercing damage for every 5 feet it moves into or within the area. The ground's transformation is camouflaged to seem natural. A creature that couldn't see the area when the spell was cast can recognize the terrain as hazardous before entering it only by taking a Search action and succeeding on a Wisdom (Perception or Survival) check made against your spell save DC.", "Spiritual Weapon": "A spectral force shaped like a weapon of your choice floats into being in a space you pick within range, and it remains for the duration. As part of the casting, you can make one melee spell attack against a single creature within 5 feet of the force. A hit deals 1d8 + your spellcasting ability modifier in Force damage. After that, you can take a Bonus Action on each later turn to shift the force as far as 20 feet and attack again, this time targeting a creature within 5 feet of the force. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 2 adds 1d8 to the damage.", "Suggestion": "Pick one creature within range that you can see and that can hear and understand you, and suggest a course of activity to it in 25 words or fewer. The suggestion has to sound achievable, and it can't include anything that would clearly cause damage to the target or its allies. For example, you might tell the creature to bring you the key that opens the cult's treasure vault, or to stop fighting, leave the library peacefully, and never come back. The target makes a Wisdom saving throw. If the save fails, the target is Charmed for the duration. This effect ends early if you or any of your allies deal damage to it. While Charmed this way, the target carries out the suggestion as well as it can. The activity can last for the whole duration, but if the target can finish it in less time, the spell ends for that target once the activity is done.", "Summon Beast": "**Material Component.** A feather, a tuft of fur, and a fish tail inside a gilded acorn worth at least 200 GP. A bestial spirit answers your call, appearing in an unoccupied space within range that you can see. It uses the Bestial Spirit stat block below. As you cast the spell, pick an environment: Air, Land, or Water. The spirit takes on the look of an animal of your choice that is native to that environment, and the environment decides certain details of its stat block. The spirit disappears when the spell ends or when its Hit Points fall to 0. The spirit counts as an ally to you and your allies. During combat, it acts on your Initiative count, taking its turn right after yours. It follows your spoken commands, and giving them takes no action on your part. If you give it no commands, it takes the Dodge action and spends its movement staying out of danger. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Wherever the stat block refers to the spell's level, use the level of the slot you expended. **Bestial Spirit.** Small Beast, Neutral. Armor Class: 11 + the spell's level. Hit Points: 20 (Air only) or 30 (Land and Water only), plus 5 for each spell level above 2. Speed: 30 feet, plus a Climb Speed of 30 feet (Land only), a Fly Speed of 60 feet (Air only), and a Swim Speed of 30 feet (Water only). Ability scores: Strength 18 (+4 modifier), Dexterity 11 (+0 modifier), Constitution 16 (+3 modifier), Intelligence 4 (-3 modifier), Wisdom 14 (+2 modifier), Charisma 5 (-3 modifier), and each saving throw modifier matches its ability modifier. Senses: Darkvision 60 feet, Passive Perception 12. Languages: it understands the languages you know. Challenge Rating: none (0 experience points), and its Proficiency Bonus equals yours. **Flyby (Air Only).** Flying out of an enemy's reach doesn't provoke Opportunity Attacks against the spirit. **Pack Tactics (Land and Water Only).** If at least one ally of the spirit is within 5 feet of a creature and that ally isn't Incapacitated, the spirit's attack rolls against that creature have Advantage. **Water Breathing (Water Only).** The spirit is able to breathe only underwater. **Multiattack.** The spirit makes Rend attacks equal in number to half the spell's level, rounded down. **Rend.** Melee attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 5 feet. A hit deals 1d8 + 4 + the spell's level in Piercing damage.", "Warding Bond": "**Material Component.** A pair of platinum rings, each worth at least 50 GP, which you and the target must each wear for the duration. You touch a willing creature other than yourself and forge a mystic connection between the two of you that lasts until the spell ends. While that creature is within 60 feet of you, it gains a +1 bonus to its Armor Class and its saving throws, and it has Resistance to all damage. In addition, you take the same amount of damage it takes each time it is damaged. Any of the following ends the spell: you drop to 0 Hit Points, you and the target end up more than 60 feet apart, or either of you becomes the target of another casting of this spell.", "Web": "Sticky webbing bursts into being at a point within range, filling a 20-foot Cube there for the duration. The webbing is Difficult Terrain, and the area inside it is Lightly Obscured. If the webbing isn't stretched between two solid masses (walls or trees, for example) or spread across a floor, wall, or ceiling, it collapses, and the spell ends when your next turn starts. Webbing spread over a flat surface is 5 feet deep. Whenever a creature starts its turn in the webs, or enters them for the first time on a turn, it must make a Dexterity saving throw. If it fails, it has the Restrained condition for as long as it stays in the webs or until it breaks free. Any creature the webs hold Restrained can spend an action trying to get loose. It is no longer Restrained if its Strength (Athletics) check succeeds against your spell save DC. The webs are flammable: fire burns away any 5-foot Cube of webbing it touches in 1 round, and a creature that starts its turn in the fire takes 2d4 Fire damage.", "Zone of Truth": "A magical zone that guards against deception fills a Sphere with a 15-foot radius, centered on a point within range. For as long as the spell lasts, any creature that starts its turn in the area, or enters it for the first time on a turn, must make a Charisma saving throw. A creature that fails can't tell a deliberate lie while it is inside the radius. You learn whether each creature passes or fails this saving throw. A creature affected by the spell knows about it, so it can avoid answering questions it would normally respond to with a lie. Such a creature may be evasive, but anything it says must be true.", "Animate Dead": "Pick a pile of bones or the corpse of a Medium or Small Humanoid within range. It becomes an Undead creature: a Skeleton if you picked bones, or a Zombie if you picked a corpse (appendix B has their stat blocks). As a Bonus Action on any of your turns, you can mentally command any creature made with this spell, provided it is within 60 feet of you. If you control several such creatures, one command can go to any number of them at once, and each receives the same order. The command sets the action the creature takes and the place it moves to on its next turn, or it can be a general order, such as guarding a chamber or corridor. A creature given no commands takes the Dodge action and moves only to stay out of harm's way. After receiving an order, the creature carries it out until the task is finished. Your control over the creature lasts 24 hours, and then it no longer obeys any command you gave it. To keep it under your control for another 24 hours, you need to cast this spell on it again before the current 24-hour period is over. Used this way, the spell renews your control of as many as four creatures you animated with it, and it animates no new creature. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 3, the number of Undead creatures you animate or reassert control over grows by two. Each creature must be made from a different corpse or pile of bones.", "Aura of Vitality": "For the duration, an aura spreads out from you in a 30-foot Emanation. When you create the aura, you can have one creature in it regain 2d6 Hit Points, and you can do so again at the start of each of your turns while the aura lasts.", "Beacon of Hope": "Pick any number of creatures within range. Until the spell ends, each of them makes Wisdom saving throws and Death Saving Throws with Advantage. Whenever such a creature receives healing, it regains the highest possible number of Hit Points.", "Bestow Curse": "Touch a creature. Unless it succeeds on a Wisdom saving throw, it is cursed for the duration. While the curse lasts, the target suffers one of these effects, chosen by you: ability checks and saving throws with one ability you pick are made with Disadvantage; it has Disadvantage on attack rolls against you; in combat, it makes a Wisdom saving throw when each of its turns starts, and on a failure it must take the Dodge action that turn; or it takes an extra 1d8 Necrotic damage whenever you deal damage to it with an attack roll or a spell. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** With a level 4 spell slot, you can keep Concentration on the spell for up to 10 minutes. With a level 5 or higher spell slot, the spell needs no Concentration, and its duration becomes 8 hours (level 5 or 6 slot) or 24 hours (level 7 or 8 slot). A level 9 spell slot makes the spell last until it is dispelled.", "Blinding Smite": "The creature you hit with the Melee weapon or Unarmed Strike that triggered this spell takes an extra 3d8 Radiant damage from that attack and has the Blinded condition until the spell ends. Each time the Blinded creature finishes one of its turns, it makes a Constitution saving throw. A success ends the spell on it. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 3, the extra damage increases by 1d8.", "Blink": "For the duration, roll 1d6 whenever one of your turns ends. On a 4, 5, or 6, you disappear from the plane of existence you are on and appear in the Ethereal Plane. If you are already on the Ethereal Plane when this happens, the spell ends at once. While you are in the Ethereal Plane, the plane you left remains perceptible to you, shown only in shades of gray, and you can't see anything there beyond 60 feet. Only other creatures on the Ethereal Plane can affect you or be affected by you. Creatures on the plane you left are unable to perceive you, except those with some special ability for perceiving things on the Ethereal Plane. If you are on the Ethereal Plane at the start of your next turn, or when the spell ends, you return to the other plane. You reappear within 10 feet of the space you vanished from, in an unoccupied space you choose and can see. If there is no unoccupied space within that distance, you appear in the nearest unoccupied space.", "Call Lightning": "A storm cloud forms at a point above you that you can see within range. The cloud is a Cylinder 10 feet tall with a 60-foot radius. As part of casting the spell, pick a point you can see beneath the cloud, and a bolt of lightning strikes it from the cloud. Every creature within 5 feet of that point must make a Dexterity saving throw. A creature takes 3d10 Lightning damage if it fails, or half as much if it succeeds. Until the spell ends, a Magic action lets you bring down another bolt in the same way, aimed at the same point or a new one. If you are outdoors during a storm when you cast this spell, you take control of that storm rather than creating a new cloud, and in that case the spell's damage increases by 1d10. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 3, the damage increases by 1d10.", "Clairvoyance": "**Material Component.** A focus worth at least 100 GP, either a jeweled horn for hearing or a glass eye for seeing. You place an Invisible sensor somewhere within range. The spot must be either a location you know (a place you have been to or have seen before) or an obvious location you don't know, such as the far side of a door, around a corner, or among a grove of trees. The sensor is intangible and invulnerable, and it stays where you put it for the duration. As you cast the spell, choose either seeing or hearing. You can use that sense through the sensor just as if you were standing in its space. You can take a Bonus Action to swap between seeing and hearing. Any creature able to see the sensor, such as one benefiting from See Invisibility or Truesight, sees a glowing orb roughly as big as your fist.", "Conjure Animals": "Nature spirits answer your call. They show up as a Large pack of spectral, intangible animals, filling an unoccupied space within range that you can see. The pack remains for the duration, and you decide what animals the spirits look like, such as birds, serpents, or wolves. While you are within 5 feet of the pack, you have Advantage on Strength saving throws. When you move on your turn, you can move the pack as well, up to 30 feet, into an unoccupied space you can see. You can call for a Dexterity saving throw from a creature you can see each time the pack moves to within 10 feet of it, and each time that creature moves into a space no more than 10 feet from the pack or finishes its turn in such a space. If it fails, it takes 3d10 Slashing damage. No creature makes this saving throw more than once per turn. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 3, the damage increases by 1d10.", "Conjure Barrage": "**Material Component.** Any Melee or Ranged weapon with a value of at least 1 CP. You brandish the weapon you used in casting the spell and summon spectral duplicates of it (or ammunition suited to it) that shoot forward and then vanish. Choose any creatures you can see in a 60-foot Cone. Each of them makes a Dexterity saving throw. A creature that fails takes 5d8 Force damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 3, the damage increases by 1d8.", "Counterspell": "You try to disrupt a creature while it is casting a spell. That creature makes a Constitution saving throw. If it fails, the spell fizzles without any effect, and the action, Bonus Action, or Reaction it used to cast the spell is wasted. If the spell was cast using a spell slot, that slot isn't expended.", "Create Food and Water": "Within range, you produce 30 gallons of fresh water and 45 pounds of food, placed on the ground or in containers. Both help ward off the dangers of malnutrition and dehydration. The food tastes bland but is nourishing, and it looks like any food you choose. The water is clean. If no one eats the food within 24 hours, it spoils.", "Crusader's Mantle": "A magical aura spreads from you in a 30-foot Emanation. Whenever you or one of your allies in the aura hits with a weapon or an Unarmed Strike, the hit deals an extra 1d4 Radiant damage.", "Daylight": "Choose a point within range. For the duration, sunlight spreads out from it to fill a 60-foot-radius Sphere. The area of sunlight is Bright Light, and it sheds Dim Light for another 60 feet beyond. You can instead target an object that no one is wearing or carrying, so that the sunlight fills a 60-foot Emanation coming from that object. Putting something opaque over the object, like a bowl or a helm, blocks the sunlight. A spell of level 3 or lower is dispelled if it created an area of Darkness that overlaps any part of this spell's area.", "Dispel Magic": "Pick a creature, an object, or a magical effect within range as the target. Each spell of level 3 or lower currently in effect on the target ends. For every spell of level 4 or higher currently in effect on it, you make an ability check with your spellcasting ability. The DC equals 10 plus the level of that spell, and a success ends that spell. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** You end a spell on the target automatically when that spell's level is equal to or lower than the level of the slot you expended.", "Elemental Weapon": "Touch a nonmagical weapon, which becomes a magic weapon, and pick one damage type: Acid, Cold, Fire, Lightning, or Thunder. Until the spell ends, the weapon grants a +1 bonus to attack rolls, and when it hits, it deals an extra 1d4 damage of the type you picked. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** With a level 5-6 spell slot, the attack roll bonus becomes +2 and the extra damage becomes 2d4. With a level 7+ spell slot, the bonus becomes +3 and the extra damage becomes 3d4.", "Fear": "Each creature in a 30-foot Cone makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure, it drops whatever it is holding and has the Frightened condition for the duration. A creature Frightened by this spell uses each of its turns to take the Dash action and move away from you along the safest route, unless it has nowhere to go. When the creature ends its turn somewhere it lacks line of sight to you, it makes another Wisdom saving throw, ending the spell on itself if it succeeds.", "Feign Death": "Touch a willing creature to place it in a cataleptic state that looks exactly like death. Until the spell ends, both outward examination and spells that check the target's status show it as dead. Its Speed is 0, and it has the Blinded and Incapacitated conditions. It also has Resistance to all damage other than Psychic damage, plus Immunity to the Poisoned condition.", "Fireball": "A bright streak shoots from you to a point within range that you choose, then bursts into a fiery explosion with a low roar. Each creature within the 20-foot-radius Sphere around that point must make a Dexterity saving throw. It takes 8d6 Fire damage on a failure or half as much on a success. Any flammable object in the area that isn't being worn or carried starts burning. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 3, the damage rises by 1d6.", "Fly": "A willing creature you touch gets a Fly Speed of 60 feet for the duration, and it can hover. When the spell ends, the creature falls if it is still in the air, unless something lets it stop falling. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 3, one more creature can be targeted.", "Gaseous Form": "A willing creature you touch transforms into a misty cloud for the duration, together with everything it is wearing and carrying. If the target drops to 0 Hit Points, or uses a Magic action to end the spell on itself, the spell ends for it. In cloud form, it can hover, and its only way to move is a Fly Speed of 10 feet. It is able to enter another creature's space and stay there. It gains Resistance to Bludgeoning, Piercing, and Slashing damage, Immunity to the Prone condition, and Advantage on saving throws using Strength, Dexterity, or Constitution. It can slip through narrow openings, yet liquids are like solid surfaces to it. The target is unable to speak or handle objects, and nothing it was holding or carrying can be dropped, used, or interacted with in any other way. Finally, it can neither attack nor cast spells. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 3, one more creature can be targeted.", "Glyph of Warding": "**Material Component.** Powdered diamond worth at least 200 GP, which the spell consumes. You draw a glyph that releases a magical effect at a later time. To hide the glyph, you place it on a surface (a table or a section of floor, for example) or inside an object that can be closed (a book or a chest, for example). The glyph's area can't exceed 10 feet in diameter. Should the surface or object be moved more than 10 feet away from the spot where you cast the spell, the glyph breaks and the spell ends without triggering. The glyph is almost impossible to see. Noticing it requires a Wisdom (Perception) check that succeeds against your spell save DC. As you draw the glyph, you set its trigger and decide which kind of glyph it is, an explosive rune or a spell glyph, as described below. **Set the Trigger.** When you cast the spell, you choose what sets off the glyph. For a glyph on a surface, typical triggers are touching it or stepping on it, removing an object that covers it, or coming within a certain distance of it. For a glyph inside an object, typical triggers are opening the object or seeing the glyph. This spell ends once the glyph is triggered. You can narrow the trigger so that it activates only for certain creature types (for example, you could set the glyph to affect Aberrations). You can also define conditions under which a creature doesn't trigger it, such as speaking a certain password. **Explosive Rune.** Once triggered, the glyph bursts with magical energy that fills a 20-foot-radius Sphere centered on it. Every creature in that Sphere makes a Dexterity saving throw. A creature that fails takes 5d8 damage of a type you chose when you created the glyph (Acid, Cold, Fire, Lightning, or Thunder), and one that succeeds takes half as much. **Spell Glyph.** As part of creating the glyph, you can cast one prepared spell of level 3 or lower to store it in the glyph. That spell must target a single creature or an area. Cast this way, the stored spell has no immediate effect. The stored spell takes effect when the glyph is triggered. A spell with a target targets the creature that set off the glyph. A spell that affects an area is centered on that same creature. Hostile creatures summoned by the spell, or harmful objects or traps it creates, show up as near to the intruder as they can and attack it. A spell that requires Concentration lasts as long as its full duration allows. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** An explosive rune deals 1d8 more damage for each spell slot level above 3. A spell glyph can hold any spell whose level is no higher than the level of the spell slot you used for Glyph of Warding.", "Haste": "Pick a willing creature within range that you can see. Until the spell ends, the target's Speed doubles, and it gains a +2 bonus to its Armor Class, Advantage on Dexterity saving throws, and an extra action on each of its turns. That extra action can be used only to take one of these actions: Attack (one attack only), Dash, Disengage, Hide, or Utilize. When the spell ends, a wave of lethargy washes over the target, and until its next turn ends, its Speed is 0 and it has the Incapacitated condition.", "Hunger of Hadar": "You open a gateway to the Far Realm, a place teeming with unspeakable horrors. Centered on a point within range, a 20-foot-radius Sphere of Darkness appears and remains for the duration. The Sphere's area counts as Difficult Terrain, and it is full of strange whispers and slurping sounds that can be heard as far as 30 feet away. No light, magical or not, can brighten the area, and any creature completely inside it has the Blinded condition. A creature takes 2d6 Cold damage when it starts its turn in the area. A creature that ends its turn there makes a Dexterity saving throw, and otherworldly tentacles deal 2d6 Acid damage to it on a failure. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 3, either the Cold damage or the Acid damage (your choice) increases by 1d6.", "Hypnotic Pattern": "A twisting pattern of colors flashes into being in a 30-foot Cube within range, shows itself for an instant, and then vanishes. Every creature in the area that can see the pattern makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure, it is Charmed until the spell ends. A creature Charmed this way is Incapacitated and has a Speed of 0. The spell ends for a creature that takes any damage, or when someone else spends an action shaking it out of its stupor.", "Leomund's Tiny Hut": "Around you, a 10-foot Emanation springs into being and stays put for the duration. If the Emanation is too small to fully contain all the creatures in its area, the spell fails as you cast it. Any creature or object that is inside when you cast the spell can pass through the Emanation's boundary freely, while all other creatures and objects are barred from crossing it. No spell of level 3 or lower can be cast through it, and the effects of those spells can't extend inside. Whatever the weather outside, the air inside stays comfortable and dry. While the spell lasts, a command from you (no action required) fills the interior with Dim Light or Darkness. Seen from outside, the Emanation is opaque and of any color you choose, yet it is transparent from within. Leaving the Emanation or casting the spell again ends it early.", "Lightning Arrow": "You cast this spell as a Bonus Action right after you hit or miss a target with a ranged attack made with a weapon. The weapon or ammunition used for that attack turns into a lightning bolt as the attack hits or misses. The target takes none of the attack's normal damage or other effects. Instead, it takes 4d8 Lightning damage if the attack hit, or half that damage if the attack missed. Then every creature within 10 feet of the target attempts a Dexterity saving throw. A creature that fails takes 2d8 Lightning damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. When this is done, the weapon or ammunition reverts to its usual form. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 3 adds 1d8 to the damage of both effects.", "Lightning Bolt": "You choose a direction, and a bolt of lightning blasts out from you as a 100-foot-long, 5-foot-wide Line. Every creature in the Line attempts a Dexterity saving throw. A creature that fails takes 8d6 Lightning damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 3 adds 1d6 to the damage.", "Magic Circle": "**Material Component.** Salt and powdered silver worth at least 100 GP, which the spell consumes. You create a Cylinder of magical energy, 10 feet in radius and 20 feet tall, centered on a point on the ground within range that you can see. Glowing runes appear wherever the Cylinder meets the floor or another surface. Choose one or more creature types from this list: Celestials, Elementals, Fey, Fiends, or Undead. A creature of a chosen type is affected by the circle as follows. It is unable to enter the Cylinder willingly by nonmagical means, and if it uses teleportation or interplanar travel to try to get in, it needs to succeed on a Charisma saving throw first. It has Disadvantage on attack rolls against targets inside the Cylinder. Targets inside the Cylinder can't be possessed by it, and it can't give them the Charmed or Frightened condition. Each time you cast the spell, you can make its magic work in reverse, so it keeps a creature of a chosen type from leaving the Cylinder and protects targets outside it instead. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 3 adds 1 hour to the duration.", "Major Image": "You make an image of an object, a creature, or another visible phenomenon, no bigger than a 20-foot Cube. It appears at a spot within range that you can see and lasts for the duration. The image seems real, complete with the sounds, smells, and temperature that fit the thing it shows, yet it is unable to deal damage or inflict conditions. If you are within the illusion's range, a Magic action lets you move the image to any other spot within range. As it moves, you can change how it looks so its movement seems natural. For example, an image of a creature that you move can be made to look as if it is walking. In the same way, you can have the illusion make different sounds at different times, and it can even hold a conversation, for example. Things pass through the image, so physical interaction with it reveals it to be an illusion. A creature can take a Study action to examine the image. If its Intelligence (Investigation) check against your spell save DC succeeds, it determines that the image is an illusion. A creature that recognizes the illusion for what it is can see through the image, and the image's other sensory qualities become faint to that creature. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Cast with a level 4+ spell slot, the spell doesn't require Concentration and lasts until it is dispelled.", "Mass Healing Word": "Choose up to six creatures within range that you can see. Each of them regains Hit Points equal to your spellcasting ability modifier + 2d4. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 3 adds 1d4 to the healing.", "Meld into Stone": "You step into a stone object or surface big enough to hold your entire body, and for the duration, you and your equipment merge with the stone. To do so, you must touch the stone. Nonmagical senses can't see or otherwise detect any sign of your presence. While you are merged with the stone, you can't see what happens outside it, and you have Disadvantage on Wisdom (Perception) checks to hear sounds outside it. You stay aware of how much time passes, and you can cast spells on yourself while inside the stone. Spending 5 feet of movement lets you leave the stone at the place you entered it, which ends the spell. You can't move in any other way. Minor physical damage to the stone doesn't hurt you. However, if the stone is partly destroyed or its shape changes so that you no longer fit inside it, you are expelled and take 6d6 Force damage. If the stone is destroyed completely or transmuted into a different substance, you are expelled and take 50 Force damage. When you are expelled, you move into the unoccupied space nearest to where you first entered, and you have the Prone condition.", "Nondetection": "**Material Component.** Diamond dust worth at least 25 GP, which the spell consumes. For the duration, a target you touch is hidden from Divination spells. You can choose a willing creature as the target, or a place or object that measures 10 feet or less in every dimension. No Divination spell can target it, and magical scrying sensors can't perceive it.", "Phantom Steed": "A Large, quasi-real creature shaped like a horse materializes on the ground in an unoccupied space you choose within range. You pick how it looks. It comes with a bridle, bit, and saddle. Any of that equipment vanishes in a puff of smoke if someone takes it farther than 10 feet from the steed. While the spell lasts, the steed can be ridden by you or by a creature you choose. It uses the Riding Horse stat block (appendix B), except that its Speed is 100 feet and it can travel 13 miles per hour. As the spell ends, the steed fades gradually, leaving the rider 1 minute to dismount. If the steed takes any damage, the spell ends early.", "Plant Growth": "This spell pours vitality into plants. Which casting time you use decides whether you get the Overgrowth or the Enrichment effect below. **Overgrowth.** Pick a point within range. Every normal plant in a 100-foot-radius Sphere centered there grows thick and overgrown. Moving through that area costs a creature 4 feet of movement for each 1 foot it travels. You can choose to leave any number of areas, of any size, inside the spell's area unaffected. **Enrichment.** Pick a point within range. For 365 days, every plant within a half-mile radius of that point is enriched, and when harvested it gives twice the usual amount of food. The same plants can gain this benefit from only one Plant Growth each year.", "Protection from Energy": "Pick Acid, Cold, Fire, Lightning, or Thunder. Until the spell ends, the willing creature you touch gains Resistance to the damage type you picked.", "Remove Curse": "Your touch ends every curse affecting one creature or object. For a cursed magic item, the curse itself remains, but the spell ends its owner's Attunement to the item, letting the owner remove or discard it.", "Revivify": "**Material Component.** A diamond worth at least 300 GP, which the spell consumes. You touch a creature that died within the last minute, and it returns to life with 1 Hit Point. The spell can't revive a creature that died of old age, and it doesn't restore missing body parts.", "Sending": "You send a short message of no more than 25 words to a creature you have met, or to a creature that someone who has met it described to you. The message sounds in the target's mind. If the target knows you, it recognizes you as the sender, and it can answer you immediately in the same way. The spell lets targets understand what your message means. The message can travel any distance and can even reach other planes of existence. When the target is on another plane, the message has a 5 percent chance of not arriving. You know if the delivery fails. When it receives your message, a creature can block you from reaching it with this spell again for 8 hours. If you try to send another message during that time, the spell fails, and you learn that you have been blocked.", "Sleet Storm": "Choose a point within range. For the duration, a 40-foot-tall, 20-foot-radius Cylinder centered there fills with falling sleet. Its area is Heavily Obscured, and exposed flames inside it go out. The ground in the Cylinder is Difficult Terrain. A creature makes a Dexterity saving throw when it starts its turn in the Cylinder or enters it for the first time on a turn. If it fails, it has the Prone condition and loses Concentration.", "Slow": "You warp time around as many as six creatures you choose within a 40-foot Cube in range. Unless a target succeeds on a Wisdom saving throw, the spell affects it for the duration. An affected target has half its normal Speed, a -2 penalty to Armor Class and Dexterity saving throws, and no ability to take Reactions. Each turn, it chooses between taking an action and taking a Bonus Action, and it can't do both. It makes only one attack when it takes the Attack action. When it casts a spell that has a Somatic component, the spell has a 25 percent chance to fail because the target makes the spell's gestures too slowly. An affected target makes the save again as each of its turns ends, and the spell ends on it if it succeeds.", "Speak with Dead": "You give a corpse of your choice within range a semblance of life, letting it reply to questions you put to it. The corpse must have a mouth. The spell fails if the deceased creature was Undead when it died, and it also fails if the corpse was targeted by this spell within the past 10 days. Until the spell ends, you can ask the corpse up to five questions. Its knowledge is limited to what it knew in life, including the languages it knew. Its replies tend to be brief, cryptic, or repetitive. If you are antagonistic toward it, or if it recognizes you as an enemy, nothing compels it to answer truthfully. The spell returns only the creature's animating spirit to its body, not its soul. For that reason, the corpse can't learn anything new, can't understand events that have happened since its death, and can't speculate about the future.", "Speak with Plants": "Plants in an immobile 30-foot Emanation gain limited sentience and animation, which lets them communicate with you and obey simple commands you give. You can question them about what has happened in the spell's area over the past day, gathering information about passing creatures, the weather, and other circumstances. You can also cause Difficult Terrain that comes from plant growth, for example thickets and undergrowth, to become ordinary terrain for the duration. Or you can make ordinary terrain that has plants in it into Difficult Terrain for the duration. Although the spell doesn't let plants pull up their roots and walk around, they can move their stalks, tendrils, and branches for you. You can communicate with any Plant creature in the area as though the two of you shared a language.", "Spirit Guardians": "For the duration, a 15-foot Emanation around you is filled with protective spirits that flit about. Their spectral form is angelic or fey (your choice) if you are good or neutral, or fiendish if you are evil. When you cast this spell, you can choose creatures that it doesn't affect. Every other creature has its Speed halved in the Emanation. Each time the Emanation enters such a creature's space, and each time such a creature moves into the Emanation or ends its turn in it, that creature makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure, the creature takes 3d8 damage, which is Radiant if you are good or neutral and Necrotic if you are evil. On a success, the damage is halved. No creature makes this save more than once per turn. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each slot level above 3, the damage increases by 1d8.", "Stinking Cloud": "Yellow, nauseating gas billows out from a point you choose within range, filling a Sphere with a 20-foot radius, and the area inside is Heavily Obscured. The gas stays for the duration, though a strong wind (like the one from Gust of Wind) disperses it early. A creature that begins its turn inside the Sphere must make a Constitution saving throw, and if it fails, it is Poisoned until the end of that turn. A creature Poisoned by this spell can take neither an action nor a Bonus Action.", "Summon Fey": "**Material Component.** A gilded flower worth at least 300 GP. A Fey spirit answers your call, appearing in an unoccupied space within range that you can see. It uses the Fey Spirit stat block below. As you cast the spell, pick a mood: Fuming, Mirthful, or Tricksy. The spirit looks like a Fey creature of your choice marked by that mood, and the mood decides certain details of its stat block. The spirit disappears when the spell ends or when its Hit Points fall to 0. The spirit counts as an ally to you and your allies. During combat, it acts on your Initiative count, taking its turn right after yours. It follows your spoken commands, and giving them takes no action on your part. If you give it no commands, it takes the Dodge action and spends its movement staying out of danger. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Wherever the stat block refers to the spell's level, use the level of the slot you expended. **Fey Spirit.** Small Fey, Neutral. Armor Class: 12 + the spell's level. Hit Points: 30, plus 10 for each spell level above 3. Speed: 30 feet, plus a Fly Speed of 30 feet. Ability scores: Strength 13 (+1 modifier), Dexterity 16 (+3 modifier), Constitution 14 (+2 modifier), Intelligence 14 (+2 modifier), Wisdom 11 (+0 modifier), Charisma 16 (+3 modifier), and each saving throw modifier matches its ability modifier. Immunities: Charmed. Senses: Darkvision 60 feet, Passive Perception 10. Languages: Sylvan, and it understands the languages you know. Challenge Rating: none (0 experience points), and its Proficiency Bonus equals yours. **Multiattack.** The spirit makes Fey Blade attacks equal in number to half the spell's level, rounded down. **Fey Blade.** Melee attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 5 feet. A hit deals 2d6 + 3 + the spell's level in Force damage. **Fey Step.** As a Bonus Action, the spirit magically teleports to an unoccupied space it can see within 30 feet of it. Then one of the following effects happens, depending on the mood you picked. **Fuming.** Before this turn ends, the spirit's next attack roll has Advantage. **Mirthful.** One creature the spirit can see within 10 feet of itself makes a Wisdom saving throw against your spell save DC, becoming Charmed by you and the spirit on a failure. The charm lasts for 1 minute or until the target takes any damage. **Tricksy.** Magical Darkness fills a 10-foot Cube within 5 feet of the spirit and lasts until the spirit's next turn ends.", "Summon Undead": "**Material Component.** A gilded skull worth at least 300 GP. An Undead spirit answers your call, appearing in an unoccupied space within range that you can see. It uses the Undead Spirit stat block below. As you cast the spell, pick one of three forms for the creature: Ghostly, Putrid, or Skeletal. The spirit looks like an Undead creature of that form, and the form decides certain details of its stat block. The spirit disappears when the spell ends or when its Hit Points fall to 0. The spirit counts as an ally to you and your allies. During combat, it acts on your Initiative count, taking its turn right after yours. It follows your spoken commands, and giving them takes no action on your part. If you give it no commands, it takes the Dodge action and spends its movement staying out of danger. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Wherever the stat block refers to the spell's level, use the level of the slot you expended. **Undead Spirit.** Medium Undead, Neutral. Armor Class: 11 + the spell's level. Hit Points: 30 for Ghostly or Putrid and 20 for Skeletal, plus 10 for each spell level above 3. Speed: 30 feet, plus a Fly Speed of 40 feet with hover (Ghostly only). Ability scores: Strength 12 (+1 modifier), Dexterity 16 (+3 modifier), Constitution 15 (+2 modifier), Intelligence 4 (-3 modifier), Wisdom 10 (+0 modifier), Charisma 9 (-1 modifier), and each saving throw modifier matches its ability modifier. Immunities: Necrotic and Poison damage, plus the Exhaustion, Frightened, Paralyzed, and Poisoned conditions. Senses: Darkvision 60 feet, Passive Perception 10. Languages: it understands the languages you know. Challenge Rating: none (0 experience points), and its Proficiency Bonus equals yours. **Festering Aura (Putrid Only).** Each creature, except you, that begins its turn within a 5-foot Emanation originating from the spirit makes a Constitution saving throw against your spell save DC. A creature that fails has the Poisoned condition until its next turn starts. **Incorporeal Passage (Ghostly Only).** The spirit is able to pass through other creatures and objects as though they were Difficult Terrain. If the spirit is inside an object when its turn ends, it gets shunted to the closest unoccupied space, taking 1d10 Force damage per 5 feet it travels. **Multiattack.** The spirit attacks a number of times equal to half the spell's level, rounded down. **Deathly Touch (Ghostly Only).** Melee attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 5 feet. A hit deals 1d8 + 3 + the spell's level in Necrotic damage, and the target is Frightened until its next turn ends. **Grave Bolt (Skeletal Only).** Ranged attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, range 150 feet. A hit deals 2d4 + 3 + the spell's level in Necrotic damage. **Rotting Claw (Putrid Only).** Melee attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 5 feet. A hit deals 1d6 + 3 + the spell's level in Slashing damage. A target that already has the Poisoned condition is also Paralyzed until its next turn ends.", "Tongues": "Whoever you touch can understand every spoken or signed language that it hears or sees. Also, when the target speaks or signs to communicate, it is understood by every creature that has at least one language and can hear what it says or see what it signs.", "Vampiric Touch": "Shadows wreathe your hand, letting its touch siphon life force from others to heal you. You make a melee spell attack against a creature within your reach. A hit deals 3d6 Necrotic damage to the target, and you regain Hit Points equal to half the Necrotic damage dealt. For as long as the spell lasts, you can repeat the attack on each of your turns by taking a Magic action, targeting either the same creature or another one. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each spell slot level above 3 adds 1d6 to the damage.", "Water Breathing": "Until the spell ends, as many as ten willing creatures you choose within range can breathe underwater. Their normal way of breathing still works too.", "Water Walk": "For the duration, as many as ten willing creatures you choose within range can move across any liquid surface, such as acid, lava, mud, quicksand, snow, or water, as though it were harmless solid ground, although the heat of molten lava can still damage creatures that cross it. Going from the liquid's surface down into the liquid, or back up again, takes an affected target a Bonus Action, but a target that falls into the liquid passes through the surface into the liquid below.", "Wind Wall": "Strong wind rises as a wall from the ground at a spot you choose within range. You can make it as long as 50 feet, as high as 15 feet, and 1 foot thick, in any shape you like so long as it follows one unbroken path along the ground. It remains for the duration. Creatures in the wall's area when it appears must each make a Strength saving throw. A creature that fails takes 4d8 Bludgeoning damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. The wind holds back fog, smoke, and other gases. Flying creatures or objects that are Small or smaller can't get through the wall. Any loose, lightweight material carried into the wall is blown upward. Ordinary projectiles such as arrows and bolts that are launched at targets on the far side of the wall get deflected upward and miss automatically. The wall has no effect on boulders hurled by Giants or siege engines, or on similar projectiles. Creatures in gaseous form can't get through the wall either.", "Arcane Eye": "An Invisible, invulnerable eye appears at a spot within range and hovers there for the duration. You mentally receive what the eye sees, and its sight covers all directions. The eye also has Darkvision out to 30 feet. You can take a Bonus Action to move the eye as far as 30 feet in any direction. A solid barrier blocks its movement, but it can squeeze through any opening at least 1 inch in diameter.", "Aura of Life": "For the duration, an aura spreads from you in a 30-foot Emanation. While inside it, you and each of your allies have Resistance to Necrotic damage, and none of you can have your Hit Point maximum reduced. Any ally that starts its turn in the aura with 0 Hit Points regains 1 Hit Point.", "Aura of Purity": "Until the spell ends, you give off an aura that fills a 30-foot Emanation. You and your allies have Resistance to Poison damage while in it. While in the aura, each of you also has Advantage on any saving throw to avoid or end an effect that imposes one of these conditions: Blinded, Charmed, Deafened, Frightened, Paralyzed, Poisoned, or Stunned.", "Banishment": "Pick one creature you can see within range. Unless it succeeds on a Charisma saving throw, it is sent away to a harmless demiplane for the duration, where it has the Incapacitated condition. Once the spell ends, the target returns to the space it left, or to the nearest unoccupied space if that one is occupied. A target that is a Fiend, a Fey, an Elemental, a Celestial, or an Aberration doesn't come back if the spell lasts for 1 minute. It is instead sent to a random location on a plane, chosen by the Dungeon Master, that is tied to its creature type. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 4, you can target one more creature.", "Blight": "Pick a creature you can see within range. It must make a Constitution saving throw. On a failure it takes 8d8 Necrotic damage, and on a success it takes half that damage. A Plant creature fails this saving throw automatically. You can instead aim the spell at a shrub, a tree, or another nonmagical plant that isn't a creature. Such a plant doesn't get a saving throw. It just withers and dies on the spot. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 4, the damage increases by 1d8.", "Charm Monster": "Choose one creature you can see within range. It makes a Wisdom saving throw, and it has Advantage on that roll when it is fighting you or your allies. If it fails, it has the Charmed condition for the duration. The condition ends early if you or your allies deal damage to it. While Charmed this way, the creature is Friendly to you. Once the spell ends, the creature knows that you Charmed it. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 4, you can target one more creature.", "Compulsion": "Choose any number of creatures you can see within range. Each one must make a Wisdom saving throw, and on a failure it has the Charmed condition until the spell ends. While the spell lasts, you can take a Bonus Action to point out a direction that is horizontal to you. On its next turn, each Charmed target must spend as much of its movement as it can moving in that direction, following the safest route. Once a target has moved this way, it repeats the saving throw, and a success ends the spell on it.", "Confusion": "Pick a point within range. A 10-foot-radius Sphere is centered on it, and every creature in that Sphere makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure, the creature can't take Bonus Actions or Reactions, and at the start of each of its turns it must roll 1d10 to find out what it does that turn. On a 1, it takes no action and spends all its movement moving in a direction set by a 1d4 roll: 1 is north, 2 is east, 3 is south, and 4 is west. On a 2 to 6, it neither moves nor takes any actions. On a 7 or 8, it stays where it is and uses the Attack action to make a single melee attack against a randomly determined creature within its reach, or it takes no action if no creature is within its reach. On a 9 or 10, it chooses its own behavior. Whenever an affected creature finishes one of its turns, it repeats the saving throw, and a success ends the spell on it. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 4, the Sphere's radius increases by 5 feet.", "Conjure Minor Elementals": "For the duration, spirits from the Elemental Planes flit about you, filling a 15-foot Emanation. Your enemies treat the ground in the Emanation as Difficult Terrain. Until the spell ends, whenever you hit a creature in the Emanation with an attack, the attack deals an extra 2d8 damage. You pick the damage type when you make the attack: Acid, Cold, Fire, or Lightning. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 4, the damage increases by 2d8.", "Conjure Woodland Beings": "For the duration, nature spirits flit about you, filling a 10-foot Emanation. You can force a creature you can see to make a Wisdom saving throw whenever the Emanation enters its space, and whenever that creature enters the Emanation or ends its turn inside it. A creature that fails takes 5d8 Force damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. No creature makes this saving throw more than once per turn. For the duration of the spell, you can also take the Disengage action as a Bonus Action. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 5, the damage increases by 1d8.", "Control Water": "Choose an area that is a Cube measuring up to 100 feet on each side. For the duration, you take control of any water inside it. Choose one of the effects below when you cast the spell. On each of your later turns, a Magic action lets you reuse the same effect or switch to another one. **Flood.** The level of all standing water in the area goes up by as much as 20 feet. If the area you choose is in a large body of water, you instead make a wave 20 feet tall that rolls across the area from one edge to the opposite edge, then crashes down. Huge or smaller vehicles in the path of the wave are carried along with it to the far side, and Huge or smaller vehicles that the wave strikes have a 25 percent chance to capsize. The raised water level holds until you switch to another effect or the spell ends. If the effect made a wave, the wave repeats when your next turn starts, as long as the flood effect lasts. **Part Water.** The water in the area parts to form a trench that stretches across the spell's area, with the separated water standing as a wall on each side. The trench lasts until you switch to another effect or the spell ends. Then the water slowly fills the trench back in over the next round until the usual water level returns. **Redirect Flow.** Flowing water in the area heads in a direction you choose, even when that means going over obstacles, climbing walls, or traveling in other unlikely directions. Within the area, the water moves as you direct, but once it flows past the spell's area, it goes back to flowing according to the terrain. The water keeps moving in your chosen direction until you switch to another effect or the spell ends. **Whirlpool.** A whirlpool forms in the middle of the area. The area must be at least 50 feet square and 25 feet deep. The whirlpool remains until you switch to another effect or the spell ends. It is 5 feet wide at its base, up to 50 feet wide at its top, and 25 feet tall. The whirlpool drags every creature in the water within 25 feet of it 10 feet toward itself. When a creature first enters the whirlpool on a turn, or when it ends its turn inside it, the creature makes a Strength saving throw. A creature that fails takes 2d8 Bludgeoning damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. To swim away from the whirlpool, a creature must first take an action to pull itself away and then make a successful Strength (Athletics) check against your spell save DC.", "Death Ward": "Your touch gives a creature some protection against death. While the spell lasts, the target is protected once from dropping to 0 Hit Points: the first time that would happen, it drops to 1 Hit Point instead, and the spell ends. Also, while the spell is still active, any effect that would kill the target instantly without dealing damage is negated against it instead, and the spell ends.", "Dimension Door": "You teleport to any spot within range, arriving at exactly the place you wanted. That place can be one you can see, one you can picture in your mind, or one you can describe by giving a distance and direction, such as \"200 feet straight down\" or \"300 feet up and to the northwest at a 45-degree angle.\" One willing creature can come with you. That creature needs to be no more than 5 feet away from you as you teleport, and it appears in a space no more than 5 feet from your destination space. The teleportation fails if you, the other creature, or both of you would arrive in a space that a creature occupies or that one or more objects fill completely. In that case, you and every creature teleporting with you take 4d6 Force damage each.", "Divination": "**Material Component.** Incense worth at least 25 GP, which the spell consumes. You make contact with a god or a god's servants and ask a single question about a particular goal, event, or activity that will happen within 7 days. The Dungeon Master gives a truthful reply, which might take the form of a short phrase or a cryptic rhyme. The spell doesn't take into account circumstances that could change the answer, such as other spells being cast. If you cast the spell more than once before you finish a Long Rest, every casting after the first adds a cumulative 25 percent chance that you get no answer.", "Dominate Beast": "Pick one Beast within range that you can see. Unless it succeeds on a Wisdom saving throw, it has the Charmed condition for the duration. If you or your allies are in a fight with it, it rolls this save with Advantage. Each time the target takes damage, it repeats the save, and on a success the spell ends on it. While the Charmed target and you are on the same plane of existence, the two of you share a telepathic link. On your turn, you can send commands through this link (no action required), for example \"Fetch that object,\" \"Attack that creature,\" or \"Move over there.\" On its turn, the target tries as hard as it can to obey. Once it finishes an order and you give it no further direction, it acts and moves as it wishes, concentrating on its own protection. You can order the target to take a Reaction, but doing so requires you to take your own Reaction as well. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Your Concentration can be maintained for longer. A level 5 spell slot allows up to 10 minutes, a level 6 slot allows up to 1 hour, and a level 7+ slot allows up to 8 hours.", "Evard's Black Tentacles": "Writhing black tentacles fill a 20-foot square of ground within range that you can see. For the duration, they make the ground in that area Difficult Terrain. Every creature in the area makes a Strength saving throw. On a failure, it takes 3d6 Bludgeoning damage and has the Restrained condition until the spell ends. Entering the area or ending its turn there also forces a creature to make this save, though no creature makes it more than once per turn. A creature Restrained by the tentacles can take an action to try a Strength (Athletics) check against your spell save DC, and on a success the condition ends for it.", "Fabricate": "You turn raw materials into finished products made of the same material. For instance, you can make a wooden bridge out of a clump of trees, a rope out of a patch of hemp, or clothes out of flax or wool. Choose raw materials within range that you can see. Given enough material, you can fabricate an object of Large size or smaller, which must fit within a 10-foot Cube or within eight connected 5-foot Cubes. When you work with metal, stone, or another mineral substance, though, the object can be no larger than Medium (fitting within a 5-foot Cube). The quality of anything you fabricate depends on the quality of the raw materials. This spell can't create creatures or magic items. It also can't create items that demand great skill to make, such as armor and weapons, unless you are proficient with the kind of Artisan's Tools needed to make them.", "Fire Shield": "For the duration, thin flames wrap around your body, and they shed Bright Light out to 10 feet and Dim Light 10 feet farther. The flames form either a warm shield or a chill shield, whichever you choose. A warm shield gives you Resistance to Cold damage, and a chill shield gives you Resistance to Fire damage. Also, whenever a melee attack roll made by a creature within 5 feet of you hits you, flame bursts from the shield. The attacker takes 2d8 Fire damage if your shield is warm, or 2d8 Cold damage if it is chill.", "Fount of Moonlight": "While the spell lasts, cool light surrounds your body, giving off Bright Light in a 20-foot radius, plus Dim Light for 20 feet beyond that. Until it ends, you have Resistance to Radiant damage, and each of your melee attacks that hits deals an extra 2d6 Radiant damage. Also, if a creature within 60 feet of you deals damage to you and you can see that creature, you can take a Reaction right after the damage to force it to make a Constitution saving throw. If it fails, it has the Blinded condition until your next turn ends.", "Freedom of Movement": "A willing creature you touch gains these benefits until the spell ends. Its movement ignores Difficult Terrain, and spells or other magical effects can neither reduce its Speed nor impose the Paralyzed or Restrained condition on it. It gains a Swim Speed that matches its Speed. It can also use 5 feet of its movement to slip free, automatically, from nonmagical restraints, such as manacles or a creature that has it Grappled. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 4, one more creature can be targeted.", "Giant Insect": "Pick a giant centipede, giant spider, or giant wasp as you cast the spell, and summon it. It shows up in an unoccupied space within range that you can see and uses the Giant Insect stat block, in which the form you picked decides some of the details. The creature vanishes when the spell ends or if it drops to 0 Hit Points. It counts as an ally of yours and of your allies. During combat, it acts on your Initiative count, taking its turn right after you take yours. It follows the commands you speak (you need no action to give them). When you give it no commands, it takes the Dodge action and moves to stay away from danger. **Giant Insect.** Large Beast, Unaligned. Armor Class 11 + the spell's level. Hit Points 30 + 10 for each spell level. Speed 40 feet, Climb 40 feet, and Fly 40 feet (wasp only). Strength 17 (modifier +3), Dexterity 13 (modifier +1), Constitution 15 (modifier +2), Intelligence 4 (modifier -3), Wisdom 14 (modifier +2), Charisma 3 (modifier -4), with each saving throw bonus equal to the modifier. Darkvision 60 feet, Passive Perception 12. It understands the languages you know. Challenge Rating None (0 experience points), and its Proficiency Bonus equals yours. **Spider Climb.** The insect can climb across difficult surfaces, ceilings included, with no ability check needed. **Multiattack.** The insect attacks a number of times equal to half the spell's level, rounded down. **Poison Jab.** Melee Attack Roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 10 feet. On a hit, it deals Piercing damage equal to 1d6 + 3 plus the spell's level, plus 1d4 Poison damage. **Web Bolt.** Spider only. Ranged Attack Roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, range 60 feet. On a hit, it deals Bludgeoning damage equal to 1d10 + 3 plus the spell's level, and until the insect's next turn starts, the target has a Speed of 0. **Venomous Spew.** Centipede only, a Bonus Action. One creature the insect can see within 10 feet of it must make a Constitution saving throw. The DC is your spell save DC. On a failure, the target has the Poisoned condition until the insect's next turn starts. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** In the stat block, use the level of the spell slot you expended as the spell's level.", "Grasping Vine": "A vine springs from a surface in an unoccupied space within range that you can see, and it remains until the spell ends. Make a melee spell attack against one creature that is no more than 30 feet from the vine. If the attack hits, the creature takes 4d8 Bludgeoning damage and is dragged as far as 30 feet toward the vine. A target of Huge size or smaller also has the Grappled condition, and the escape DC equals your spell save DC. The vine can hold only a single Grappled creature at once, and you can have it let go of that creature (no action required). As a Bonus Action on each of your later turns, you can make the attack again against a creature no more than 30 feet from the vine. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 4, the vine can grapple one more creature.", "Greater Invisibility": "The creature you touch gains the Invisible condition, which lasts until the spell ends.", "Guardian of Faith": "For the duration, a Large spectral guardian hovers in an unoccupied space within range that you can see. It fills that space and is invulnerable, and it takes a form fitting your deity or pantheon. The first time on a turn that an enemy moves into a space within 10 feet of the guardian, or whenever an enemy starts its turn there, that enemy makes a Dexterity saving throw. It takes 20 Radiant damage on a failure or half as much on a success. Once the guardian has dealt 60 damage in total, it vanishes.", "Hallucinatory Terrain": "Within range, you make natural terrain in a 150-foot Cube look, sound, and smell like some other kind of natural terrain. For instance, you could make open fields or a road look like a swamp, a hill, a crevasse, or another kind of difficult or impassable ground. You could make a pond look like a grassy meadow, a cliff edge like a gentle slope, or a gully full of rocks like a broad, smooth road. Structures built by people, equipment, and creatures in the area stay as they are. The terrain still feels the way it really does, so creatures that enter the area are likely to notice the illusion. If touch doesn't make the difference obvious, a creature that examines the illusion can use the Study action to attempt an Intelligence (Investigation) check against your spell save DC, disbelieving it on a success. A creature that discerns the terrain is an illusion sees the illusion as a vague image laid over the real terrain.", "Ice Storm": "Pick a point within range. Hail pounds down in a Cylinder, 20 feet in radius and 40 feet high, centered on that point. Every creature inside the Cylinder makes a Dexterity saving throw. A creature that fails takes 2d10 Bludgeoning damage plus 4d6 Cold damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. Until your next turn ends, the hailstones make the ground in the Cylinder Difficult Terrain. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 4 adds 1d10 to the Bludgeoning damage.", "Leomund's Secret Chest": "**Material Component.** A chest measuring 3 by 2 by 2 feet, built from rare materials worth at least 5,000 GP, plus a Tiny replica of that chest, made from the same materials and worth at least 50 GP. You hide a chest and everything in it on the Ethereal Plane. You must touch both the chest and the miniature replica that serve as the spell's Material components. The chest holds nonliving material only, up to 12 cubic feet (3 by 2 by 2 feet). While the chest is on the Ethereal Plane, a Magic action spent touching the replica calls the chest back to you, and it appears within 5 feet of you, in an unoccupied space on the ground. To return the chest to the Ethereal Plane, you take a Magic action and touch both the chest and the replica. At the end of each day after the first 60 days, the spell has a cumulative 5 percent chance of ending. The spell also ends if you cast it again or if the Tiny replica is destroyed. If the spell ends while the larger chest is still on the Ethereal Plane, it stays there for you or someone else to find.", "Locate Creature": "Name or describe a creature that is familiar to you. If it is within 1,000 feet of you, you sense which direction it is in, and if it is moving, you also know which way it is heading. The spell can find a specific creature you know, or the nearest creature of a particular kind (such as a human or a unicorn), as long as you have seen such a creature from close up, no more than 30 feet away, at least once. The spell fails to find the creature you named or described if it has taken a different form, for example because a Flesh to Stone or Polymorph spell is affecting it. Lead of any thickness on the direct path between you and the creature also keeps the spell from finding it.", "Mordenkainen's Faithful Hound": "A phantom watchdog appears in an unoccupied space within range that you can see. It stays for the duration, or until you and the hound are more than 300 feet away from each other. The hound is intangible and invulnerable, and only you can see it. When you cast the spell, you set a password. If a creature of Small size or larger approaches within 30 feet of the hound without first speaking the password, the hound begins to bark loudly. The hound has Truesight out to 30 feet. As each of your turns starts, the hound tries to bite an enemy that is within 5 feet of it. The enemy makes a Dexterity saving throw and takes 4d8 Force damage on a failure. On your later turns, a Magic action lets you move the hound up to 30 feet.", "Mordenkainen's Private Sanctum": "You magically secure an area within range, which is a Cube that can measure anywhere from 5 feet to 100 feet on each side. The protection lasts for the duration. As you cast the spell, choose any of the following properties to decide what kind of security it gives. Sound can't cross the warded area's boundary. The boundary looks dark and foggy, so no vision (including Darkvision) can see through it. Divination spells can't create sensors inside the protected area or send them across its boundary. Divination spells can't target creatures in the area. Teleportation into or out of the area is impossible. Planar travel doesn't work within the area. If you cast this spell on the same spot daily for 365 days, it then lasts until dispelled. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 4 lets you add 100 feet to the size of the Cube.", "Otiluke's Resilient Sphere": "A glimmering sphere forms around one creature or object within range that is Large or smaller. A creature that is unwilling makes a Dexterity saving throw and, on a failure, is enclosed for the duration. Nothing can cross the barrier in either direction, whether physical objects, energy, or other spell effects, although a creature inside can still breathe. The sphere is immune to all damage. Attacks and effects coming from outside can't damage a creature or object within it, and a creature within it can't damage anything outside. The sphere has no weight and is only large enough to hold the creature or object it encloses. A creature trapped inside can use an action to shove against the walls, rolling the sphere a distance of up to half that creature's Speed. Other creatures can likewise pick up the globe and carry it. If a Disintegrate spell targets the globe, the globe is destroyed and nothing inside it is harmed.", "Phantasmal Killer": "Pick a creature you can see within range. Drawing on that creature's nightmares, you conjure an illusion of the things it fears most, visible to it alone. The target makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure, it takes 4d10 Psychic damage and, for the duration, has Disadvantage on attack rolls and ability checks. A successful save halves the damage and ends the spell. While the spell lasts, the target repeats the Wisdom saving throw as each of its turns ends. A failure deals the Psychic damage to it again, and a success ends the spell. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each slot level above 4, the damage increases by 1d10.", "Polymorph": "Choose a creature you can see within range and try to transform it into a Beast. Unless it succeeds on a Wisdom saving throw, it shape-shifts into a Beast form for the duration. You pick the form, which can be any Beast with a Challenge Rating no higher than the target's. If the target has no Challenge Rating, use its level instead. The chosen Beast's stat block replaces the target's game statistics, but the target keeps its personality, alignment, creature type, Hit Points, and Hit Point Dice. Some Beast stat blocks are provided in appendix B. The target also receives Temporary Hit Points in an amount that matches the Beast form's Hit Points. If the target runs out of Temporary Hit Points, the spell ends early on it. Its new form's anatomy restricts which actions the target can take. It also can't cast spells or speak. Any gear the target has melds into its new form, and the creature can't use that equipment or gain any benefit from it.", "Staggering Smite": "You cast this spell as a Bonus Action right after your attack with a Melee weapon or an Unarmed Strike hits a creature. That attack deals an extra 4d6 Psychic damage to the target. The target also makes a Wisdom saving throw, and on a failure it has the Stunned condition until your next turn ends. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** The extra damage rises by 1d6 for each spell slot level above 4.", "Stone Shape": "You touch either a stone object that is Medium or smaller, or a section of stone that measures no more than 5 feet in any dimension, and mold it into whatever shape you like. For example, a large rock could become a weapon, a statue, or a coffer, or you could carve a small passage through a 5-foot-thick wall. Reshaping a stone door or its frame can also seal the door shut. The object you form can have a latch and at most two hinges, but finer mechanical detail isn't possible.", "Stoneskin": "**Material Component.** Diamond dust worth at least 100 GP, which the spell consumes. A willing creature you touch gains Resistance against Bludgeoning, Piercing, and Slashing damage until the spell ends.", "Summon Aberration": "**Material Component.** A pickled tentacle and an eyeball inside a platinum-inlaid vial worth at least 400 GP. An aberrant spirit answers your call, appearing in an unoccupied space within range that you can see. It uses the Aberrant Spirit stat block below. As you cast the spell, pick Beholderkin, Mind Flayer, or Slaad. The spirit takes on the look of that kind of Aberration, and your pick decides certain details of its stat block. The spirit disappears when the spell ends or when its Hit Points fall to 0. The spirit counts as an ally to you and your allies. During combat, it acts on your Initiative count, taking its turn right after yours. It follows your spoken commands, and giving them takes no action on your part. If you give it no commands, it takes the Dodge action and spends its movement staying out of danger. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Wherever the stat block refers to the spell's level, use the level of the slot you expended. **Aberrant Spirit.** Medium Aberration, Neutral. Armor Class: 11 + the spell's level. Hit Points: 40, plus 10 for each spell level above 4. Speed: 30 feet, plus a Fly Speed of 30 feet with hover (Beholderkin only). Ability scores: Strength 16 (+3 modifier), Dexterity 10 (+0 modifier), Constitution 15 (+2 modifier), Intelligence 16 (+3 modifier), Wisdom 10 (+0 modifier), Charisma 6 (-2 modifier), and each saving throw modifier matches its ability modifier. Immunities: Psychic. Senses: Darkvision 60 feet, Passive Perception 10. Languages: Deep Speech, and it understands the languages you know. Challenge Rating: none (0 experience points), and its Proficiency Bonus equals yours. **Regeneration (Slaad Only).** If the spirit has at least 1 Hit Point when its turn starts, it regains 5 Hit Points. **Whispering Aura (Mind Flayer Only).** When each of its turns begins, the spirit gives off psionic energy unless it has the Incapacitated condition. Each creature other than you that is no more than 5 feet from it makes a Wisdom saving throw against your spell save DC and takes 2d6 Psychic damage on a failure. **Multiattack.** The spirit attacks a number of times equal to half the spell's level, rounded down. **Claw (Slaad Only).** Melee attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 5 feet. A hit deals 1d10 + 3 + the spell's level in Slashing damage, and the target is unable to regain Hit Points until the spirit's next turn starts. **Eye Ray (Beholderkin Only).** Ranged attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, range 150 feet. A hit deals 1d8 + 3 + the spell's level in Psychic damage to the target. **Psychic Slam (Mind Flayer Only).** Melee attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 5 feet. A hit deals 1d8 + 3 + the spell's level in Psychic damage.", "Summon Construct": "**Material Component.** A lockbox worth at least 400 GP. The spirit of a Construct answers your call, appearing in an unoccupied space within range that you can see. It uses the Construct Spirit stat block below. As you cast the spell, pick a material: Clay, Metal, or Stone. The spirit looks like an animated statue made of that material (you decide its appearance), and the material decides certain details of its stat block. The spirit disappears when the spell ends or when its Hit Points fall to 0. The spirit counts as an ally to you and your allies. During combat, it acts on your Initiative count, taking its turn right after yours. It follows your spoken commands, and giving them takes no action on your part. If you give it no commands, it takes the Dodge action and spends its movement staying out of danger. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Wherever the stat block refers to the spell's level, use the level of the slot you expended. **Construct Spirit.** Medium Construct, Neutral. Armor Class: 13 + the spell's level. Hit Points: 40, plus 15 for each spell level above 4. Speed: 30 feet. Ability scores: Strength 18 (+4 modifier), Dexterity 10 (+0 modifier), Constitution 18 (+4 modifier), Intelligence 14 (+2 modifier), Wisdom 11 (+0 modifier), Charisma 5 (-3 modifier), and each saving throw modifier matches its ability modifier. It has Resistance to Poison damage and is immune to the Charmed, Exhaustion, Frightened, Paralyzed, and Poisoned conditions. Senses: Darkvision 60 feet, Passive Perception 10. Languages: it understands the languages you know. Challenge Rating: none (0 experience points), and its Proficiency Bonus equals yours. **Heated Body (Metal Only).** Whenever a creature hits the spirit with a melee attack, that creature takes 1d10 Fire damage, and so does a creature that begins its turn in a grapple with the spirit. **Stony Lethargy (Stone Only).** If the spirit can see a creature that begins its turn within 10 feet of it, the spirit can aim magical energy at that creature. The target makes a Wisdom saving throw against your spell save DC. If it fails, the target's Speed is halved and it can't make Opportunity Attacks until its next turn starts. **Multiattack.** The spirit makes Slam attacks equal in number to half the spell's level, rounded down. **Slam.** Melee attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 5 feet. A hit deals 1d8 + 4 + the spell's level in Bludgeoning damage. **Berserk Lashing (Clay Only).** Reaction, triggered when a creature damages the spirit. In response, the spirit makes a Slam attack against that creature if it can. Otherwise, the spirit moves up to half its Speed toward that creature, and this movement doesn't provoke Opportunity Attacks.", "Summon Elemental": "**Material Component.** A gold-inlaid vial worth at least 400 GP that holds air, a pebble, ash, and water. An Elemental spirit answers your call, appearing in an unoccupied space within range that you can see. It uses the Elemental Spirit stat block below. As you cast the spell, pick an element: Air, Earth, Fire, or Water. The spirit looks like a bipedal figure wrapped in that element, and the element decides certain details of its stat block. The spirit disappears when the spell ends or when its Hit Points fall to 0. The spirit counts as an ally to you and your allies. During combat, it acts on your Initiative count, taking its turn right after yours. It follows your spoken commands, and giving them takes no action on your part. If you give it no commands, it takes the Dodge action and spends its movement staying out of danger. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Wherever the stat block refers to the spell's level, use the level of the slot you expended. **Elemental Spirit.** Medium Elemental, Neutral. Armor Class: 11 + the spell's level. Hit Points: 50, plus 10 for each spell level above 4. Speed: 40 feet, plus a Burrow Speed of 40 feet (Earth only), a Fly Speed of 40 feet with hover (Air only), and a Swim Speed of 40 feet (Water only). Ability scores: Strength 18 (+4 modifier), Dexterity 15 (+2 modifier), Constitution 17 (+3 modifier), Intelligence 4 (-3 modifier), Wisdom 10 (+0 modifier), Charisma 16 (+3 modifier), and each saving throw modifier matches its ability modifier. Resistances by element: Air has Lightning and Thunder, Earth has Piercing and Slashing, and Water has Acid. Immunities: Fire damage (Fire only) and Poison damage, plus the Exhaustion, Paralyzed, Petrified, and Poisoned conditions. Senses: Darkvision 60 feet, Passive Perception 10. Languages: Primordial, and it understands the languages you know. Challenge Rating: none (0 experience points), and its Proficiency Bonus equals yours. **Amorphous Form (Air, Fire, and Water Only).** The spirit can pass through a space as narrow as 1 inch wide, and doing so doesn't count as Difficult Terrain for it. **Multiattack.** The spirit makes Slam attacks equal in number to half the spell's level, rounded down. **Slam.** Melee attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 5 feet. A hit deals 1d10 + 4 + the spell's level damage, and the element sets the damage type: Lightning for Air, Bludgeoning for Earth, Fire for Fire, and Cold for Water.", "Vitriolic Sphere": "You point at a spot within range, and a glowing ball of acid 1 foot across streaks to it and bursts in a 20-foot-radius Sphere. Every creature within the Sphere makes a Dexterity saving throw. On a failure, a creature takes 10d4 Acid damage at once and another 5d4 Acid damage when its next turn ends. On a success, it takes only half of the initial damage. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each spell slot level above 4 adds 2d4 to the initial damage.", "Wall of Fire": "A wall of fire rises on a solid surface of your choice within range and lasts for the duration. You can make it either a wall up to 60 feet in length and 20 feet in height, or a ring up to 20 feet across and 20 feet in height. In both forms it is 1 foot thick. Either way, the wall is opaque. Creatures in the wall's area when it appears must each make a Dexterity saving throw. A creature that fails takes 5d8 Fire damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. As you cast the spell, choose one side of the wall. That side deals 5d8 Fire damage to every creature that ends its turn inside the wall or within 10 feet of that side. A creature also takes this damage the first time on a turn that it enters the wall, or when it ends its turn there. No damage comes from the wall's opposite side. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each spell slot level above 4 adds 1d8 to the damage.", "Animate Objects": "Objects come to life at your command. Choose nonmagical objects within range. None of them can be Gargantuan, fixed to a surface, or worn or carried by anyone. Your spellcasting ability modifier sets the most objects you can choose. In that count, a Medium or smaller target counts as one object, a Large target as two, and a Huge target as three. Each target springs to life, sprouts legs, and turns into a Construct whose statistics are those of the Animated Object stat block. Your control over it lasts until it drops to 0 Hit Points or the spell ends. Every creature this spell makes counts as an ally of yours and of your allies. During combat, it acts on your Initiative count, taking its turn right after yours. Until the spell ends, you can take a Bonus Action to give a mental command to any creature you made with this spell that is within 500 feet of you. If you control several creatures, you can command any of them at once, giving each one the same command. If you give it no commands, it takes the Dodge action and moves only to stay out of harm's way. When the creature drops to 0 Hit Points, it turns back into its object form, and any damage left over carries over to that form. **Animated Object.** Huge or smaller Construct, Unaligned. Armor Class 15. Hit Points 10 (Medium or smaller), 20 (Large), or 40 (Huge). Speed 30 feet. Strength 16 (+3). Dexterity and Constitution are 10 (+0) each. Intelligence and Wisdom are 3 (-4) each. Charisma is 1 (-5). Its saving throw modifiers are the same as these ability modifiers. Immunities: Poison and Psychic damage, and the Charmed, Exhaustion, Frightened, Paralyzed, and Poisoned conditions. Senses: Blindsight 30 feet, Passive Perception 6. Languages: it understands the languages you know. Challenge Rating: none, worth 0 experience points. Its Proficiency Bonus equals yours. Slam action: a Melee Attack Roll whose bonus equals your spell attack modifier, with a reach of 5 feet. On a hit, it deals Force damage. A Medium or smaller object deals 1d4 + 3. A Large object deals 2d6 + 3 + your spellcasting ability modifier, and a Huge object deals 2d12 + 3 + that same modifier. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** The Slam damage of the creature goes up by 1d4 (Medium or smaller), 1d6 (Large), or 1d12 (Huge) for each spell slot level above 5.", "Antilife Shell": "For the duration, an aura spreads from you in a 10-foot Emanation. Constructs and Undead can pass or reach through it, but every other creature is blocked from doing so. A creature it affects can still cast spells or attack with Ranged or Reach weapons across the barrier. If you move in a way that forces an affected creature to pass through the barrier, the spell ends.", "Awaken": "**Material Component.** An agate worth at least 1,000 GP, which the spell consumes. Throughout the casting time, you trace magical pathways inside a precious gemstone, and then you touch the target. The target must be a Beast or Plant creature whose Intelligence is 3 or lower, or a natural plant that isn't a creature. Its Intelligence becomes 10, and it can now speak one language you know. A natural plant target turns into a Plant creature. It can now move its limbs, roots, vines, creepers, and the like, and its senses become similar to a human's. The Dungeon Master picks fitting statistics for the awakened Plant, for example the Awakened Shrub or Awakened Tree statistics from the Monster Manual. The awakened target is Charmed for 30 days, though the condition ends sooner if you or your allies deal it damage. Once that condition ends, the awakened creature decides its own attitude toward you.", "Banishing Smite": "Your attack deals an extra 5d10 Force damage to the creature you hit. If that attack drops the target to 50 Hit Points or below, it makes a Charisma saving throw. On a failure, it is whisked away to a harmless demiplane for the duration and has the Incapacitated condition while there. Once the spell ends, the target comes back to the space it left. If something now occupies that space, it appears in the closest unoccupied space instead.", "Bigby's Hand": "A Large hand of shimmering magical energy appears in an unoccupied space within range that you can see. It lasts for the duration, and it moves at your command, imitating the motions of your own hand. The hand is an object. Its Armor Class is 20, its Hit Points equal your Hit Point maximum, and the spell ends if it drops to 0 Hit Points. The hand doesn't occupy its space. As part of casting the spell, and by taking a Bonus Action on each of your later turns, you can send the hand moving up to 60 feet and then have it produce one of the following effects. **Clenched Fist.** The hand punches a target that is within 5 feet of it. You make a melee spell attack, and the target takes 5d8 Force damage on a hit. **Forceful Hand.** The hand tries to shove a creature of Huge size or smaller that is within 5 feet of it. The target makes a Strength saving throw. On a failure, the hand pushes it a distance of up to 5 feet plus five times your spellcasting ability modifier in feet, and the hand follows along, staying within 5 feet of the target. **Grasping Hand.** The hand tries to seize a creature of Huge size or smaller that is within 5 feet of it. The target makes a Dexterity saving throw. On a failure, the target has the Grappled condition, and your spell save DC is the DC to escape. While the hand holds the target this way, a Bonus Action lets you make the hand squeeze it, dealing Bludgeoning damage of 4d6 plus your spellcasting ability modifier. **Interposing Hand.** You have Half Cover against attacks and other effects that start in the hand's space or pass through it. In addition, your enemies treat the hand's space as Difficult Terrain. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 5, the Clenched Fist deals 2d8 more damage and the Grasping Hand deals 2d6 more damage.", "Circle of Power": "For the duration, you radiate an aura in a 30-foot Emanation. While inside the aura, you and your allies have Advantage whenever making a saving throw against a spell or another magical effect. When a spell or magical effect lets a creature take only half damage on a successful saving throw, a creature benefiting from the aura that succeeds on that saving throw takes no damage instead.", "Cloudkill": "A 20-foot-radius Sphere of yellow-green fog forms, centered on a point within range. The fog remains for the duration, unless a strong wind (like the one from Gust of Wind) scatters it first, which ends the spell. The Sphere's area is Heavily Obscured. Every creature in the Sphere makes a Constitution saving throw. A creature that fails takes 5d8 Poison damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. A creature also has to make this saving throw when the Sphere moves into its space, when it enters the Sphere, and when it ends its turn there, but it makes the saving throw no more than once per turn. At the start of each of your turns, the Sphere moves 10 feet away from you. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 5, the damage increases by 1d8.", "Commune": "You reach out to a deity or a divine proxy and may ask as many as three questions that can be answered yes or no. All questions must be asked before the spell ends, and each one gets a correct answer. Because divine beings don't necessarily know everything, a question about information outside the deity's knowledge might get \"unclear\" as its answer. If a one-word reply could mislead or would go against the deity's interests, the Dungeon Master might give a short phrase as the answer instead. Every casting after the first that you make before finishing a Long Rest adds a cumulative 25 percent chance that you receive no answer.", "Commune with Nature": "You commune with nature spirits, who tell you about the land around you. Outdoors, the spell covers everything within 3 miles of you. Underground, in caves and other natural settings, the radius drops to 300 feet. The spell fails to work in places where construction has replaced nature, such as castles and settlements. Pick three of the facts below, and you learn them as they apply to the spell's area: where any settlements are, where any portals to other planes of existence are, the location of one Celestial, Elemental, Fey, Fiend, or Undead of Challenge Rating 10 or higher (the Dungeon Master chooses which), the most common kind of plant, mineral, or Beast (you choose which of the three to learn), and where any bodies of water are. For instance, you could find out where a powerful monster is in the area, where the bodies of water are, and where any towns are.", "Cone of Cold": "A blast of freezing air bursts from you. Every creature in a 60-foot Cone extending from you makes a Constitution saving throw. A creature takes 8d8 Cold damage if it fails, or half as much if it succeeds. Any creature this spell kills turns into a frozen statue until it thaws. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 5, the damage increases by 1d8.", "Conjure Elemental": "A Large, intangible spirit from the Elemental Planes appears in an unoccupied space within range and stays for the duration. Choose its element, which sets its damage type: Lightning for air, Thunder for earth, Fire for fire, or Cold for water. While the spirit has no creature Restrained, you can force a creature you can see to make a Dexterity saving throw whenever that creature moves into the spirit's space or begins its turn no more than 5 feet away from it. On a failure, the creature takes 8d8 damage of the spirit's type and has the Restrained condition until the spell ends. Each time one of its turns starts, the Restrained creature repeats the saving throw. If it fails, it takes 4d8 damage of the spirit's type. If it succeeds, the spirit no longer Restrains it. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 5, the damage increases by 2d8.", "Conjure Volley": "**Material Component.** Any Melee or Ranged weapon with a value of at least 1 CP. Raising the weapon you used to cast the spell, you choose a point within range. Hundreds of spectral copies of that weapon (or of ammunition suited to it) rain down in a single volley and then vanish. Any creatures you choose that you can see within a Cylinder centered on that point, 40 feet in radius and 20 feet high, make a Dexterity saving throw. A creature that fails takes 8d8 Force damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much.", "Contact Other Plane": "You reach out with your mind to a demigod, the spirit of a sage long dead, or another knowledgeable entity from a different plane. Touching such an otherworldly intelligence can shatter your mind. When casting the spell, you must make a DC 15 Intelligence saving throw. If you succeed, you can ask the entity as many as five questions, all of which must be asked before the spell ends. The Dungeon Master answers each one with a single word, such as \"no,\" \"yes,\" \"never,\" \"maybe,\" \"irrelevant,\" or \"unclear\" (when the entity doesn't know the answer). If a one-word answer would mislead you, the Dungeon Master might give a short phrase instead. If you fail, you take 6d6 Psychic damage and are Incapacitated until you finish a Long Rest. Casting Greater Restoration on you ends this effect.", "Contagion": "Your touch spreads a magical disease. The target makes a Constitution saving throw. If it fails, it takes 11d8 Necrotic damage and has the Poisoned condition. When you cast the spell, you also choose one ability. While the target is Poisoned, it has Disadvantage on saving throws made with that ability. The target must repeat the saving throw each time it ends its turn, until it has either three successes or three failures. Three successes end the spell on the target. Three failures make the spell last for 7 days on it. Any time the Poisoned target receives an effect that would remove the Poisoned condition, the condition stays unless the target succeeds on a Constitution saving throw.", "Creation": "You draw wisps of shadow stuff from the Shadowfell and shape them into an object within range. The object is made either of vegetable matter (such as soft goods, rope, or wood) or of mineral matter (such as stone, crystal, or metal). It can be no bigger than a 5-foot Cube, and its form and material must be ones you have seen. How long the spell lasts depends on the object's material, as given in the Materials list below. If the object contains more than one material, the shortest duration applies. If anything created by this spell is used as the Material component of another spell, that other spell fails. **Materials.** Vegetable matter lasts 24 hours. Stone or crystal lasts 12 hours. Precious metals last 1 hour. Gems last 10 minutes. Adamantine or mithral lasts 1 minute. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 5, the Cube grows by 5 feet.", "Destructive Wave": "A wave of destructive energy rolls out from you, filling a 30-foot Emanation. Each creature of your choice in the Emanation makes a Constitution saving throw. A creature that fails takes 5d6 Thunder damage plus 5d6 Radiant or Necrotic damage (you choose which) and has the Prone condition. A creature that succeeds takes half as much damage and suffers no other effect.", "Dispel Evil and Good": "Until the spell ends, any attack roll made against you by an Undead, a Fiend, a Fey, an Elemental, or a Celestial has Disadvantage. You can end the spell early to use either of the special functions below. **Break Enchantment.** You take a Magic action to touch a creature that one or more creatures of those types have possessed or have given the Charmed or Frightened condition. The target is freed from being possessed, Charmed, or Frightened by those creatures. **Dismissal.** You take a Magic action to target a creature of one of those types that you can see within 5 feet of you. Unless it succeeds on a Charisma saving throw, it is returned to its home plane, if it isn't already there. If an Undead or Fey target isn't on its home plane, an Undead is sent to the Shadowfell, and a Fey is sent to the Feywild.", "Dominate Person": "Pick one Humanoid within range that you can see. Unless it succeeds on a Wisdom saving throw, it has the Charmed condition for the duration. If you or your allies are in a fight with it, it rolls this save with Advantage. Each time the target takes damage, it repeats the save, and on a success the spell ends on it. While the Charmed target and you are on the same plane of existence, the two of you share a telepathic link. On your turn, you can send commands through this link (no action required), for example \"Fetch that object,\" \"Attack that creature,\" or \"Move over there.\" On its turn, the target tries as hard as it can to obey. Once it finishes an order and you give it no further direction, it acts and moves as it wishes, concentrating on its own protection. You can order the target to take a Reaction, but doing so requires you to take your own Reaction as well. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Your Concentration can be maintained for longer. A level 6 spell slot allows up to 10 minutes, a level 7 slot allows up to 1 hour, and a level 8+ slot allows up to 8 hours.", "Dream": "Your target must be a creature you know, and it must share your plane of existence. The dream messenger is either you or a willing creature you touch, and it slips into a trance to play that role. During the trance, the messenger has the Incapacitated condition and a Speed of 0. While the target sleeps, the messenger shows up in its dreams and can talk with it for as long as it stays asleep, up to the spell's duration. The messenger can also shape the setting of the dream, making landscapes, objects, and other images. The messenger can leave the trance whenever it likes, which ends the spell. When the target wakes up, it remembers the dream perfectly. Should the target be awake at the time of casting, the messenger knows this and can either end the trance (which also ends the spell) or wait for the target to fall asleep and then enter its dreams. You can also give the messenger a terrifying appearance to the target. In that case, the messenger can speak a message that is ten words long at most, and the target then makes a Wisdom saving throw. If it fails, the target gets no benefit from that rest, and it takes 3d6 Psychic damage upon waking up.", "Flame Strike": "A column of brilliant fire plunges straight down from above. Choose a point within range. Each creature inside a Cylinder 10 feet in radius and 40 feet high, centered there, must make a Dexterity saving throw. On a failure, it takes 5d6 Fire damage and 5d6 Radiant damage, and on a success, it takes half as much of each. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 5, both the Fire damage and the Radiant damage rise by 1d6.", "Geas": "You give a spoken command to one creature within range that you can see, ordering it to perform some service or to avoid an action or course of activity, as you decide. Unless the target succeeds on a Wisdom saving throw, it has the Charmed condition for the duration. It succeeds automatically if it can't understand your command. While Charmed, the creature takes 5d10 Psychic damage whenever it acts in a way directly contrary to your command, but no more than once each day. You can give any command you like, except an activity that would lead to certain death. If you give a suicidal command, the spell ends. This spell can be ended by Remove Curse, Greater Restoration, or Wish. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** A level 7 or 8 spell slot makes the duration 365 days. With a level 9 spell slot, the spell lasts until one of the spells named above ends it.", "Greater Restoration": "**Material Component.** Diamond dust worth at least 100 GP, which the spell consumes. Touch a creature to magically rid it of one of the following effects: 1 Exhaustion level, the Charmed or Petrified condition, any reduction to its Hit Point maximum, any reduction to one of its ability scores, or a curse (which includes the target's Attunement to a cursed magic item).", "Hallow": "**Material Component.** Incense worth at least 1,000 GP, which the spell consumes. You touch a point and imbue the area around it with holy or unholy power. The radius of that area can be as large as 60 feet. If the radius would include any area already affected by Hallow, the spell fails. The following effects apply in the area. **Hallowed Ward.** Pick any number of these creature types: Aberration, Celestial, Elemental, Fey, Fiend, Undead. A creature of a type you picked can't enter the area willingly. While in the area, a creature that such a creature has possessed, Charmed, or Frightened is no longer possessed, Charmed, or Frightened by it. **Extra Effect.** Bind one more effect to the area, chosen from the list below. **Courage.** While in the area, creatures of whichever types you choose can't gain the Frightened condition. **Darkness.** The area is filled with Darkness. Normal light can't illuminate it, and neither can magical light created by spells of a lower level than this one. **Daylight.** The area is filled with bright light, which magical Darkness created by spells of a lower level than this one can't extinguish. **Peaceful Rest.** Corpses buried in the area can't be made into Undead. **Extradimensional Interference.** Creatures of whichever types you choose can't use teleportation or interplanar travel to get into or out of the area. **Fear.** While in the area, creatures of whichever types you choose have the Frightened condition. **Resistance.** While in the area, creatures of whichever types you choose have Resistance to one damage type that you pick. **Silence.** No sound can leave the area, and no sound can enter it. **Tongues.** Creatures of whichever types you choose can communicate with every other creature in the area, even without sharing a language. **Vulnerability.** While in the area, creatures of whichever types you choose have Vulnerability to one damage type that you pick.", "Hold Monster": "Choose one creature in range that you can see. Unless it succeeds on a Wisdom saving throw, it has the Paralyzed condition while the spell lasts. When each of its turns ends, it tries the save again, and a success ends the spell on it. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 5 lets you affect one additional creature.", "Insect Plague": "Pick a point within range. Swarming locusts fill a 20-foot-radius Sphere centered there. The Sphere remains while the spell lasts, and its area is both Lightly Obscured and Difficult Terrain. When the swarm appears, every creature in it attempts a Constitution saving throw. A creature that fails takes 4d10 Piercing damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. A creature must also make the save the first time on a turn that it moves into the area, and when it ends its turn there. No creature makes this save more than once in a turn. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 5 adds 1d10 to the damage.", "Jallarzi's Storm of Radiance": "A storm of flashing light and roaring thunder bursts out in a Cylinder, 10 feet in radius and 40 feet high, centered on a point within range that you can see. Any creature inside this area is Blinded and Deafened, and it can't cast a spell that includes a Verbal component. When the storm appears, every creature in it attempts a Constitution saving throw. A creature that fails takes 2d10 Radiant damage plus 2d10 Thunder damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. A creature must also make the save the first time on a turn that it moves into the area, and when it ends its turn there. No creature makes this save more than once in a turn. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 5 adds 1d10 to both the Radiant damage and the Thunder damage.", "Legend Lore": "**Material Component.** Four ivory strips worth at least 50 GP each, plus incense worth at least 250 GP that the spell consumes. You name or describe a person, place, or object that is famous. Your mind then receives a short summary of the important lore about that famous thing, as the Dungeon Master describes it. The lore might contain key details, amusing revelations, or even secret knowledge that was never widely known. Your existing knowledge matters: the more you already know about it, the sharper and fuller the details you receive. The information is accurate, though the Dungeon Master might phrase it in figurative language or poetry. If the thing you chose turns out not to be famous, the spell fails, and you hear a trombone playing sad musical notes.", "Mass Cure Wounds": "Healing energy rolls outward in a wave from a point within range that you can see. Pick up to six creatures inside a 30-foot-radius Sphere centered there. Every target heals, regaining Hit Points equal to your spellcasting ability modifier + 5d8. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each slot level above 5 adds 1d8 to the healing.", "Mislead": "You have the Invisible condition, and at the same moment an illusory copy of you appears where you stand. The double lasts for the duration. Your invisibility, however, ends as soon as you make an attack roll, cast a spell, or deal damage. As a Magic action, you can move the double up to twice your Speed and have it gesture, speak, and act however you like. It is intangible and invulnerable. You perceive what it sees and hears, using its eyes and ears, as if you stood in its place.", "Modify Memory": "You try to rework the memories of another creature. Choose one creature within range that you can see. It makes a Wisdom saving throw, with Advantage if you are fighting it. If it fails, it is Charmed for the duration. While Charmed this way, it is also Incapacitated and has no awareness of its surroundings, though it can still hear you. Taking any damage or being targeted by another spell ends this spell, and then no memories change. As long as the charm lasts, you can affect the target's memory of one event it lived through in the last 24 hours that lasted 10 minutes or less. You can wipe out every memory of that event for good, let the target remember the event in perfect detail, change what it remembers about the details of the event, or give it a memory of some other event. You have to talk to the target to describe how its memories change, and it has to understand your language for the new memories to take root. Its mind fills any gaps in the details you describe. If the spell ends before you finish your description, the creature's memory stays as it was. Otherwise, the changed memories settle in when the spell ends. A changed memory won't necessarily alter how the creature acts, especially when the memory goes against its natural inclinations, alignment, or beliefs. The creature dismisses an illogical memory as a bad dream, such as a false memory of how much it loved swimming in acid. The Dungeon Master might rule that a modified memory is too nonsensical to affect a creature. Casting Remove Curse or Greater Restoration on the target brings back its true memory. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** The event you alter can be older: up to 7 days ago with a level 6 spell slot, up to 30 days ago with a level 7 slot, up to 365 days ago with a level 8 slot, or any time in the creature's past with a level 9 slot.", "Passwall": "Choose a point you can see within range on a surface made of wood, plaster, or stone, such as a wall, floor, or ceiling. A passage opens there and lasts for the duration. The opening can measure as much as 20 feet deep, 8 feet tall, and 5 feet wide, as you choose. It doesn't make any structure around it unstable. When the opening vanishes, any creatures or objects still inside the passage the spell made are ejected safely to the unoccupied space closest to the surface you cast the spell on.", "Planar Binding": "**Material Component.** A jewel worth at least 1,000 GP, which the spell consumes. You attempt to bind a creature into your service: a Fiend, a Fey, an Elemental, or a Celestial. The creature must stay within range for the whole casting. (Usually, the creature is first summoned into the center of a reversed Magic Circle spell so it stays trapped while you cast this spell.) When you finish casting, the target makes a Charisma saving throw. If it fails, it is bound to serve you for the duration. A creature that another spell summoned or created has that spell's duration extended to match this one. A bound creature must obey your commands as well as it can. For example, you might have it guard a location, deliver a message, or join you on an adventure. A Hostile creature tries to twist your commands to serve its own goals. If it fully carries out your commands before the spell ends and you are both on the same plane of existence, it comes to you to report that it is done. Otherwise, if you are on another plane, it returns to the spot where you bound it and waits there until the spell expires. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** A higher slot lengthens the duration: 10 days with a level 6 slot, 30 days with level 7, 180 days with level 8, and 366 days with level 9.", "Raise Dead": "**Material Component.** A diamond worth at least 500 GP, which the spell consumes. With a touch, you bring a dead creature back to life, provided it has been dead for 10 days or less and wasn't Undead when it died. The creature comes back with 1 Hit Point. The spell also neutralizes any poisons that were affecting the creature when it died. All mortal wounds close, but missing body parts aren't restored. If the creature lacks a body part or organ it needs to survive (its head, for example), the spell automatically fails. Returning from death is an ordeal. The target has a -4 penalty to D20 Tests. Each time the target finishes a Long Rest, the penalty shrinks by 1 until it reaches 0.", "Rary's Telepathic Bond": "You create a telepathic link among as many as eight willing creatures you choose within range, connecting each of them psychically to all the others for the duration. The spell doesn't affect creatures that can't communicate in any language. Until the spell ends, the targets can talk telepathically through the bond whether or not they have a language in common. This communication works across any distance, but it can't reach other planes of existence.", "Reincarnate": "**Material Component.** Rare oils worth at least 1,000 GP, which the spell consumes. Touch a dead Humanoid, or a piece of one. As long as it died no more than 10 days ago, the spell creates a new body for it and calls its soul into that body. Roll 1d10 to determine the new body's species: Aasimar on a 1, Dragonborn on a 2, Dwarf on a 3, Elf on a 4, Gnome on a 5, Goliath on a 6, Halfling on a 7, Human on an 8, Orc on a 9, and Tiefling on a 10. Alternatively, the Dungeon Master chooses another playable species. The reincarnated creature makes any choices its species' description offers, and it remembers its former life. It keeps the capabilities it had in its original form, except that it trades the traits of its previous species for those of its new one.", "Scrying": "**Material Component.** A focus worth at least 1,000 GP, such as a crystal ball, a mirror, or a font filled with water. Choose a creature that shares your plane of existence. You can watch and listen to it if it fails a Wisdom saving throw. That save is adjusted by how well you know the target and by the kind of physical connection you have to it, as shown below. The target doesn't know what it is saving against. It only feels uneasy. Save modifier for how well you know the target: +5 if your knowledge is Secondhand (you have heard of the target), +0 if it is Firsthand (you have met the target), and -5 if it is Extensive (you know the target well). Save modifier for the physical connection you have: -2 for a picture or other likeness, -4 for a garment or other possession, and -10 for a body part, a lock of hair, or a bit of nail. If the save succeeds, the target is unaffected, and you can't target it with this spell again for the next 24 hours. If the save fails, the spell makes an Invisible, intangible sensor appear no more than 10 feet from the target. Through the sensor, you see and hear as though you were present. The sensor moves with the target, staying within 10 feet of it for the duration. Anything that can see the sensor perceives it as a glowing orb roughly the size of your fist. Rather than targeting a creature, you can choose a location you have seen as the target. If you do, the sensor appears at that location and stays put.", "Seeming": "Choose any number of creatures you can see within range, and each one takes on an illusory appearance. Any target that is unwilling can make a Charisma saving throw, and the spell doesn't affect it on a success. The targets can all share one appearance, or you can give them different ones. The spell can change how the targets' bodies and equipment look. You can make each creature look 1 foot shorter or taller and seem heavier or lighter. A target's new look must keep the target's basic limb arrangement, but beyond that, how far the illusion goes is your call. The spell lasts for the duration. Under physical inspection, the changes this spell creates fall apart. For instance, if the spell gives a creature's outfit a hat, objects pass through that hat. A creature can take the Study action to scrutinize a target. It then makes an Intelligence (Investigation) check against your spell save DC and, if it succeeds, becomes aware that the target is disguised.", "Steel Wind Strike": "**Material Component.** A Melee weapon worth at least 1 SP. With a flourish of the weapon used in the casting, you vanish and strike like the wind. Pick up to five creatures within range that you can see, and make a melee spell attack against each of them. Each target you hit takes 6d10 Force damage. Afterward, you teleport to an unoccupied space that you can see and that lies within 5 feet of one of the targets.", "Summon Celestial": "**Material Component.** A reliquary worth at least 500 GP. A Celestial spirit answers your call, appearing in angelic form in an unoccupied space within range that you can see. It uses the Celestial Spirit stat block below. As you cast the spell, pick Avenger or Defender, and your pick decides certain details of its stat block. The spirit disappears when the spell ends or when its Hit Points fall to 0. The spirit counts as an ally to you and your allies. During combat, it acts on your Initiative count, taking its turn right after yours. It follows your spoken commands, and giving them takes no action on your part. If you give it no commands, it takes the Dodge action and spends its movement staying out of danger. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Wherever the stat block refers to the spell's level, use the level of the slot you expended. **Celestial Spirit.** Large Celestial, Neutral. Armor Class: 11 + the spell's level, + 2 more (Defender only). Hit Points: 40, plus 10 for each spell level above 5. Speed: 30 feet, plus a Fly Speed of 40 feet. Ability scores: Strength 16 (+3 modifier), Dexterity 14 (+2 modifier), Constitution 16 (+3 modifier), Intelligence 10 (+0 modifier), Wisdom 14 (+2 modifier), Charisma 16 (+3 modifier), and each saving throw modifier matches its ability modifier. Resistances: Radiant. Immunities: Charmed, Frightened. Senses: Darkvision 60 feet, Passive Perception 12. Languages: Celestial, and it understands the languages you know. Challenge Rating: none (0 experience points), and its Proficiency Bonus equals yours. **Multiattack.** The spirit attacks a number of times equal to half the spell's level, rounded down. **Radiant Bow (Avenger Only).** Ranged attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, range 600 feet. A hit deals 2d6 + 2 + the spell's level in Radiant damage. **Radiant Mace (Defender Only).** Melee attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 5 feet. A hit deals 1d10 + 3 + the spell's level in Radiant damage, and the spirit can also choose itself or a different creature within 10 feet of the target that it can see, and that creature gains 1d10 Temporary Hit Points. **Healing Touch (1/Day).** Once per day, the spirit can touch another creature to restore 2d8 + the spell's level Hit Points to it.", "Summon Dragon": "**Material Component.** An object engraved with the image of a dragon, worth at least 500 GP. A Dragon spirit answers your call, appearing in an unoccupied space within range that you can see. It uses the Draconic Spirit stat block below. The spirit disappears when the spell ends or when its Hit Points fall to 0. The spirit counts as an ally to you and your allies. During combat, it acts on your Initiative count, taking its turn right after yours. It follows your spoken commands, and giving them takes no action on your part. If you give it no commands, it takes the Dodge action and spends its movement staying out of danger. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Wherever the stat block refers to the spell's level, use the level of the slot you expended. **Draconic Spirit.** Large Dragon, Neutral. Armor Class: 14 + the spell's level. Hit Points: 50, plus 10 for each spell level above 5. Speed: 30 feet, plus a Fly Speed of 60 feet and a Swim Speed of 30 feet. Ability scores: Strength 19 (+4 modifier), Dexterity 14 (+2 modifier), Constitution 17 (+3 modifier), Intelligence 10 (+0 modifier), Wisdom 14 (+2 modifier), Charisma 14 (+2 modifier), and each saving throw modifier matches its ability modifier. It has Resistance to Acid, Cold, Fire, Lightning, and Poison damage, and it is immune to the Charmed, Frightened, and Poisoned conditions. Senses: Blindsight 30 feet, Darkvision 60 feet, Passive Perception 12. Languages: Draconic, and it understands the languages you know. Challenge Rating: none (0 experience points), and its Proficiency Bonus equals yours. **Shared Resistances.** When you summon the spirit, pick one of its Resistances. You have Resistance to that damage type until the spell ends. **Multiattack.** The spirit makes Rend attacks equal in number to half the spell's level, rounded down, and it also uses Breath Weapon. **Rend.** Melee attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 10 feet. A hit deals 1d6 + 4 + the spell's level in Piercing damage. **Breath Weapon.** The spirit breathes out a 30-foot Cone, and every creature inside it makes a Dexterity saving throw against your spell save DC. A creature that fails takes 2d6 damage of a type the spirit has Resistance to (you choose the type when you cast the spell), and one that succeeds takes half as much.", "Swift Quiver": "**Material Component.** A Quiver worth at least 1 GP. As part of casting the spell, you can make two attacks with a weapon that shoots Arrows or Bolts, such as a Light Crossbow or a Longbow. Until the spell ends, you can make two such attacks again as a Bonus Action. The spell's magic creates the ammunition each of these attacks requires. An Arrow or Bolt made this way deals the same damage as a nonmagical piece of ammunition of its kind, and it disintegrates the moment it hits or misses.", "Synaptic Static": "You make psychic energy burst out from a point within range. Creatures in the 20-foot-radius Sphere around that point make Intelligence saving throws. A creature that fails takes 8d6 Psychic damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. A creature that fails is also left with muddled thoughts for 1 minute, and for that minute it subtracts 1d6 from every attack roll and ability check it makes, and from any Constitution saving throw it makes to maintain Concentration. The creature makes an Intelligence saving throw whenever one of its turns ends, and a success ends the effect on it.", "Telekinesis": "You gain the power to move or manipulate creatures and objects with your mind. As part of casting the spell, and again with a Magic action on any of your later turns before the spell ends, you can focus your will on one creature or object you can see within range, producing the matching effect below. You can keep affecting the same target round after round, or switch to a new one at any time. Once you switch, the previous target stops being affected by the spell. **Creature.** You can try to move a creature that is Huge or smaller. The target makes a Strength saving throw, and on a failure you shift it as far as 30 feet in any direction, staying within the spell's range. The creature has the Restrained condition until the end of your next turn, and if you lift it into the air, it stays suspended there. When your next turn ends, it drops, unless by then you have targeted it with this option again and it has failed that new saving throw. **Object.** You can try to move an object that is Huge or smaller. If no one is wearing or carrying the object, you shift it automatically as far as 30 feet in any direction, staying within the spell's range. If a creature is wearing or carrying it, that creature makes a Strength saving throw, and on a failure you wrench the object away and shift it as far as 30 feet in any direction, staying within the spell's range. Your telekinetic grip also allows fine control of objects, letting you do things like operate a simple tool, open a door or a container, put an item into or take one out of an open container, or pour out what a vial holds.", "Teleportation Circle": "**Material Component.** Rare inks worth at least 50 GP, which the spell consumes. While casting the spell, you draw a circle with a 5-foot radius on the ground and mark it with sigils. The sigils connect your location to a permanent teleportation circle you choose. You must know that circle's sigil sequence, and it must be on your plane of existence. Inside the circle you drew, a shimmering portal opens, and it stays open until your next turn ends. Any creature that steps into the portal appears at once within 5 feet of the destination circle, or, if that space is occupied, in the closest unoccupied space. Permanent teleportation circles exist in many major temples, guildhalls, and other important sites. Each such circle has its own unique sigil sequence, a string of runes laid out in a particular pattern. At the time you first become able to cast this spell, the Dungeon Master determines two destinations on the Material Plane, and you learn their sigil sequences. You might learn more sigil sequences during your adventures, and you can memorize a new one after studying it for 1 minute. If you cast this spell at the same location daily for 365 days, a permanent teleportation circle forms there.", "Tree Stride": "You can step into a tree and travel through its interior into another tree of the same kind no more than 500 feet away. Each of the two trees must be alive and no smaller than you. It costs 5 feet of movement to enter a tree. You immediately sense the location of every other tree of that kind within 500 feet, and as part of the movement you spent entering, you can either move into one of those trees or step back out of the one you're in. Exiting puts you in a spot you choose within 5 feet of the destination tree and takes another 5 feet of movement. With no movement remaining, you instead emerge within 5 feet of the tree you went into. This transportation works only once on each of your turns, and you must be outside a tree when each of your turns ends.", "Wall of Force": "An Invisible wall of force springs up at a point you choose within range. You decide its orientation: horizontal, vertical, or angled. It can rest on a solid surface or float freely. Its shape can be a hemispherical dome or a globe, either one up to 10 feet in radius, or a flat surface built from ten panels, each 10 feet by 10 feet and touching at least one other panel. In any shape, the wall is 1/4 inch thick, and it remains for the duration. A creature whose space the wall cuts through when it appears is pushed to the side of the wall you choose. Nothing can pass through the wall physically. Dispel Magic can't dispel the wall, and it is immune to all damage, but a Disintegrate spell destroys it instantly. Because the wall extends into the Ethereal Plane, ethereal travel through it is blocked.", "Wall of Stone": "At a point of your choice within range, a nonmagical wall of solid stone springs into being. The wall is 6 inches thick and consists of ten 10-foot-by-10-foot panels, each of which must touch at least one other panel. Alternatively, the panels can be 10 feet by 20 feet, but then they are only 3 inches thick. When the wall appears, any creature whose space it cuts through gets pushed to whichever side of the wall you choose. A creature that would end up enclosed on every side by the wall (or by the wall and another solid surface) can make a Dexterity saving throw. If it succeeds, it can take a Reaction to move as far as its Speed so the wall no longer encloses it. You can give the wall any shape you want, though it can't share a space with a creature or an object. It needn't be vertical or sit on a firm foundation, but it has to merge with existing stone that solidly supports it. This means you can use the spell to bridge a chasm or build a ramp. A span longer than 20 feet requires you to halve each panel's size to make supports. You can also roughly shape the wall to form battlements and similar features. The wall is a stone object that can be damaged and broken through. Each panel has Armor Class 15 and 30 Hit Points per inch of thickness, plus Immunity to Poison and Psychic damage. A panel reduced to 0 Hit Points is destroyed, and the Dungeon Master decides whether connected panels collapse as a result. Maintaining Concentration on the spell for the whole duration makes the wall permanent, and it then can't be dispelled. If not, the wall disappears when the spell ends.", "Yolande's Regal Presence": "Unearthly majesty surrounds you in a 10-foot Emanation. You can force a creature you can see to make a Wisdom saving throw whenever the Emanation enters its space, or whenever it enters the Emanation or ends its turn there. A creature that fails takes 4d6 Psychic damage and gains the Prone condition, and you can also push it as far as 10 feet away. A creature that succeeds takes only half as much damage. No creature makes this saving throw more than once per turn.", "Arcane Gate": "You open a pair of linked teleportation portals. Choose two unoccupied Large spaces on the ground that you can see. One of them must be within range, and the other must be within 10 feet of you. A circular portal appears in each space and stays for the duration. Each portal is a flat, glowing ring full of mist that blocks vision, hovering a few inches above the ground and standing at a right angle to it. Only one side of each portal is open, and you choose which. Whatever goes into the open side of one portal comes out of the open side of the other, as though the two portals were next to each other. You can take a Bonus Action to change which way the open sides face.", "Blade Barrier": "A wall of spinning blades formed of magical energy appears within range and lasts for the duration. You choose its shape: a straight wall up to 100 feet in length, 20 feet in height, and 5 feet in thickness, or a ring up to 60 feet in diameter, 20 feet in height, and 5 feet in thickness. The wall gives Three-Quarters Cover, and its space counts as Difficult Terrain. Any creature in the wall's space must make a Dexterity saving throw. It takes 6d10 Force damage if it fails, or half as much if it succeeds. A creature also makes this saving throw when it enters the wall's space or ends its turn there. No creature makes this saving throw more than once per turn.", "Chain Lightning": "You hurl a bolt of lightning at a target you can see within range. From that first target, three more bolts jump out, striking up to three other targets of your choice. Every one of those targets must be no more than 30 feet from the first target. Each target can be a creature or an object, and no target can be struck by more than one bolt. Each target makes a Dexterity saving throw. A target that fails takes 10d8 Lightning damage, and a target that succeeds takes half as much. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 6, one more bolt jumps from the first target to another target.", "Circle of Death": "**Material Component.** Powder from a crushed black pearl worth at least 500 GP. A wave of negative energy spreads outward from a point you choose within range, filling a 60-foot-radius Sphere. Every creature in that area makes a Constitution saving throw. Those that fail take 8d8 Necrotic damage, and those that succeed take half as much. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 6, the damage increases by 2d8.", "Conjure Fey": "A Medium spirit from the Feywild appears in an unoccupied space you can see within range. It remains for the duration and takes the look of any Fey creature you choose. When it arrives, you can attack one creature within 5 feet of the spirit with a melee spell attack. If it hits, the target takes 3d12 Psychic damage, plus damage equal to your spellcasting ability modifier, and it is Frightened until the start of your next turn. Both you and the spirit count as the source of its fear. On each of your later turns, a Bonus Action lets you teleport the spirit up to 30 feet from the space it left, into an unoccupied space you can see, and then make that same attack against a creature within 5 feet of the spirit. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 6, the damage increases by 2d12.", "Contingency": "**Material Component.** A statuette of yourself, encrusted with gems and worth at least 1,500 GP. Pick a spell of level 5 or lower that you are able to cast, that takes an action to cast, and that can target you. As part of casting Contingency, you also cast that spell (the contingent spell), expending a spell slot for each, but the contingent spell doesn't take effect yet. Instead, it waits for a particular trigger, which you describe when you cast the two spells. For instance, if you cast Contingency with Water Breathing, you could set Water Breathing to take effect when water or a similar liquid engulfs you. The first time the trigger occurs, the contingent spell takes effect right away, whether you want it to or not, and Contingency then ends. The contingent spell affects only you, even if it could normally target other creatures. Only one Contingency spell can be in use for you at a time. Casting this spell again ends any other Contingency spell's effect on you. Contingency also ends on you whenever its material component is no longer on your person.", "Create Undead": "**Material Component.** One black onyx stone worth at least 150 GP for each corpse. This spell can be cast only at night. Pick as many as three corpses within range, each belonging to a Medium or Small Humanoid. Each one rises as a Ghoul under your control (its stat block is in the Monster Manual). During each of your turns, you can spend a Bonus Action to mentally command any creature you animated with this spell that is within 120 feet of you. If you control more than one, you can command any or all of them at once, giving each the same command. You decide what action the creature takes and where it moves on its next turn, or you can give a general order, such as to guard a certain place. A creature that gets no commands takes the Dodge action and only moves when it needs to avoid harm. Once it has an order, the creature keeps following it until the task is done. The creature obeys you for 24 hours, and after that it stops following any command you have given it. Keeping control for another 24 hours requires you to cast this spell on the creature before the current 24-hour period ends. Cast this way, the spell doesn't animate new creatures. It instead restores your control over as many as three creatures that you previously animated with it. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** A higher slot changes how many creatures, and which kinds, you can animate or reassert control over: four Ghouls with a level 7 spell slot, five Ghouls or two Ghasts or Wights with a level 8 spell slot, and six Ghouls, three Ghasts or Wights, or two Mummies with a level 9 spell slot. Their stat blocks are in the Monster Manual.", "Disintegrate": "A green ray shoots from you at a target you can see within range. It can be a nonmagical object, a creation of magical force (such as the wall that Wall of Force makes), or a creature. If the target is a creature, it makes a Dexterity saving throw, and on a failure it takes 10d6 + 40 Force damage. If this damage leaves the creature at 0 Hit Points, the creature and all the nonmagical things it is wearing and carrying turn into gray dust. Only a True Resurrection or a Wish spell can revive it. A Large or smaller nonmagical object or creation of magical force is disintegrated automatically by this spell. If such a target is Huge or larger, the spell disintegrates a portion of it the size of a 10-foot Cube. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 6, the damage increases by 3d6.", "Drawmij's Instant Summons": "**Material Component.** A sapphire worth at least 1,000 GP. You touch the sapphire used to cast the spell along with an object that weighs no more than 10 pounds and is no longer than 6 feet in its longest dimension. The spell places an Invisible mark on the object and writes the object's name invisibly on the sapphire. You must use a new sapphire every time you cast this spell. Afterward, you can take a Magic action to say the object's name and crush the sapphire. The object appears in your hand at once, no matter how far away it is physically or on which plane it lies, and the spell ends. If a different creature is holding or carrying the object when you crush the sapphire, the object isn't moved. Instead, you learn who that creature is and where it is right now.", "Eyebite": "While the spell lasts, your eyes become an inky void. Pick one creature within 60 feet of you. You must be able to see it. It must succeed on a Wisdom saving throw, or one of the effects below, chosen by you, affects it for the duration. On each of your turns while the spell lasts, you can take a Magic action to pick another creature as a target. A creature that has already succeeded on a save against this casting can't be targeted again. **Asleep.** It has the Unconscious condition. Taking any damage wakes it up, as does another creature using an action to shake it awake. **Panicked.** It has the Frightened condition, and on each of its turns, it must take the Dash action and flee from you along the safest, shortest route it has. The effect ends once the target moves to a space that is 60 feet or more from you and from which it can't see you. **Sickened.** It has the Poisoned condition.", "Find the Path": "**Material Component.** Divination tools worth at least 100 GP, such as a set of cards or runes. Magic shows you the most direct physical route to a place you name. You must be familiar with that place. The spell fails if the destination you name lies on another plane of existence, moves around (a mobile fortress, for example), or is not specific (for example, \"a green dragon's lair\"). Until the spell ends, as long as you and the destination share a plane of existence, you know how far away it is and in which direction. Whenever the way there splits into a choice of paths, you know which path is the most direct.", "Flesh to Stone": "You try to turn one creature within range that you can see into stone. It must make a Constitution saving throw. If it fails, it has the Restrained condition for the duration. If it succeeds, it has a Speed of 0 until your next turn starts. A Construct succeeds on the save automatically. While Restrained, the target makes another Constitution saving throw whenever one of its turns ends. Once it has succeeded against this spell three times, the spell ends. After three failures, it becomes stone, gaining the Petrified condition for the duration. The results don't have to happen one after another. Track both successes and failures until the target has three of the same kind. If you keep your Concentration on this spell for its whole possible duration, the target stays Petrified until Greater Restoration or comparable magic removes the condition.", "Forbiddance": "**Material Component.** Ruby dust worth at least 1,000 GP. You create a ward against magical travel that shields up to 40,000 square feet of floor space, reaching 30 feet above the floor. While the spell lasts, no creature can teleport into the area or enter it through a portal, such as one opened by the Gate spell. The area is also protected from planar travel, which stops creatures from getting into it by way of the Feywild, the Shadowfell, the Astral Plane, the Ethereal Plane, or the Plane Shift spell. The spell also damages creature types that you pick when you cast it, choosing one or more from this list: Aberrations, Celestials, Elementals, Fey, Fiends, Undead. A creature of a chosen type takes 5d10 Radiant or Necrotic damage (your choice at casting) the first time on a turn that it enters the area, and also whenever it ends its turn there. You can choose a password when you cast the spell. Any creature that speaks the password while entering the area is spared the spell's damage. This spell's area can't overlap the area of a different Forbiddance spell. If you cast Forbiddance at the same location daily for 30 days, it then lasts until it is dispelled, and the last casting consumes the Material components.", "Globe of Invulnerability": "A shimmering, immobile barrier forms in a 10-foot Emanation around you and stays until the spell ends. Nothing within the barrier can be affected by a spell of level 5 or lower that is cast from outside it. Such a spell can still target creatures and objects inside, but it doesn't affect them. In the same way, areas of effect from such spells leave out the space inside the barrier. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 6, the level of spells the barrier stops goes up by 1.", "Guards and Wards": "**Material Component.** A silver rod worth at least 10 GP. You set up a ward that guards as much as 2,500 square feet of floor space. Its height can reach 20 feet. You can shape it as a single 50-foot square, as one hundred 5-foot squares that touch one another, or as twenty-five 10-foot squares that touch one another. When you cast the spell, you can name individuals whom its effects don't affect. You can also choose a password, and anyone who says it aloud within 5 feet of the warded area becomes immune to the effects. The spell produces the effects below in the warded area. Dispel Magic doesn't affect Guards and Wards itself, but it can dispel each of the following effects, and the spell ends if all four are dispelled. If you cast the spell on the same area every day for 365 days, from then on it lasts until every one of its effects has been dispelled. **Corridors.** Fog fills every warded corridor, so each one is Heavily Obscured. Also, at every intersection or branching passage where a choice of direction exists, any creature other than you has a 50 percent chance to think it is heading the opposite way from the direction it picks. **Doors.** Every door in the warded area is magically locked, as though the Arcane Lock spell sealed it. You can also disguise up to ten doors with an illusion so they look like ordinary stretches of wall. **Stairs.** Every staircase in the warded area is filled with webs from top to bottom, as in the Web spell. Strands destroyed while Guards and Wards lasts grow back 10 minutes later. **Other Spell Effect.** Put one of these magical effects in the warded area: Magic Mouth in two locations, Dancing Lights in four corridors (running a simple program that the lights repeat for as long as Guards and Wards lasts), Gust of Wind in a single corridor or room (with wind that blows without pause while the spell lasts), Stinking Cloud in two locations (if the vapors are dispersed while Guards and Wards lasts, they come back within 10 minutes), or Suggestion in one 5-foot square (a creature entering that square receives the suggestion mentally).", "Harm": "You release virulent magic on a creature within range that you can see, and it makes a Constitution saving throw. A failed save deals 14d6 Necrotic damage to it and lowers its Hit Point maximum by the amount of Necrotic damage it took. If it succeeds, it takes only half as much damage. This spell can't lower a target's Hit Point maximum below 1.", "Heal": "Pick a creature within range that you can see. A surge of positive energy flows through it, and it regains 70 Hit Points. The spell also removes the Blinded, Deafened, and Poisoned conditions from that creature. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Add 10 to the Hit Points regained for each slot level above 6.", "Heroes' Feast": "**Material Component.** A gem-encrusted bowl worth at least 1,000 GP, which the spell consumes. A feast shows up on a surface within an unoccupied 10-foot Cube adjacent to you. Eating it takes 1 hour, the food vanishes when that hour ends, and none of its benefits begin until the hour is over. As many as twelve creatures can share the meal. Each creature that partakes has these benefits for 24 hours: Resistance to Poison damage, Immunity to the Frightened and Poisoned conditions, and a Hit Point maximum increased by 2d10, along with gaining that same number of Hit Points.", "Magic Jar": "**Material Component.** A gem, crystal, or reliquary worth at least 500 GP. Your soul leaves your body, which falls into a catatonic state, and enters the container used as the spell's Material component. While inside the container, your soul perceives the surroundings as if you stood in the container's space. You are unable to move or take Reactions. The one action available to you is projecting your soul as far as 100 feet out of the container, either to return to your living body, which ends the spell, or to attempt to possess the body of a Humanoid. The possession attempt can target any Humanoid you can see within 100 feet of you, but a creature protected by a Magic Circle or Protection from Evil and Good spell can't be possessed. The target attempts a Charisma saving throw. On a failure, your soul enters its body, and the target's soul is trapped in the container. On a success, it resists you, and you can't attempt to possess that creature again for 24 hours. Once you are in a creature's body, you control that body. You use the creature's Strength, Dexterity, and Constitution, its Speed and senses, and its Hit Points and Hit Point Dice in place of your own, and you keep all your other game statistics. Meanwhile, the trapped soul can perceive from the container with its own senses, though it is Incapacitated and unable to move. While in a host body, you can take a Magic action to leave it and go back to the container, provided the container is within 100 feet of you, and the host's soul then goes back to its body. Should the host body die with you inside, the creature dies, and you roll a Charisma saving throw against your own spell save DC. If you succeed, you return to the container, as long as it is no more than 100 feet away from you. If you fail, you die. Your soul goes back to your body if the container is destroyed or the spell ends. If your body is dead or more than 100 feet away from you, you die. Should the container be destroyed while another creature's soul is inside, that soul goes back to its body if the body is alive and no more than 100 feet away. If not, that creature dies. The container is destroyed when the spell ends.", "Mass Suggestion": "You suggest a course of activity, described in 25 words or fewer, to as many as twelve creatures within range that you can see and that can hear and understand you. The suggestion has to sound achievable, and it can't involve anything that would plainly cause damage to any target or to the targets' allies. You might, for example, tell them to follow that road to the village and help the villagers bring in the harvest until sundown. Or you might tell them that this is no time for fighting, so they should throw down their weapons and dance, stopping after an hour. Each target makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure, it is Charmed for the duration, or until you or your allies damage it. A Charmed target carries out the suggestion as well as it can. The suggested activity can go on for the whole duration, but if it can be finished sooner, the spell ends for a target once that target completes it. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** The duration grows with a level 7 spell slot (10 days), a level 8 slot (30 days), or a level 9 slot (366 days).", "Move Earth": "Pick an area of terrain within range that measures no more than 40 feet on a side. For the duration, you can reshape the dirt, sand, or clay there however you like. You can raise or lower the ground's elevation, dig or fill in a trench, build up or flatten a wall, or shape a pillar. No such change can be bigger than half the area's largest dimension. With a 40-foot square, for example, you could raise a pillar as tall as 20 feet, lift or lower the whole square by as much as 20 feet, or dig a trench as deep as 20 feet, and so on. Each change takes 10 minutes to finish. The ground shifts so slowly that creatures in the area usually can't be trapped or injured by it. Each time you have concentrated on the spell for another 10 minutes, you can pick a new area of terrain within range to affect. Natural stone and stone construction are beyond the spell's power to manipulate. Rocks and structures move to fit the new terrain, and a structure might collapse if your reshaping makes it unstable. In the same way, the spell has no direct effect on plant growth, and any plants ride along with the earth you move.", "Otiluke's Freezing Sphere": "You send a frigid globe streaking toward a point you choose within range, where it bursts in a 60-foot-radius Sphere. Every creature in that area makes a Constitution saving throw. A creature takes 10d6 Cold damage if it fails, or half that damage if it succeeds. If the globe hits a body of water, the water freezes to a depth of 6 inches across a 30-foot-square area, and that ice lasts for 1 minute. Creatures that were swimming on the surface of the frozen water are stuck in the ice and have the Restrained condition. A stuck creature can take an action to try to break out, making a Strength (Athletics) check against your spell save DC. You can also finish the casting without firing the globe. If you hold it back, a globe about as big as a sling bullet forms in your hand, and it feels cool when touched. At any time, you or a creature you pass it to can throw it as far as 40 feet or fling it with a sling out to the sling's normal range. On impact it shatters, producing the effect a normal casting of the spell would. Setting the globe down doesn't break it. If 1 minute passes and it hasn't shattered yet, it explodes. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each slot level above 6, the damage increases by 1d6.", "Otto's Irresistible Dance": "Choose one creature you can see within range. It makes a Wisdom saving throw. If it succeeds, the target dances in a comical way until the end of its next turn, and during that turn it has to spend its entire movement dancing in place. A target that fails has the Charmed condition for the duration. During that time, it dances comically and must spend all its movement dancing in place. It also makes Dexterity saving throws and attack rolls with Disadvantage, while attack rolls that other creatures make against it have Advantage. The target can take an action on each of its turns to pull itself together and repeat the saving throw. On a success, the spell ends on it.", "Planar Ally": "You call on an otherworldly entity for help. You must know this being, which could be a god, a demon prince, or another being of cosmic power. The entity sends a Celestial, an Elemental, or a Fiend that is loyal to it to help you, and that creature shows up in an unoccupied space within range. When you know a particular creature's name, you can say it during the casting to ask for that creature, but the Dungeon Master might send a different one anyway. When the creature arrives, nothing forces it to act in any particular way. You can ask it to carry out a service for payment, but it doesn't have to agree. The task can be simple (carry us over the chasm, or help us win a fight) or complex (spy on our foes, or guard us as we explore the dungeon). To bargain for its services, you must be able to communicate with the creature. Payment can take many forms. A Celestial might want a large donation of gold or magic items given to an allied temple, and a Fiend might ask for treasure or a living sacrifice. Some creatures may exchange their service for a quest you undertake. A task measured in minutes costs 100 GP per minute. A task measured in hours costs 1,000 GP per hour. A task measured in days (no more than 10 days) costs 10,000 GP per day. The Dungeon Master can change these payments depending on the circumstances of the casting. If the task fits the creature's ethos, the payment might be cut in half or waived entirely. A task with no danger usually needs only half the suggested payment, and a very dangerous task might call for a bigger gift. Creatures seldom agree to tasks that look suicidal. When the creature finishes the task, or when the agreed term of service runs out, it reports back to you if it can and then returns to its home plane. If the two of you can't settle on a price for its service, the creature returns to its home plane at once.", "Programmed Illusion": "**Material Component.** Jade dust worth at least 25 GP. Within range, you create an illusion of a creature, an object, or some other visible phenomenon that activates when a particular trigger happens. Until then, the illusion can't be perceived. It can be no larger than a 30-foot Cube, and when you cast the spell, you decide how the illusion will behave and what sounds it will make. This scripted performance can run for up to 5 minutes. When the trigger you set happens, the illusion appears and acts as you described. After the illusion finishes its performance, it vanishes and stays dormant for 10 minutes, and then it can be activated again. The trigger can be as broad or as specific as you want, but it has to depend on sights or sounds that happen no more than 30 feet from the area. As an example, you could make an illusion of yourself appear and warn away anyone who tries to open a trapped door. Touching the image physically reveals that it is illusory, because things can pass through it. A creature that uses the Study action to inspect the image makes an Intelligence (Investigation) check against your spell save DC and, if it succeeds, recognizes the illusion. For a creature that discerns the illusion for what it is, the image becomes see-through, and any noise from it sounds hollow.", "Summon Fiend": "**Material Component.** A bloody vial worth at least 600 GP. A fiendish spirit answers your call, appearing in an unoccupied space within range that you can see. It uses the Fiendish Spirit stat block below. As you cast the spell, pick Demon, Devil, or Yugoloth. The spirit looks like a Fiend of that type, and your pick decides certain details of its stat block. The spirit disappears when the spell ends or when its Hit Points fall to 0. The spirit counts as an ally to you and your allies. During combat, it acts on your Initiative count, taking its turn right after yours. It follows your spoken commands, and giving them takes no action on your part. If you give it no commands, it takes the Dodge action and spends its movement staying out of danger. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Wherever the stat block refers to the spell's level, use the level of the slot you expended. **Fiendish Spirit.** Large Fiend, Neutral. Armor Class: 12 + the spell's level. Hit Points: 50 (Demon only), 40 (Devil only), or 60 (Yugoloth only), plus 15 for each spell level above 6. Speed: 40 feet, plus a Climb Speed of 40 feet (Demon only) and a Fly Speed of 60 feet (Devil only). Ability scores: Strength 13 (+1 modifier), Dexterity 16 (+3 modifier), Constitution 15 (+2 modifier), Intelligence 10 (+0 modifier), Wisdom 10 (+0 modifier), Charisma 16 (+3 modifier), and each saving throw modifier matches its ability modifier. Resistances: Fire. Immunities: Poison damage and the Poisoned condition. Senses: Darkvision 60 feet, Passive Perception 10. Languages: Abyssal, Infernal, and telepathy out to 60 feet. Challenge Rating: none (0 experience points), and its Proficiency Bonus equals yours. **Death Throes (Demon Only).** The spirit explodes when the spell ends or when its Hit Points fall to 0. Each creature in a 10-foot Emanation coming from the spirit makes a Dexterity saving throw against your spell save DC. A creature that fails takes Fire damage equal to 2d10 plus the spell's level, and one that succeeds takes half as much. **Devil's Sight (Devil Only).** The spirit's Darkvision isn't hindered by magical Darkness. **Magic Resistance.** Saving throws the spirit makes against spells and other magical effects have Advantage. **Multiattack.** The spirit attacks a number of times equal to half the spell's level, rounded down. **Bite (Demon Only).** Melee attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 5 feet. A hit deals 1d12 + 3 + the spell's level in Necrotic damage. **Claws (Yugoloth Only).** Melee attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 5 feet. A hit deals 1d8 + 3 + the spell's level in Slashing damage. Whether this attack hits or misses, the spirit can teleport right afterward to an unoccupied space it can see as far as 30 feet away. **Fiery Strike (Devil Only).** Melee or ranged attack roll with a bonus equal to your spell attack modifier, reach 5 feet or range 150 feet. A hit deals 2d6 + 3 + the spell's level in Fire damage.", "Sunbeam": "A beam of sunlight shoots out from you in a Line 5 feet wide and 60 feet long. Every creature in the Line makes a Constitution saving throw. A creature that fails takes 6d8 Radiant damage and is Blinded until your next turn starts. A creature that succeeds takes only half that damage. For as long as the spell lasts, a Magic action lets you send out a new Line of radiance. For the duration, a mote of brilliant radiance glows above you. Its Bright Light reaches 30 feet, with Dim Light extending 30 feet farther. This light counts as sunlight.", "Tasha's Bubbling Cauldron": "**Material Component.** A gilded ladle worth at least 500 GP. You conjure a claw-footed cauldron of bubbling liquid. It shows up on the ground in an unoccupied space no more than 5 feet from you and remains for the duration. It can't be moved, and when the spell ends it vanishes along with the liquid inside. The liquid copies the properties of a potion you choose, which must be Common or Uncommon (for example, a Potion of Healing). You or an ally can take a Bonus Action to reach in and pull out one potion of that kind. Each potion comes in a vial that vanishes after the potion is used up. The cauldron holds enough liquid for a number of potions equal to your spellcasting ability modifier (minimum 1). Once the final potion is drawn out, the cauldron vanishes and the spell ends. Any potions taken from the cauldron that haven't been consumed vanish when you cast this spell again.", "Transport via Plants": "This spell magically links a Large or larger inanimate plant within range to a second plant, which can be any distance away as long as it is on the same plane of existence. You must have seen or touched the destination plant at least once before. For the duration, any creature can step into the target plant and come out of the destination plant by using 5 feet of movement.", "True Seeing": "**Material Component.** Mushroom powder worth at least 25 GP, which the spell consumes. Until the spell ends, a willing creature you touch gains Truesight with a range of 120 feet.", "Wall of Ice": "A wall of ice takes shape on a solid surface within range. You can make it a hemispherical dome or a globe, either one up to 10 feet in radius, or a flat surface made of ten 10-foot-square panels, each touching at least one other panel. Whatever form you choose, the wall stays for the duration and is 1 foot thick. When the wall appears, any creature whose space it cuts through gets pushed to whichever side of the wall you choose, and that creature then makes a Dexterity saving throw. That creature takes 10d6 Cold damage on a failure or half as much on a success. The wall is an object, so it can be damaged and broken through. Each 10-foot section has Armor Class 12 and 30 Hit Points. The wall has Vulnerability to Fire damage, and its damage Immunities are Cold, Poison, and Psychic. A 10-foot section reduced to 0 Hit Points is destroyed, and a sheet of frigid air remains in the space it filled. The first time on a turn that a creature moves through the frigid air, it makes a Constitution saving throw. It takes 5d6 Cold damage if it fails, or half as much if it succeeds. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 6, the damage the wall deals when it appears rises by 2d6, and the damage from passing through the frigid air rises by 1d6.", "Wall of Thorns": "A wall of tangled brush covered in needle-sharp thorns appears on a solid surface within range and lasts for the duration. You decide its shape: either a wall up to 60 feet in length and 10 feet in height, or a circle with a 20-foot diameter and a height of up to 20 feet. Both shapes are 5 feet thick. Line of sight through the wall is blocked. Creatures in its area when it appears must each make a Dexterity saving throw. A creature that fails takes 7d8 Piercing damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. Creatures can move through the wall, but slowly and painfully: each 1 foot of travel through it costs 4 feet of movement. Also, the first time on a turn that a creature enters a space in the wall, or when it ends its turn there, it makes a Dexterity saving throw. It takes 7d8 Slashing damage if it fails, or half as much if it succeeds. A creature makes this saving throw no more than once per turn. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each spell slot level above 6 adds 1d8 to both types of damage.", "Wind Walk": "You and as many as ten willing creatures you choose within range take on gaseous forms for the duration, looking like wisps of cloud. While in cloud form, a target can hover and has a Fly Speed of 300 feet. It also has Immunity to the Prone condition, along with Resistance to Bludgeoning, Piercing, and Slashing damage. A target in this form can take only two actions: the Dash action, or a Magic action to start returning to its normal form. Returning takes 1 minute, and the target has the Stunned condition throughout that minute. While the spell lasts, the target can change back into cloud form, which likewise takes a Magic action and then 1 minute of transformation. If the effect ends while a target is flying in cloud form, it descends 60 feet per round for 1 minute and lands safely. If it still hasn't landed after 1 minute, it falls the rest of the way.", "Word of Recall": "You and as many as five willing creatures within 5 feet of you teleport instantly to a sanctuary you designated earlier. You and any creatures teleporting with you appear in the unoccupied space nearest to the spot you designated when you prepared the sanctuary. If you cast the spell without first preparing a sanctuary, it has no effect. To designate a location, such as a temple, as your sanctuary, you must cast this spell there.", "Conjure Celestial": "A spirit from the Upper Planes answers your call and appears as a pillar of light. The pillar is a Cylinder, 10 feet in radius and 40 feet high, whose center is a point you pick within range. For every creature you can see in the Cylinder, pick one of the lights described below to shine on it. Until the spell ends, the Cylinder is filled with Bright Light, and when you move on your turn, you can move the Cylinder up to 30 feet as well. Each time the Cylinder moves into a space occupied by a creature you can see, and each time such a creature moves into the Cylinder or finishes its turn inside it, you can bathe that creature in one of the lights. No creature can be affected by this spell more than once per turn. **Healing Light.** The target regains 4d12 Hit Points, adding your spellcasting ability modifier to the total. **Searing Light.** The target makes a Dexterity saving throw. A target that fails takes 6d12 Radiant damage, and a target that succeeds takes half as much. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 7, the healing and the damage each increase by 1d12.", "Delayed Blast Fireball": "A beam of yellow light shoots from you and condenses into a glowing bead at a point you choose within range, where it remains as long as the spell lasts. The bead explodes once the spell ends. Every creature within a 20-foot-radius Sphere centered on that point must make a Dexterity saving throw. A creature that fails takes Fire damage equal to the total damage built up, and one that succeeds takes half as much. The spell's base damage is 12d6. Each time your turn ends while the spell is still going, the damage increases by 1d6. Any creature that touches the glowing bead while the spell lasts makes a Dexterity saving throw. If it fails, the spell ends and the bead explodes. If it succeeds, it can throw the bead as far as 40 feet. Should the thrown bead enter a creature's space or hit a solid object, the bead explodes and the spell ends. Any flammable objects in the blast that no one is wearing or carrying catch fire when the bead explodes. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** For each spell slot level above 7, the base damage increases by 1d6.", "Divine Word": "You speak a single word charged with power drawn from the Upper Planes. Every creature of your choice within range makes a Charisma saving throw. When a target fails and has 50 Hit Points or fewer, its current Hit Points decide what happens to it. With 0 to 20 Hit Points, it dies. With 21 to 30, for 1 hour it has the Blinded, Deafened, and Stunned conditions. With 31 to 40, it has the Blinded and Deafened conditions for 10 minutes. With 41 to 50, it has the Deafened condition for 1 minute. No matter how many Hit Points it has, a Celestial, an Elemental, a Fey, or a Fiend that fails the save is hurled back to its plane of origin (unless it is already there). For 24 hours afterward, it can't come back to the current plane by any means other than a Wish spell.", "Etherealness": "You step into the edges of the Ethereal Plane, in the region where it overlaps your current plane, and you stay in the Border Ethereal for the duration. While there, you can move in any direction, but each foot you move up or down costs 1 extra foot of movement. The plane you left is visible to you in shades of gray, though you can't see anything on it beyond 60 feet. As long as you are on the Ethereal Plane, the only creatures, objects, and effects that you can affect, or that can affect you, are those on that plane. Creatures that aren't there can't perceive you or interact with you unless some feature gives them the means. Once the spell is over, you go back to the plane you left, at the spot corresponding to your space in the Border Ethereal. If that spot is already occupied, you are shoved into the closest unoccupied space, and you take Force damage equal to double the number of feet you were shifted. If you cast the spell while on the Ethereal Plane, or on a plane that has no border with it (one of the Outer Planes, for example), the spell ends immediately. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** Each spell slot level above 7 lets you target up to three willing creatures, counting yourself. All of them need to be within 10 feet of you at the moment you cast the spell.", "Finger of Death": "You direct negative energy at a creature you can see within range, which must make a Constitution saving throw. A failed save deals 7d8 + 30 Necrotic damage to it, and a successful one deals half as much. When your next turn starts, any Humanoid this spell killed rises as a Zombie (see appendix B) and carries out the orders you speak.", "Fire Storm": "Within range, a firestorm erupts. Its area is made up of as many as ten 10-foot Cubes, arranged however you like, and every Cube has to be contiguous with another Cube. Each creature in the area must make a Dexterity saving throw. A creature takes 7d10 Fire damage if it fails, or half as much if it succeeds. Any flammable object in the area that isn't being worn or carried catches fire.", "Forcecage": "**Material Component.** Ruby dust worth at least 1,500 GP, which the spell consumes. An immobile, Invisible, Cube-shaped prison of magical force springs into being around an area of your choice within range. You decide whether the prison is a cage or a solid box. A cage can measure up to 20 feet on each side and is built from bars 1/2 inch in diameter with 1/2 inch of space between them. A box can measure up to 10 feet on each side, forming a solid barrier that stops all matter from passing through and blocking any spell cast into or out of the area. When you cast the spell, any creature entirely inside the area is trapped. A creature only partly inside the area, or too large to fit in it, is pushed away from the area's center until it is completely outside. A creature trapped inside can't leave by nonmagical means. If it tries to leave by teleportation or interplanar travel, a Charisma saving throw comes first. A success lets it use that magic to get out. On a failure, it doesn't get out, and the spell or effect is wasted. The prison also extends into the Ethereal Plane, blocking ethereal travel. Dispel Magic can't dispel this spell.", "Mirage Arcane": "In an area as large as 1 mile square, you make the terrain look, sound, smell, and even feel like a different kind of terrain. For example, a road or open fields could take on the look of a swamp, hill, crevasse, or other rough or impassable ground. A pond can be made to look like a grassy meadow, a cliff like a gentle slope, or a gully full of rocks like a wide, smooth road. In the same way, you can change how structures look, or add structures where there are none. The spell doesn't disguise, hide, or add creatures. Because the illusion includes sounds, sights, textures, and smells, it can turn clear ground into Difficult Terrain (or the reverse) or otherwise hinder movement through the area. If a piece of the illusory terrain, like a stick or a rock, is taken out of the spell's area, it vanishes at once. A creature with Truesight sees the terrain's true form through the illusion. Every other element of the illusion still remains for them, though, so such a creature knows the illusion is there but can still physically interact with it.", "Mordenkainen's Magnificent Mansion": "**Material Component.** A miniature door worth at least 15 GP. A shimmering door appears within range and remains for the duration. It is 5 feet wide and 10 feet tall, and it opens onto an extradimensional dwelling. While the door is open, you and any creatures you chose when casting the spell can enter the dwelling. While you are within 30 feet of the door, you can open or close it (no action required). When closed, the door can't be perceived. Through the door lies a magnificent foyer, with many rooms past it. The air in the dwelling is clean, fresh, and warm. You can lay out the dwelling with any floor plan you like, as long as it takes up no more than 50 contiguous 10-foot Cubes. You choose how the place is furnished and decorated. It holds enough food for a nine-course banquet serving up to 100 people. Furnishings and other objects that the spell creates turn into smoke and dissipate if they are taken out of the dwelling. A staff of 100 near-transparent servants waits on everyone who enters. You decide how these servants look and what they wear. They are invulnerable and follow your commands. A servant can do any task a human could, but no servant can attack or do anything that would directly harm another creature. They can, for instance, fetch items, tidy up, mend, fold laundry, light fires, serve meals, and pour wine. The servants are unable to leave the dwelling. Once the spell ends, any creatures or objects still inside the extradimensional space are pushed out into the unoccupied spaces closest to the entrance.", "Mordenkainen's Sword": "**Material Component.** A miniature sword worth at least 250 GP. A spectral sword appears, hovering within range, and remains for the duration. As it appears, make a melee spell attack against a target no more than 5 feet from the sword. On a hit, the target takes 4d12 Force damage plus extra Force damage equal to your spellcasting ability modifier. On each of your later turns, a Bonus Action lets you fly the sword as far as 30 feet to a spot you can see, then attack again, against either the same target or another one.", "Plane Shift": "**Material Component.** A forked metal rod worth at least 250 GP that is attuned to a plane of existence. You and as many as eight willing creatures holding hands in a circle are carried to another plane of existence. You can name a destination in broad terms, for example the palace of Dispater on the Nine Hells' second level, or the City of Brass in the Elemental Plane of Fire. You arrive in or near that destination, as the Dungeon Master determines. Alternatively, if you know the sigil sequence for a teleportation circle located on another plane, the spell can bring you to that circle. If the circle is too cramped to fit every creature you brought, those creatures appear in the unoccupied spaces closest to the circle.", "Power Word Fortify": "Choose up to six creatures within range that you can see. The spell gives them a total of 120 Temporary Hit Points, which you split among those creatures as you like.", "Prismatic Spray": "Eight rays of light flash out from you and fill a 60-foot Cone. Every creature in that area makes a Dexterity saving throw. For each target, roll 1d8 to see which color of ray affects it: 1 is Red, 2 is Orange, 3 is Yellow, 4 is Green, 5 is Blue, 6 is Indigo, 7 is Violet, and 8 is Special. **Red.** A failed save means 12d6 Fire damage, and a successful one halves it. **Orange.** A failed save means 12d6 Acid damage, and a successful one halves it. **Yellow.** A failed save means 12d6 Lightning damage, and a successful one halves it. **Green.** A failed save means 12d6 Poison damage, and a successful one halves it. **Blue.** A failed save means 12d6 Cold damage, and a successful one halves it. **Indigo.** A target that fails the save has the Restrained condition. When each of its turns ends, it makes a Constitution saving throw. Three successful saves end the condition. Three failed saves give it the Petrified condition until an effect such as the Greater Restoration spell frees it. The successes and failures don't need to come in a row. Keep count of both until the target reaches three of one kind. **Violet.** On a failed save, the target has the Blinded condition. At the start of your next turn, it makes a Wisdom saving throw. A success ends the condition. A failure ends the condition too, but the creature is teleported to a different plane of existence chosen by the Dungeon Master. **Special.** Two rays strike the target. Roll the die twice, and reroll any 8.", "Project Image": "**Material Component.** A statuette of yourself worth at least 5 GP. You make an illusory duplicate of yourself that lasts for the duration. The duplicate can appear at any place within range that you have seen before, no matter what obstacles lie in between. It has your look and your voice but is intangible. Any damage to it makes it disappear and ends the spell. You can see and hear from the illusion's location, using its eyes and ears as if you occupied its space. By taking a Magic action, you can make it move up to 60 feet and gesture, talk, and behave however you like. It imitates your mannerisms perfectly. Touching the image physically reveals that it is illusory, because things can pass through it. A creature that uses the Study action to inspect the image makes an Intelligence (Investigation) check against your spell save DC and, if it succeeds, recognizes the illusion. For a creature that discerns the illusion for what it is, the image becomes see-through, and any noise from it sounds hollow.", "Regenerate": "Your touch restores 4d8 + 15 Hit Points to a creature. Until the spell ends, that creature also regains 1 Hit Point when each of its turns starts, and any of its body parts that have been severed grow back after 2 minutes.", "Resurrection": "**Material Component.** A diamond worth at least 1,000 GP, which the spell consumes. With a touch, you return a dead creature to life if it wasn't Undead when it died, its death wasn't from old age, and it has been dead for no more than a century. The creature comes back with all its Hit Points. The spell neutralizes any poisons that were affecting the creature when it died. It also heals every mortal wound and regrows any missing body parts. Returning from death is an ordeal. The target has a -4 penalty to D20 Tests. Each time the target finishes a Long Rest, the penalty shrinks by 1 until it reaches 0. Reviving a creature dead for 365 days or more strains you. You then have Disadvantage on D20 Tests and can't cast any more spells until you finish a Long Rest.", "Reverse Gravity": "Pick a point within range. Gravity reverses inside a Cylinder 50 feet in radius and 100 feet high, centered on that point. Every creature and object in that area that isn't anchored to the ground falls upward until it reaches the top of the Cylinder. To avoid that upward fall, a creature can make a Dexterity saving throw to catch hold of a fixed object within its reach. If creatures and objects meet a ceiling or an anchored object during this upward fall, they strike it just as they would in a normal downward fall. An affected creature or object that reaches the top of the Cylinder without hitting anything hovers there for the duration. When the spell ends, the affected objects and creatures fall back down.", "Sequester": "**Material Component.** Gem dust worth at least 5,000 GP, which the spell consumes. With a touch, you magically conceal an object or a willing creature. While the spell lasts, the target has the Invisible condition. Divination spells can't target it, and magic can neither detect it nor view it from afar. A creature sequestered this way falls into suspended animation: it has the Unconscious condition, stops aging, and doesn't need air, food, or water. You can name a condition that will end the spell early. You decide what it is, but it must happen or be visible within 1 mile of the target, such as after 1,000 years pass, or when the tarrasque wakes up. Any damage to the target also ends the spell.", "Simulacrum": "**Material Component.** Powdered ruby worth at least 1,500 GP, which the spell consumes. You shape a simulacrum of one Beast or Humanoid, which must remain within 10 feet of you throughout the casting. To complete the spell, you touch both the creature and a heap of ice or snow matching its size, and the heap turns into the simulacrum, which is a creature. It has the game statistics the original creature had when you cast the spell, with these exceptions: it is a Construct, its Hit Point maximum is halved, and it is unable to cast this spell. It is Friendly toward you and toward any creatures you designate. It carries out your commands and takes its actions on your turn in combat. It can't gain levels or take Short or Long Rests. Lost Hit Points can be restored to the simulacrum in only one way: you repair it while you take a Long Rest. The repair uses up components worth 100 GP for each Hit Point restored, and the simulacrum must stay within 5 feet of you while you work on it. The simulacrum remains until its Hit Points drop to 0. At that point it turns back into snow and melts away. Casting this spell again instantly destroys any simulacrum you made with it before.", "Symbol": "**Material Component.** Powdered diamond worth at least 1,000 GP, which the spell consumes. You draw a harmful glyph either on a surface, such as part of a floor or wall, or inside a closable object like a book or a chest. The glyph can be at most 10 feet in diameter. An object bearing the glyph has to stay where it is. If it is moved more than 10 feet from the place where you cast the spell, the glyph breaks and the spell ends without triggering. The glyph is almost impossible to see. Spotting it requires a Wisdom (Perception) check that succeeds against your spell save DC. While inscribing it, you also set the glyph's trigger and choose the effect the symbol carries: Death, Discord, Fear, Pain, Sleep, or Stunning, each described below. **Set the Trigger.** You choose what sets off the glyph when you cast the spell. Typical triggers for a glyph on a surface are touching or stepping on it, removing another object that covers it, or coming within a certain distance of it. For a glyph inside an object, typical triggers are seeing the glyph or opening the object. You can also narrow the trigger, for instance so the glyph activates only for creatures of a certain type, such as Aberrations. You can also define conditions under which creatures don't trigger it, such as speaking a particular password. Once triggered, the glyph glows, shedding Dim Light throughout a 60-foot-radius Sphere for 10 minutes, after which the spell ends. The effect targets each creature in the Sphere at the moment the glyph activates, and it also targets a creature that ends its turn in the Sphere or moves into it for the first time on a turn. No creature is targeted more than once per turn. **Death.** Every target makes a Constitution saving throw. A target that fails takes 10d10 Necrotic damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. **Discord.** Every target makes a Wisdom saving throw. A target that fails quarrels with other creatures for 1 minute, and during that time it can't communicate in any meaningful way and has Disadvantage on attack rolls and ability checks. **Fear.** Every target makes a Wisdom saving throw, and those that fail have the Frightened condition for 1 minute. On each of its turns, a target Frightened this way must, if able, move a minimum of 30 feet away from the glyph. **Pain.** Every target makes a Constitution saving throw, and those that fail have the Incapacitated condition for 1 minute. **Sleep.** Every target makes a Wisdom saving throw, and those that fail have the Unconscious condition for 10 minutes. A sleeping creature wakes up if it takes damage or if someone uses an action to shake it awake. **Stunning.** Every target makes a Wisdom saving throw, and those that fail have the Stunned condition for 1 minute.", "Teleport": "This spell instantly carries you and up to eight willing creatures you can see within range, or one object you can see within range, to a destination you choose. If you target an object, it has to be no bigger than Large, and no unwilling creature can be holding or carrying it. You must know the destination, and it must be on your current plane of existence. How familiar you are with the destination decides whether you get there. The Dungeon Master rolls 1d100 and checks the Teleportation Outcome table, then applies the explanations that follow it. **Teleportation Outcome.** Results by familiarity: Permanent circle gives On Target on 01–00. Linked object gives On Target on 01–00. Very familiar gives Mishap on 01–05, Similar Area on 06–13, Off Target on 14–24, and On Target on 25–00. Seen casually gives Mishap on 01–33, Similar Area on 34–43, Off Target on 44–53, and On Target on 54–00. Viewed once or described gives Mishap on 01–43, Similar Area on 44–53, Off Target on 54–73, and On Target on 74–00. False destination gives Mishap on 01–50 and Similar Area on 51–00. **Familiarity.** The entries in the table's Familiarity column mean the following. \"Permanent circle\" refers to a permanent teleportation circle, and you know its sigil sequence. \"Linked object\" applies when you have in your possession something removed from the destination within the last six months, for example a book taken from a wizard's library. \"Very familiar\" covers somewhere you have visited often or studied carefully, or somewhere visible to you as you cast the spell. \"Seen casually\" covers a place you have seen more than once but don't know well. A place counts as \"Viewed once or described\" if you saw it a single time, possibly with magic, or if you know it from someone else's account, maybe through a map. \"False destination\" refers to a place that doesn't exist. Maybe you tried to scry an enemy's sanctum but saw an illusion instead, or you are trying to teleport to a place that is gone. **Mishap.** The spell's unpredictable magic makes the trip a rough one. Every creature that teleports, or the target object, takes 3d10 Force damage. The Dungeon Master then rolls on the table again to find where you end up. Mishaps can happen more than once, dealing damage each time. **Similar Area.** You and your group (or the target object) arrive in a different area that looks like the intended one or matches its theme, in the closest such place. For example, if you were heading to your home laboratory, you might end up in a laboratory belonging to someone else in the same city. **Off Target.** You and your group (or the target object) arrive 2d12 miles from the destination in a random direction. The direction comes from a 1d8 roll: a 1 means east, a 2 southeast, a 3 south, a 4 southwest, a 5 west, a 6 northwest, a 7 north, and an 8 northeast. **On Target.** Everyone teleporting, or the target object, arrives at the intended spot.", "Animal Shapes": "Pick any number of willing creatures you can see within range. Each target changes shape into a Large or smaller Beast of your choice whose Challenge Rating is 4 or lower, and each target can take a different form. On your later turns, a Magic action lets you change the targets into new forms. The chosen Beast's game statistics replace those of the target, but the target retains its Intelligence, Wisdom, and Charisma scores, its alignment, its creature type, its Hit Points and Hit Point Dice, and its ability to communicate. What the target can do is limited by the anatomy of its Beast form, and it can't cast spells. Its equipment merges into the new form, and it can't use any of that equipment while transformed. Each target also gains Temporary Hit Points, and the amount is the Hit Points of its Beast form. For each target, the transformation lasts until the duration ends, until the target has no Temporary Hit Points left, or until the target uses a Bonus Action to leave the form.", "Antimagic Field": "A 10-foot Emanation of antimagic surrounds you. Spellcasting, Magic actions, and the creation of other magical effects are all impossible inside the aura, and none of these can target or otherwise affect anything in it. The magical properties of magic items don't function inside the aura, and they don't work on anything inside it either. Areas of effect from spells or other magic stop at the aura's edge. Teleporting into or out of the aura is impossible, and so is planar travel inside it. A portal in the aura is closed for as long as it remains there. Ongoing spells are suppressed in the area, unless an Artifact or a deity cast them. A suppressed effect doesn't function, but the time it spends suppressed still counts against its duration. Dispel Magic doesn't affect the aura, and auras from different Antimagic Field spells don't cancel each other out.", "Antipathy/Sympathy": "When you cast the spell, you choose between antipathy and sympathy, and you target a single creature or object of Huge size or smaller. Next, name a kind of creature, for example red dragons, goblins, or vampires. Any creature of that kind that comes within 120 feet of the target must make a Wisdom saving throw. Your choice of antipathy or sympathy sets the result for a creature that fails it. **Antipathy.** The creature gains the Frightened condition and, on each of its turns, has to spend its movement getting as far from the target as possible by the safest path. **Sympathy.** The creature gains the Charmed condition and, on each of its turns, has to spend its movement getting as close to the target as possible by the safest path. Once within 5 feet of the target, the creature can't move away willingly. If the target deals damage to the Charmed creature, that creature can attempt a Wisdom saving throw that would end the effect, as detailed below. **Ending the Effect.** A Frightened or Charmed creature that ends its turn over 120 feet from the target makes a Wisdom saving throw. If it succeeds, the target stops affecting it. Any creature that succeeds on a saving throw against this effect becomes immune to it for 1 minute, and after that it can be affected again.", "Befuddlement": "You strike at the mind of one creature within range that you can see, and it makes an Intelligence saving throw. If it fails, it takes 10d12 Psychic damage and becomes unable to cast spells or take the Magic action. Each time 30 days pass, the target repeats the saving throw at the end of that period, and a success ends the effect. A Greater Restoration, Heal, or Wish spell can also end the effect. If the target succeeds on the first saving throw, it takes only half as much damage.", "Clone": "**Material Component.** A sealable vessel worth at least 2,000 GP that is big enough to hold the creature being cloned, plus a diamond worth at least 1,000 GP, which the spell consumes. Touch a creature, or a piece of its flesh at least 1 cubic inch in size. Inside the vessel used to cast the spell, an inert copy of that creature begins to form, and it is fully grown after 120 days. You decide whether the finished clone matches the creature's current age or is younger. As long as nothing disturbs its vessel, the clone stays inert and lasts indefinitely. If the original creature dies once the clone has finished forming, the creature's soul moves into the clone, provided that soul is free and willing to come back. The clone's body is physically identical to the original's. It also shares the original's personality, memories, and abilities, though it carries none of the original's equipment. Whatever remains of the original creature's body becomes inert and can't be brought back to life, because the creature's soul now resides elsewhere.", "Control Weather": "For the duration, you control the weather within 5 miles of you. You must be outdoors to cast this spell, and the spell ends early if you go indoors. As you cast it, you alter the current weather conditions, which the Dungeon Master determines. You can alter precipitation, temperature, and wind. New conditions take 1d4 × 10 minutes to set in, and once they have, you can alter the conditions again. When the spell ends, the weather slowly goes back to normal. To alter a condition, find its current stage on the lists below and move it one stage up or down. When you alter the wind, you can also change which way it blows. **Precipitation.** Stage 1, clear. Stage 2, light clouds. Stage 3, overcast or ground fog. Stage 4, rain, hail, or snow. Stage 5, torrential rain, driving hail, or blizzard. **Temperature.** Stage 1, heat wave. Stage 2, hot. Stage 3, warm. Stage 4, cool. Stage 5, cold. Stage 6, freezing. **Wind.** Stage 1, calm. Stage 2, moderate wind. Stage 3, strong wind. Stage 4, gale. Stage 5, storm.", "Demiplane": "A shadowy Medium door appears on a flat, solid surface you can see within range. The door can be opened and closed, and it opens onto a demiplane: an empty room that measures 30 feet in every dimension, built of wood or stone, whichever you choose. When the spell ends, the door disappears, and any objects in the demiplane stay there. Creatures inside also stay unless they choose to be pushed out through the door as it disappears, in which case they land with the Prone condition in the unoccupied spaces nearest to where the door was. Whenever you cast this spell, you can make a new demiplane or link the shadowy door to a demiplane you made with an earlier casting. You can instead link the shadowy door to a demiplane that another creature made with this spell, as long as you know that demiplane's nature and contents.", "Dominate Monster": "Pick one creature within range that you can see. Unless it succeeds on a Wisdom saving throw, it has the Charmed condition for the duration. If you or your allies are in a fight with it, it rolls this save with Advantage. Each time the target takes damage, it repeats the save, and on a success the spell ends on it. While the Charmed target and you are on the same plane of existence, the two of you share a telepathic link. On your turn, you can send commands through this link (no action required), for example \"Fetch that object,\" \"Attack that creature,\" or \"Move over there.\" On its turn, the target tries as hard as it can to obey. Once it finishes an order and you give it no further direction, it acts and moves as it wishes, concentrating on its own protection. You can order the target to take a Reaction, but doing so requires you to take your own Reaction as well. **Using a Higher-Level Spell Slot.** With a level 9 spell slot, your Concentration can be maintained for up to 8 hours.", "Earthquake": "Pick a point on the ground within range that you can see. For the duration, a violent tremor tears through the ground within a 100-foot-radius circle around that point, turning that ground into Difficult Terrain. Every creature on the ground in the area must make a Dexterity saving throw as soon as you cast the spell and again whenever one of your turns ends while the spell lasts. A creature that fails has the Prone condition and loses its Concentration. You can also bring about the effects below. **Fissures.** When the turn on which you cast the spell ends, 1d6 fissures in total split open in the area. You decide where they go, though none can lie beneath a structure. Every fissure is 10 feet wide and 1d10 x 10 feet deep, stretching from one edge of the area to another. A creature occupying the same space as a fissure falls in unless it succeeds on a Dexterity saving throw. A creature that succeeds is carried along with the fissure's edge as it opens. **Structures.** When you cast the spell, and again whenever one of your turns ends until the spell ends, the tremor deals 50 Bludgeoning damage to every structure touching the ground in the area. Any structure reduced to 0 Hit Points collapses. A Dexterity saving throw is required of each creature whose distance from the collapsing structure is no more than half the structure's height. On a failure, the creature takes 12d6 Bludgeoning damage and has the Prone condition, and it is also buried under the rubble. To get free, it must take an action and succeed on a DC 20 Strength (Athletics) check. If it succeeds on the save, it takes only half as much damage.", "Glibness": "Until the spell ends, each time you make a Charisma check, you can treat the number you rolled as a 15 instead. In addition, no matter what you say, any magic that would reveal whether you are telling the truth shows that you are being truthful.", "Holy Aura": "**Material Component.** A reliquary worth at least 1,000 GP. For the duration, an aura extends from you as a 30-foot Emanation. Creatures of your choice inside the aura have Advantage on every saving throw, while attack rolls that other creatures make against them have Disadvantage. In addition, when a Fiend or an Undead hits one of these protected creatures with a melee attack roll, the attacker makes a Constitution saving throw. On a failure, it is Blinded until its next turn ends.", "Incendiary Cloud": "Pick a point within range. A swirling cloud of smoke and embers fills a 20-foot-radius Sphere centered there, and the cloud's area is Heavily Obscured. The cloud stays for the duration unless a strong wind, like the one Gust of Wind creates, scatters it sooner. Each creature in the cloud when it appears makes a Dexterity saving throw. It takes 10d8 Fire damage on a failure, or half as much on a success. The same save is required when the Sphere moves into a creature's space, when a creature enters the Sphere, and when a creature ends its turn inside it. No creature has to make this save more than once in a turn. At the start of each of your turns, the cloud travels 10 feet in a direction you choose, away from you.", "Maze": "You banish a creature within range that you can see into a labyrinthine demiplane. It stays there for the duration or until it finds its way out of the maze. To try to escape, the target can take a Study action and make a DC 20 Intelligence (Investigation) check. If the check succeeds, it gets out, which ends the spell. Once the spell is over, the target returns to the space it left, or to the closest unoccupied space if something else now fills that space.", "Mind Blank": "For as long as the spell lasts, a willing creature you touch has Immunity to the Charmed condition and to Psychic damage. Effects that would read its thoughts, sense its alignment or emotions, or detect its location by magic have no effect on it. No spell can observe the target from afar, learn information about it, or take control of its mind, and this applies even to Wish.", "Power Word Stun": "Pick one creature you can see within range and flood its mind with power. A target with no more than 150 Hit Points has the Stunned condition. If it has more, its Speed drops to 0 until your next turn starts. A creature Stunned this way makes a Constitution saving throw as each of its turns ends. On a success, the condition ends on it.", "Sunburst": "Brilliant sunlight bursts across a 60-foot-radius Sphere centered on a point of your choosing within range. Every creature inside the Sphere must make a Constitution saving throw. Those that fail take 12d6 Radiant damage and have the Blinded condition for 1 minute, and those that succeed take only half that damage. A creature Blinded by this spell repeats the Constitution saving throw whenever one of its turns ends, and a success frees it from the effect. The spell also dispels any Darkness in its area that was made by a spell.", "Telepathy": "You form a telepathic link between yourself and a willing creature you are familiar with. Distance doesn't matter as long as the creature is somewhere on your plane of existence, and the spell ends if the two of you are ever on different planes. Until the spell ends, the two of you can exchange words, images, sounds, and other sensory messages through the link without delay, and the target knows that you are the one communicating with it. The spell lets a creature grasp the meaning of your words and of any sensory messages you send it.", "Tsunami": "You call a wall of water into being at a point of your choice within range. Its dimensions can be up to 300 feet in length, 300 feet in height, and 50 feet in thickness, and it remains for the duration. As the wall appears, every creature in its area must make a Strength saving throw. A creature that fails takes 6d10 Bludgeoning damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. From then on, at the start of each of your turns, the wall moves 50 feet away from you, sweeping along any creatures inside it. Each Huge or smaller creature that is inside the wall, or whose space the moving wall enters, makes a Strength saving throw and takes 5d10 Bludgeoning damage if it fails. No creature can take this damage more than once per round. When that turn ends, the wall loses 50 feet of height, and the damage it deals in later rounds is reduced by 1d10. Once the wall's height reaches 0 feet, the spell ends. A creature trapped in the wall can move by swimming, but the force of the wave means it can move only if it passes a Strength (Athletics) check with a DC equal to your spell save DC. If the check fails, the creature can't move at all. Any creature that moves out of the wall drops to the ground.", "Astral Projection": "**Material Component.** For each target of the spell, one jacinth worth at least 1,000 GP and one silver bar worth at least 100 GP. The spell consumes all of them. You and up to eight willing creatures within range send your astral bodies to the Astral Plane. If you are already on that plane, the spell ends at once. Each target's physical body stays behind in suspended animation: it has the Unconscious condition, needs no food or air, and doesn't age. An astral form looks almost exactly like its body and copies its game statistics and possessions. The main difference is a silvery cord that trails from between the form's shoulder blades and fades from sight 1 foot away. The cord is cut only when an effect says so, and then the target's body and astral form both die. An astral form can move through the Astral Plane. The moment the form exits that plane, the target's body and possessions are carried along the silver cord, and the target re-enters its body on the new plane. Damage and other effects on an astral form don't affect the body, and the reverse is also true. The spell ends for a target whose body or astral form drops to 0 Hit Points. Taking a Magic action to dismiss the spell ends it for every target. A target that isn't dead when the spell ends for it reappears in its body and leaves suspended animation.", "Foresight": "A willing creature you touch gains a limited power to glimpse the immediate future. Until the spell ends, it has Advantage on D20 Tests, and attack rolls made against it by other creatures have Disadvantage. Casting this spell again ends it early.", "Gate": "**Material Component.** A diamond worth at least 5,000 GP. You open a portal joining an unoccupied space within range that you can see to an exact location on another plane of existence. The portal is a round opening whose diameter you choose, from 5 to 20 feet, and you can point it in any direction. It remains for the duration, and you can see its destination through it. The portal has a front and a back on each plane where it appears, and only something moving through the front can pass through it. Whatever does so is carried instantly to the other plane, arriving in the unoccupied space closest to the portal. Deities and other rulers of planes can prevent portals created by this spell from opening near them or anywhere within their domains. As you cast the spell, you can speak the name of one specific creature. A pseudonym, title, or nickname won't work. If the named creature is on a different plane from you, the portal opens right next to it and brings it through to the closest unoccupied space on your side. The spell grants you no special power over that creature, which acts as the Dungeon Master sees fit. It might attack you, help you, or leave.", "Imprisonment": "**Material Component.** A statuette of the target worth at least 5,000 GP. You bind a creature within range that you can see with a magical restraint. The target attempts a Wisdom saving throw. If it succeeds, the spell has no effect on it, and it has Immunity to this spell for 24 hours. If it fails, it becomes imprisoned. An imprisoned target doesn't age and has no need to eat, drink, or breathe. Divination spells can't locate or perceive it, and it is unable to teleport. For as long as the spell lasts, the target is also subject to one effect of your choice from this list. **Burial.** Deep under the earth, the target is sealed inside a hollow globe of magical force that is just big enough to hold it. Nothing can get into or out of the globe. **Chaining.** Chains anchored firmly in the ground keep the target where it is. It is Restrained, and no means can move it. **Hedged Prison.** The target is shut away in a demiplane, which is protected against both teleportation and planar travel. You decide whether the demiplane is a labyrinth, a cage, a tower, or something similar. **Minimus Containment.** The target shrinks to a height of 1 inch and is held inside a gemstone or similar object that can't be destroyed. Light passes through the gemstone, letting the target see out and other creatures see in, but no other thing can cross it by any means. **Slumber.** The target is Unconscious and can't be woken up. **Ending the Spell.** As you cast the spell, you set a trigger that ends it. The trigger may be simple or elaborate, as you choose, but the Dungeon Master has to agree that it is highly likely to occur within the next ten years. It has to be an action that can be observed, for example someone bringing a certain offering to your god's temple, saving your true love, or defeating a specific monster. Dispel Magic can end this spell only when it is cast with a level 9 spell slot and it targets the prison or the component that was used to create it.", "Mass Heal": "A flood of healing energy pours out of you into the creatures nearby. You can restore a total of up to 700 Hit Points, dividing them as you like among any number of creatures within range that you can see. Every creature healed this way also has the Blinded, Deafened, and Poisoned conditions removed from it.", "Meteor Swarm": "Blazing orbs of fire plunge to the ground at four different points within range that you can see. Every creature in a 40-foot-radius Sphere centered on any of those points attempts a Dexterity saving throw. A creature that fails takes 20d6 Fire damage plus 20d6 Bludgeoning damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. A creature inside more than one of the fiery Spheres is affected only once. A nonmagical object in the spell's area that no one is wearing or carrying also takes the damage, and it starts burning if it is flammable.", "Power Word Heal": "One creature you can see within range is bathed in a wave of healing energy and regains all its Hit Points. The spell also ends any of these conditions on it: Charmed, Frightened, Paralyzed, Poisoned, or Stunned. If the creature is Prone, it can stand up by using its Reaction.", "Power Word Kill": "You command one creature you can see within range to die. A target with 100 Hit Points or fewer dies. Otherwise, it takes 12d12 Psychic damage.", "Prismatic Wall": "A shimmering, multicolored plane of light forms an opaque vertical wall centered on a point within range. The wall can be up to 1 inch thick, 30 feet high, and 90 feet long. Or you can instead form it into a globe with a diameter of up to 30 feet, centered on a point within range. It remains for the duration. If you place the wall in a space a creature occupies, the spell ends at once with no effect. Bright Light extends 100 feet from the wall, and Dim Light extends a further 100 feet. You and any creatures you designate at the time of casting can pass through the wall and be near it without harm. Any other creature that can see the wall must make a Constitution saving throw when it moves to a spot no more than 20 feet from the wall or begins its turn in such a spot. On a failure, it has the Blinded condition for 1 minute. The wall is made of seven layers, each a different color. A creature that reaches into or passes through the wall goes through every layer, one at a time. At each layer, the creature makes a Dexterity saving throw, and if it fails, that layer's properties affect it. The layers are described below in order, first to last. The wall has an Armor Class of 10. Each layer has its own means of destruction, and the layers can be destroyed only one by one, starting with red and ending with violet. Once destroyed, a layer stays gone for the duration. The wall is unaffected by Antimagic Field, and the only layer Dispel Magic can affect is the violet one. **Red.** A failed save means 12d6 Fire damage, and a successful one halves it. Nonmagical ranged attacks can't get through this layer. Taking at least 25 Cold damage destroys it. **Orange.** A failed save means 12d6 Acid damage, and a successful one halves it. Magical ranged attacks can't get through it. A strong wind, such as the one Gust of Wind creates, destroys it. **Yellow.** A failed save means 12d6 Lightning damage, and a successful one halves it. Taking at least 60 Force damage destroys this layer. **Green.** A failed save means 12d6 Poison damage, and a successful one halves it. A Passwall spell destroys this layer, as does any other spell of the same or higher level that can open a portal in a solid surface. **Blue.** A failed save means 12d6 Cold damage, and a successful one halves it. Taking at least 25 Fire damage destroys this layer. **Indigo.** A target that fails the save has the Restrained condition. When each of its turns ends, it makes a Constitution saving throw. Three successful saves end the condition. Three failed saves give it the Petrified condition until an effect such as the Greater Restoration spell frees it. The successes and failures don't need to come in a row. Keep count of both until the target reaches three of one kind. Spells can't be cast through this layer. Bright Light shed by the Daylight spell destroys it. **Violet.** On a failed save, the target has the Blinded condition. At the start of your next turn, it makes a Wisdom saving throw. A success ends the condition. A failure ends the condition too, but the creature is teleported to a different plane of existence chosen by the Dungeon Master. Dispel Magic destroys this layer.", "Shapechange": "**Material Component.** A jade circlet worth at least 1,500 GP. You take on the shape of another creature until the spell ends. While the spell lasts, you can take a Magic action to switch to a different eligible form. Any form you assume must be a kind of creature you have seen before, with a Challenge Rating that doesn't exceed your level or your own Challenge Rating. Constructs and Undead aren't allowed. Each shape-shift grants you Temporary Hit Points matching the form's Hit Points. If you run out of Temporary Hit Points, the spell ends early. The chosen form's stat block replaces your game statistics, but you keep your personality, alignment, and creature type, your Hit Points and Hit Point Dice, your Intelligence, Wisdom, and Charisma scores, your proficiencies, and your ability to communicate. You also keep the Spellcasting feature if you have it. When you shape-shift, you decide whether your equipment drops to the ground or reshapes and resizes to fit you for as long as you stay in that form.", "Storm of Vengeance": "For the duration, a churning storm cloud spreads out to a 300-foot radius around a point within range. When the cloud appears, each creature beneath it makes a Constitution saving throw. On a failure, the creature takes 2d6 Thunder damage and is Deafened (it has the Deafened condition) until the spell ends. At the start of each of your later turns, the storm creates a different effect, as described below. **Turn 2.** Acid rain pours down, dealing 4d6 Acid damage to every creature and object under the cloud. **Turn 3.** You draw six bolts of lightning from the cloud, each striking a different creature or object below it. Each of those targets must make a Dexterity saving throw. A target that fails takes 10d6 Lightning damage, and one that succeeds takes half as much. **Turn 4.** Hailstones fall, and each creature under the cloud takes 2d6 Bludgeoning damage. **Turns 5–10.** On each of these turns, freezing rain and gusts batter the ground below the cloud, dealing 1d6 Cold damage to every creature in that area. For the rest of the spell, the area counts as Heavily Obscured and as Difficult Terrain, a strong wind sweeps across it, and no one there can make a ranged attack with a weapon.", "Time Stop": "For a short while, time stops flowing for everyone but you. You take 1d4 + 1 turns in a row, and during them no time passes for any other creature. On those turns you can move and use actions normally. The spell ends early if something you do in this period, whether an action you use or an effect you create, affects any creature besides you or an object being worn or carried by anyone besides you. It also ends if you move to any spot farther than 1,000 feet from where you cast it.", "True Polymorph": "Pick one creature or nonmagical object within range that you can see. A creature can be reshaped into another creature or into a nonmagical object. An object can be reshaped into a creature, provided no one is wearing or carrying it. The transformation lasts for the duration, but it ends early if the target dies or is destroyed. Should you keep Concentration on the spell for its entire duration, the spell lasts until dispelled. If the creature is unwilling, it can make a Wisdom saving throw, and a success means the spell doesn't affect it. **Creature into Creature.** When a creature becomes a different kind of creature, you pick the new form, which can be any kind whose Challenge Rating doesn't exceed the target's own Challenge Rating or level. The new form's stat block replaces the target's game statistics, except that the target holds on to its alignment, personality, Hit Points, and Hit Point Dice. It also gains Temporary Hit Points equal to the new form's Hit Points. Once those Temporary Hit Points are gone, the spell ends early on the target. What the target can do is limited by its new body's anatomy, and it can't cast spells or speak. Its gear merges into the new form, and it can neither use that equipment nor gain any benefit from it. **Object into Creature.** An object can become any kind of creature whose Challenge Rating is 9 or lower and whose size doesn't exceed the object's size. That creature is Friendly to you and your allies. During combat, it takes each of its turns right after yours and follows your commands. If the spell lasts longer than an hour, you stop controlling the creature. Depending on how you have treated it, it might stay Friendly to you. **Creature into Object.** A creature can become an object no larger than the creature itself, and everything it wears and carries transforms with it. It takes on the object's statistics. When the spell ends and the creature returns to normal, it remembers nothing of its time in that form.", "True Resurrection": "**Material Component.** Diamonds worth at least 25,000 GP, which the spell consumes. Touch a creature that died no more than 200 years ago from any cause except old age. It comes back to life with all its Hit Points. Every wound closes, any poison is neutralized, all magical contagions are cured, and any curses that were on the creature when it died are lifted. The spell also replaces organs and limbs that are damaged or missing. A creature that was Undead returns in its non-Undead form. When the original body no longer exists, the spell can provide a new body, but you must say the creature's name aloud. The revived creature then appears in an unoccupied space of your choice no more than 10 feet away from you.", "Weird": "You attempt to plant illusory terrors in the minds of others. Choose any number of creatures within a Sphere of 30-foot radius centered on a point within range. Each chosen creature makes a Wisdom saving throw. A target that fails takes 10d10 Psychic damage and is Frightened until the spell ends, while one that succeeds takes only half as much damage. Whenever one of its turns ends, a Frightened target repeats the Wisdom saving throw. If it fails, it takes 5d10 Psychic damage, and if it succeeds, the spell ends for that target.", "Wish": "Wish is the most powerful spell a mortal can cast. Simply by speaking aloud, you can change reality itself. At its simplest, the spell copies any other spell of level 8 or lower. When you do so, you don't need to meet any of that spell's requirements, costly components included, and the spell just takes effect. Using Wish for any effect other than duplicating another spell puts you under a weakening stress. After you endure that stress, until you finish a Long Rest, every spell you cast deals 1d10 Necrotic damage to you per level of that spell, and nothing can reduce or prevent this damage. Also, your Strength score drops to 3 for 2d4 days. For each day of that period you spend resting, with nothing more strenuous than light activity, the remaining recovery time shrinks by 2 days. Finally, if you suffer this stress, there is a 33 percent chance that you can never cast Wish again. Instead of duplicating a spell, you can produce any one of the effects below. **Object Creation.** One object worth up to 25,000 GP, which isn't a magic item, comes into being. Its size can't exceed 300 feet in any dimension. It shows up on the ground in an unoccupied space you can see. **Instant Health.** You and up to twenty creatures you can see regain all Hit Points, and you end every effect listed in the Greater Restoration spell on each of you. **Resistance.** Up to ten creatures you can see gain permanent Resistance to one damage type you choose. **Spell Immunity.** Up to ten creatures you can see gain immunity to one spell or other magical effect for 8 hours. **Sudden Learning.** You swap one of your feats for another feat you qualify for, losing every benefit of the old feat and gaining the benefits of the new one. A feat that serves as a prerequisite for another of your feats or features can't be swapped out. **Roll Redo.** You undo one recent event: any die roll made during the last round (your last turn included) is rolled again, and reality reshapes itself to fit the new result. Wish could, for instance, reverse a foe's Critical Hit or an ally's failed saving throw. You can make the reroll happen with Advantage or Disadvantage, and you choose whether the reroll or the original roll counts. **Reshape Reality.** You can wish for something that none of the other effects covers. To do so, tell the Dungeon Master your wish as precisely as you can. The Dungeon Master has great latitude in ruling what happens in such a case, and the greater the wish, the more likely it is that something goes wrong. The spell might simply fail, the effect you want might be only partly achieved, or the way you phrased the wish might bring an unforeseen consequence. As an example, wishing a villain dead might send you ahead in time to an era after that villain's death, which effectively takes you out of the game. Likewise, a wish for an Artifact or a Legendary magic item might whisk you instantly to wherever the item's current owner is. If the wish comes true and its effects ripple across an entire community, region, or world, powerful foes are likely to take notice of you. A wish that would affect a god might lead that god's divine servants to intervene at once, either to prevent it or to push you to word the wish a certain way. A wish fails if it would unmake the multiverse itself, endanger the City of Sigil, or have any effect at all on the Lady of Pain. In that case, you briefly see her image in your mind as she shakes her head."};
